&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA["Leisure·Nature·Chill & Fun" Expo showcases diversity of local agriculture, fisheries and natural ecology (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900460.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900460.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:40:44 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) is holding the &quot;Leisure&middot;Nature&middot;Chill &amp; Fun&quot; Expo for two consecutive days starting from today (October 10) at East Point City in Tseung Kwan O. Through themed exhibitions, workshops, guided experiential tours, seminars, game booths and Hong Kong Harvest sale booths featuring agricultural and fisheries products, the Expo introduces the public to Hong Kong&#39;s unique agriculture and fisheries culture, natural ecological resources, as well as leisure fisheries and farming experiences.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Officiating at the opening ceremony, the Secretary for Environment and Ecology, Mr Tse Chin-wan, said that the &quot;Leisure&middot;Nature&middot;Chill &amp; Fun&quot; Expo comprehensively showcases the diversity of local leisure fisheries, leisure farming and eco-tourism. Not only does it enhance public awareness and interest in this area, but it also helps fishermen and farmers broaden their sources of income, thereby promoting the sustainable development of local agriculture and fisheries. The First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) proposes developing Hong Kong&#39;s agricultural and fisheries products into one of the demonstration brands within the Greater Bay Area; public support for local agriculture and fisheries is especially important in realising this vision.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Expo, for the first time, has brought together agriculture, fisheries and nature attractions from across all districts of Hong Kong that cover the elements of &quot;Eat, Play, Shop and Learn&quot;, introducing to the public, in one go, a number of less noticed yet highly worthwhile destinations for exploration, and encouraging the public to visit them more often to experience the joy of leisure fisheries and farming activities.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, the AFCD has simultaneously launched a new website, &quot;enJoy Leisure in Nature&quot; (<a href="https://enjoyleisure.afcd.gov.hk/en-US" target="_blank">enjoyleisure.afcd.gov.hk</a>),which provides a one-stop integration of information on leisure fish farms, agricultural farms and natural attractions across all districts of Hong Kong (including country parks, marine parks, geopark, the Hong Kong Wetland Park, and the Long Valley Nature Park). The website covers activity details, locations, transportation, opening hours and contact information, making it convenient for the public and visitors to plan in-depth local tours and experience the unique charm of Hong Kong&#39;s agriculture, fisheries and natural resources.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The &quot;Leisure&middot;Nature&middot;Chill &amp; Fun&quot; Expo is open from noon to 7pm today and tomorrow (October 10 and 11). Admission is free and the public are welcome to participate. Advance registration is not required for the seminars, while workshops and guided tours offer limited on-site registration on a first-come, first-served basis. For event details and the schedule, please visit the &quot;enJoy Leisure in Nature&quot; website.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Promoting the development of leisure farming and fisheries is one of the key directions of the Blueprint for the Sustainable Development of Agriculture and Fisheries, and is also a measure put forward in the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address. The AFCD will continue to enhance the promotion and publicity of local leisure farming and fisheries, and actively explore suitable development models for Hong Kong, with a view to assisting the industry in upgrading and transforming.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340858.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340858.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340859.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340859.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340860.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340860.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340861.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340861.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340862.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340862.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340863.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900460_photo_1340863.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Persons illegally bringing in undercooked game, meat, poultry and eggs will be prosecuted ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900724.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900724.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As the new Huanggang Port will commence operation on October 12, the Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) today (October 10) reminded members of the public again not to illegally bring regulated foods such as undercooked game, meat, poultry and eggs into Hong Kong, regardless of the boundary control point used upon return.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Imported Game, Meat, Poultry and Eggs Regulations (Cap. 132AK), all imported game, meat, poultry and eggs, whether for personal use or not, must be accompanied by a health certificate issued by the issuing entity of the place of origin or written permission from the FEHD. Any person bringing the aforesaid regulated foods into Hong Kong from the Mainland must provide a health certificate issued by Mainland Customs along with the goods. Any offender shall be liable on conviction to a maximum fine of $50,000 and imprisonment for six months. In 2025, the CFS initiated prosecutions in a total of 2 148 cases, including cases that resulted in custodial sentences.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the CFS said, &quot;Members of the public who illegally bring regulated foods into Hong Kong through any boundary control point will not only face prosecution, but also need to be aware that these foods may be improperly stored during travel, such as at an improper temperature or with unhygienic packaging, which can lead to the growth of pathogenic bacteria and increase food safety and personal health risks.&quot; To avoid criminal liability and health risks, the CFS urges members of the public not to illegally bring regulated foods into Hong Kong.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman stressed that any undercooked game, meat, poultry and eggs, regardless of their form of packaging (including vacuum packed or tinfoil), are all subject to legal regulation. Since blanched meat is not fully cooked, it is also prohibited from being brought into Hong Kong. The CFS will deploy quarantine detector dogs specially trained in detecting meat at various land boundary control points to assist law enforcement officers in carrying out their duties, thereby enhancing interception effectiveness.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CFS has been striving to safeguard food safety in Hong Kong, and will continue to combat illegal acts through interdepartmental operations at various boundary control points, as well as step up publicity and educational efforts.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[TD's Autonomous Vehicle Applications Promotion Working Group visits trial in Tung Chung, North Lantau (with photos/video)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900583.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100900583.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:17:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the Autonomous Vehicle Applications Promotion Working Group of the Transport Department (TD) today (October 10) visited the autonomous vehicle (AV) trial in Tung Chung, North Lantau. They took a ride in autonomous private cars, which ran steadily and smoothly throughout.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Members were first briefed by the pilot proprietor on the trial&#39;s progress. The North Lantau trial was approved in February 2026 to extend the Airport Island trial routes to Tung Chung and Sunny Bay, commencing a cross-district trial. The number of AVs operating simultaneously was also increased and their operations have been smooth. In end-July, the trial was approved to introduce remote backup operators at the remote control centre to monitor the AVs in tandem with onboard backup operators, with a view to collecting more trial data. The trial has proceeded largely smoothly so far. Members then took the AVs in batches to pass through the vicinity of Tung Chung Town Centre safely and returned in an orderly manner. They were pleased to note the stable, safe and smooth performance of the AVs throughout the journey.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A spokesman for the TD said, &quot;This is the Working Group&#39;s first on-site visit to an AV trial. It helps members, comprising autonomous driving researchers, information technology professionals, and other experts and stakeholders, engage in direct exchanges with the pilot proprietor on the latest technological developments and application experiences. The proprietor is encouraged to optimise technologies based on feedback from passengers in test rides, ensuring the secure, reliable and seamless implementation of autonomous driving. This will drive deeper integration of traffic and transport with artificial intelligence technologies, translating into a convenient travel experience genuinely felt by the public.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The TD and the pilot proprietor will brief the Traffic and Transport Committee under the Islands District Council at its meeting on October 13 on proposed public experience events.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340851.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340851.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340852.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340852.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340854.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340854.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340855.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340855.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340856.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340856.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340857.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100900583_photo_1340857.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CHP investigates confirmed Mpox case and continues to take multipronged approach to break transmission chains]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000238.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000238.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health (DH) announced today (October 10) that it is investigating a confirmed Mpox (also known as Monkeypox) case. Due to the recent significant increase in Mpox cases in Hong Kong, the CHP is implementing targeted prevention and control measures through a multi-pronged approach in order to break the transmission chains. Owing to these measures, the number of vaccines administered under the Mpox vaccination programme has risen significantly, with the number of doses administered earlier this month surging by more than 70 per cent compared to the same period last month. The CHP once again urges individuals who have engaged in high-risk sexual contact, particularly with strangers, to remain vigilant and take appropriate personal protective measures, including receiving the Mpox vaccination and avoid high-risk sexual contact, so as to lower the risk of infection. The CHP also noted that over half of the patients recorded in recent months used dating apps to find casual sexual partners. In some cases, patients were unable to contact their newly met sexual partners, which hinders effective contact tracing and the implementation of corresponding prevention and control measures. The CHP strongly urges the public to retain their sexual partners&#39; contact information to facilitate contact tracing when necessary.<br />
　<br />
Case details<br />
--------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The new case involves a 29-year-old man who developed a papule over his buttock on September 28, followed by rashes over his limbs and buttock, along with a fever and swollen lymph nodes. On October 8, while attending an outreach vaccination service organised by the CHP, in collaboration with the Hong Kong Christian Service, a non-governmental organization (NGO), CHP staff noticed his symptoms and arranged for him to receive isolation treatment at Princess Margaret Hospital. Upon laboratory testing by the CHP&#39;s Public Health Laboratory Services Branch (PHLSB), his clinical specimen tested positive for the Monkeypox virus. The patient is currently in stable condition.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to information provided by the patient, he had high-risk sexual exposure with a man in Hong Kong during the incubation period. The CHP is continuing its epidemiological investigation of the case and will report it to the World Health Organization.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Latest situation of Mpox cases<br />
------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since 2022, Hong Kong has recorded 114 Mpox cases (90 local cases and 24 imported cases). All patients are males. As of today, 30 cases have been recorded this year, including 23 local cases and seven imported cases. Since August, there have been 21 new cases, accounting for 70 per cent of the total number of cases this year.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Epidemiological investigations showed that all 21 cases identified since August reported engaging in high-risk sexual behaviours during the incubation period, including having sex with male strangers or multiple partners, and having sex without a condom. Most of the patients had not received the Mpox vaccination.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s PHLSB conducted genetic analysis of the virus and found that some cases had identical genetic sequences. This suggests that the cases may have shared a common source of infection, with multiple transmission chains existing among men who have sex with men population.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Newly Mpox vaccination venues<br />
-----------------------------------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mpox vaccine can prevent infection and severe disease. Since late September, in addition to regular vaccination venues, the CHP has set up an additional Mpox vaccination centre at the CHP Building, and has collaborated with three NGOs (namely Community Health Organisation for Intervention, Care and Empowerment; Hong Kong AIDS Foundation; and Hong Kong Christian Service) to provide outreach vaccination services. Together with the various existing vaccination venues, the CHP has successfully administered the Mpox vaccine to approximately 360 high-risk individuals over the past two weeks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To further reduce the risk of community infection and transmission, the CHP will continue to actively increase the locations and time slots for Mpox vaccination. Following the completion of three outreach vaccination sessions recently, the CHP has arranged the following services:<br />
<br />
(i) Hong Kong AIDS Foundation - Mong Kok Service Centre<br />
Date and time: October 14 (Wednesday), 5pm to 8pm<br />
Location: Bright Way Tower, 33 Mong Kok Road, Mong Kok, Kowloon<br />
(Online appointments: <a href="https://forms.gle/ytDgGsn9V2R8wL4VA" target="_blank">https://forms.gle/ytDgGsn9V2R8wL4VA</a>)<br />
<br />
(ii) Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre (additional session)<br />
Date and time: October 24 (Saturday), 10am to 1pm<br />
Location: 7/F, Yau Ma Tei Jockey Club Polyclinic, 145 Battery Street, Yau Ma Tei<br />
(Phone reservations: Please call Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre at 2195 6588 from 8.30am to 5pm, Monday to Friday, to make an appointment. Places are limited.)<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Strengthening public education and awareness<br />
------------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s Mpox hotline (2125 2373) provides health assessments advice and vaccination arrangements for affected individuals. The hotline operates daily from 9am to 6pm.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP has consistently conducted comprehensive and in-depth epidemiological investigations into every Mpox case, striving to contact individuals who have had high-risk contact with the patients. The CHP&#39;s epidemiological investigations showed that patients more frequently used specific dating apps to find casual sexual partners. After testing positive, some patients were unable to contact their newly met sexual partners, which hindered the CHP&#39;s ability to conduct effective contact tracing and implement corresponding prevention and control measures. In view of this, the CHP has increased its outreach on social media platforms to promote Mpox prevention and vaccination, and has successfully collaborated with the operators of relevant dating apps to regularly push information about monkeypox prevention and vaccination to all users in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP staff visited various entertainment venues, including saunas, to promote health and remind individuals at high risk of exposure that vaccination protects against infection and reduces the likelihood of transmitting it to others. Due to the current surge in Mpox cases, individuals should limit the number of sexual partners and use condoms in addition to getting vaccinated to further reduce the risk of infection.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
More information on Mpox<br />
----------------------------------<br />
<br />
　　Mpox is not transmitted through respiratory droplets or aerosols in general, and transmission would not occur through social contact. The CHP reminds the public, especially those at higher risk of exposure, to take precautions and avoid close physical contact with persons or animals suspected of being infected. They should seek medical attention as soon as possible if they experience relevant symptoms, including rash, fever, chills, swollen lymph nodes, exhaustion, muscle pain, and severe headaches. They should not engage in activities with others that may involve contact with skin rash or body fluids.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The DH has been providing Mpox vaccination services for high-risk groups, including individuals with high-risk sexual practices (such as having multiple sexual partners), sex workers, or having a history of sexually transmitted infection within the past 12 months. High-risk target groups can receive Mpox walk-in vaccinations at any of the DH&#39;s SocHS (namely Chai Wan SocHS, Wan Chai Male SocHS, Wan Chai Female SocHS, Yau Ma Tei Male SocHS, Yau Ma Tei Female SocHS, Yung Fung Shee SocHS, Fanling SocHS and Tuen Mun SocHS) and the DH&#39;s Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre, without prior appointments. Meanwhile, the DH&#39;s Kowloon Bay Integrated Treatment Centre and the HA&#39;s Special Medical Clinics at Queen Elizabeth Hospital and Princess Margaret Hospital also provide Mpox vaccination services for their clients.<br />
<br />
　　For more details, please visit the CHP&#39;s webpages on&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/features/105683.html" target="_blank">Mpox</a>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/features/106090.html" target="_blank">Mpox Vaccination Programme</a>.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at some beaches]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000221.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000221.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:26:22 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 10) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Stanley Main Beach, Shek O Beach and Big Wave Bay Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island; Silverstrand Beach and Clear Water Bay Second Beach in Sai Kung District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Toronto ETO supports Hong Kong Gaudeamus Dunhuang Ensemble's Canadian debut (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100700623.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100700623.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 08:42:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office (Toronto) (Toronto ETO) supported the Hong Kong Gaudeamus Dunhuang Ensemble in making its inaugural Canadian debut in October. The Ensemble held concerts entitled &quot;Resonance of Dunhuang&quot; alongside academic lectures in Toronto and Ottawa on October 9 and 10 (Toronto time) respectively, fostering cultural exchanges between Hong Kong and Canada. The events attracted over 1 000 guests.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The &quot;Resonance of Dunhuang&quot; concerts featured eight outstanding young Hong Kong musicians playing original compositions on replicas of ancient instruments. Coupled with dynamic visual projections inspired by the Dunhuang murals, the performances transformed ancient cultural treasures into an immersive audiovisual feast.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To enrich the cultural elements of the concert experience, the Ensemble also presented several academic lectures delivered by renowned Dunhuang art scholar and former Director of the Dunhuang Academy, Dr Zhao Shengliang, who provided comprehensive analyses on the artistic essence of the Mogao Caves murals.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Toronto ETO hosted a reception on October 9 (Toronto time) at the first stop of the tour in Toronto. Speaking at the event, the Director of the Toronto ETO, Mr Bill Wong, said that the Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) had just announced the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the HKSAR (2026-2030), which reaffirms Hong Kong&rsquo;s important role as an East-meets-West centre for international cultural exchanges, as well as its mission to promote the profound traditional Chinese culture and develop cultural contents with Hong Kong characteristics.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Wong said, &quot;The Toronto ETO is honored to support this tour. Through the power of music that transcends languages and cultures, we are delighted to showcase the rich facets of profound Chinese culture to Canadian audiences.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Funded by the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau of the HKSAR Government, the tour received support from the Embassy of the People&#39;s Republic of China in Canada, Hong Kong Tourism Board, and the Canada-China Cultural Development Association, among other organisations.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340850.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340850.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340849.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340849.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340848.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340848.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340847.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340847.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340846.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026100700623_photo_1340846.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Yellow fire danger warning]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000152.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000152.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 06:04:04 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention duty announcers, radio and TV stations:<br />
<br />
Yellow fire danger warning<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The fire danger situation today (October 10) is yellow and the risk of fire is high. People are urged to prevent fires breaking out and hikers should also be vigilant.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Regional flag day today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100800418.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026100800418.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 06:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Three charities have been issued Public Subscription Permits to hold flag sales from 7am to 12.30pm today (October 10). They are, on Hong Kong Island, Taishan Charitable Association Limited; in Kowloon, The Hong Kong Christian Kun Sun Association Limited; and in the New Territories, Shatin Women&#39;s Association Limited, a spokesman for the Social Welfare Department (SWD) said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Arrangements have been made with the charities to help people to distinguish among the three flag-selling activities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Information on the three flag-selling organisations on October 10 is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:552px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:127px">Region</td>
			<td style="width:220px">Name of organisation</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Colour of collection bag</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Colour of flag</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:127px">Hong Kong Island</td>
			<td style="width:220px">Taishan Charitable Association Limited</td>
			<td style="width:102px">White</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Magenta</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:127px">Kowloon</td>
			<td style="width:220px">The Hong Kong Christian Kun Sun Association Limited</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Blue</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Beige</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:127px">New Territories</td>
			<td style="width:220px">Shatin Women&#39;s Association Limited</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Green</td>
			<td style="width:102px">Green</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Details of the charitable fund-raising activities, including any updated information, covered by the issued Public Subscription Permits have been published on the GovHK website (<a href="https://www.gov.hk/en/theme/fundraising/search" target="_blank">www.gov.hk/en/theme/fundraising/search</a>). Permits for flag days containing information on contact methods of the flag-selling organisations and the approved flag-selling activities have also been uploaded to the SWD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.swd.gov.hk/en/ngo/controlofc/flagdays" target="_blank">www.swd.gov.hk/en/ngo/controlofc/flagdays</a>) for reference. For enquiries, please call the SWD&#39;s hotline at 2343 2255, or the designated hotline of the 1823 Call Centre at 3142 2678.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In the case of suspected fraudulent flag day activities, people should not make any donation and should immediately report the matter to the Police, the spokesman added.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inter-departmental collaboration to ensure seamless transition between original and new Huanggang Ports (with photo/video)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000007.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000007.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 01:53:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The redeveloped Huanggang Port (new Huanggang Port) will open at 6.30am on October 12 (Monday). The Immigration Department (ImmD), the Hong Kong Customs and Excise Department (C&amp;ED), the Transport Department (TD) and the Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) yesterday (October 9) released the following latest information regarding the arrangements for the opening of the new Huanggang Port:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Immigration clearance arrangements<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The ImmD will commence immigration clearance services at the new Huanggang Port starting from 6.30am on October 12. Before 6.30am on the same day, all passengers are still required to use the Lok Ma Chau Control Point to travel between Hong Kong and Shenzhen. The ImmD will ensure that all passengers arriving at the Lok Ma Chau Control Point or the temporary port building of the Huanggang Port in Shenzhen before 6.30am can complete their immigration clearance procedures.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; During the initial opening of the new Huanggang Port, it is expected that traffic in the vicinity may be relatively busy. The ImmD reminds passengers planning to use the new Huanggang Port to plan their journeys ahead and allow sufficient time to adapt to the new arrangements. The ImmD will set up a joint command centre with relevant departments, including the HKPF, the C&amp;ED and the TD, and maintain close liaison with Mainland authorities such as the Shenzhen General Station of Exit and Entry Frontier Inspection, to closely monitor the operation of the new port and take timely response measures. The ImmD will also flexibly deploy manpower to cope with the possible surge in passenger and vehicular traffic during the initial stage, ensuring smooth and orderly clearance for both passengers and vehicles.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Furthermore, the ImmD appeals to eligible Hong Kong residents to enrol for the new &quot;collaborative inspection&quot; automated channels in advance through the &quot;HK e-Channel&quot; mobile application. The ImmD also reminds passengers that, when using the &quot;collaborative inspection&quot; automated channels to travel between Hong Kong and the Mainland, they are still required to carry valid travel documents that meet the immigration requirements of both sides, and to check the validity of their documents carefully before departure.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Customs clearance arrangements<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; To ensure a smooth transition of passenger and vehicular clearance services between the original and new ports, the C&amp;ED has planned ahead and deployed sufficient manpower at both the new Huanggang Port and the Lok Ma Chau Control Point. The C&amp;ED will maintain close liaison with relevant government departments and Shenzhen counterparts, and closely monitor the arrival time of the last inbound and outbound cross-boundary buses and private cars at the Lok Ma Chau Control Point to ensure that all passengers and cross-boundary vehicles of the final departures have completed clearance procedures and departed the Lok Ma Chau Control Point.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The new Huanggang Port will implement new clearance arrangements for cross-boundary vehicles featuring &quot;joint one-stop&quot; vehicle lanes, where drivers can complete clearance procedures of both places in one go simply by completing the collection of body temperature, travel document, facial features and fingerprints through the &quot;integrated information collection device&quot; in the lane. Drivers using the &quot;joint one-stop&quot; vehicle lanes for the first time are required to complete the relevant authorisation at the &quot;integrated information collection device&quot;. Outbound vehicles will first pass through the &quot;customs front kiosk&quot; for customs risk assessment, and vehicles requiring customs inspection must turn right as directed to proceed to the inspection area for examination. In addition, the new Huanggang Port will adopt a &quot;passenger-vehicle separation&quot; arrangement, under which general passengers of cross-boundary private cars are required to disembark at the drop-off area and carry their personal luggage and belongings to proceed to the immigration clearance hall for on-board passengers to undergo passenger clearance procedures.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Traffic and transport arrangements for switchover between original and new Huanggang Ports<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; To tie in with the suspension of passenger clearance services between the Lok Ma Chau Control Point and the original Huanggang Port, as well as the opening of the new Huanggang Port at 6.30am on October 12, the TD has steered local and cross-boundary public transport operators (PTOs) in formulating vehicle deployments, service schedules, and passenger notices for the routes concerned.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A Government spokesman said, &quot;Before and after the switchover between the original and new Huanggang Ports, traffic in the vicinity may be relatively busy. Members of the public are urged to follow the instructions of the Police and PTO staff, and refrain from lingering at the original and new ports or the San Tin Public Transport Interchange (PTI), heed service schedules, and maintain order while queuing. The public should plan their journeys in advance, choose suitable boundary control points according to their needs, and do not need to rush to use the new Huanggang Port.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
1) Public transport arrangements for Lok Ma Chau Control Point/original Huanggang Port<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The last northbound Lok Ma Chau-Huanggang cross-boundary shuttle bus (Yellow Bus) to the original Huanggang Port will depart from San Tin PTI between 6.15am and 6.20am on October 12, and the operator will stop latecomers from joining the queue as appropriate. Passengers who miss the last Yellow Bus may take three franchised bus daytime routes (KMB B11, NLB B12 and Citybus B13) and three green minibus (GMB) daytime routes (44B, 44B1 and 78) serving San Tin PTI to travel to the new Huanggang Port for boundary crossing. The last southbound Yellow Bus from the original Huanggang Port to San Tin PTI will depart only after all cross-boundary travellers entering the original Huanggang Port before 6.30am have completed clearance procedures and boarded the bus.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In addition, five 24-hour short-haul cross-boundary coach (CBC) routes will tie in with the switchover at 6.30am on October 12. The last northbound departures from urban areas to the original Huanggang Port, and the last southbound departures from the original Huanggang Port to urban areas on that morning are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:538px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Route</td>
			<td style="width:233px">Last departure to original Huanggang Port (northbound)</td>
			<td style="width:208px">Last departure to urban areas (southbound)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Mong Kok</td>
			<td style="width:233px">5.30am</td>
			<td style="width:208px">6.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Yau Tsim</td>
			<td style="width:233px">5.45am</td>
			<td style="width:208px">6.15am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Kwun Tong</td>
			<td style="width:233px">5.30am</td>
			<td style="width:208px">6.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Wan Chai</td>
			<td style="width:233px">5.15am</td>
			<td style="width:208px">6.15am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Tsuen Wan</td>
			<td style="width:233px">5.30am</td>
			<td style="width:208px">6.30am</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Regarding GMBs and taxis, the TD has alerted relevant operators and trades to render full facilitation to the switchover arrangements. If any northbound vehicles heading to the original Huanggang Port fail to arrive before the Lok Ma Chau Control Point suspends service, they will be arranged to wait at the public transport buffer area near the new Huanggang Port and be redirected to the new port when it opens.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
2) Public transport arrangements for new Huanggang Port<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On that day, the first departure times of the five franchised bus and three GMB daytime routes to the new Huanggang Port are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:586px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">Route</td>
			<td style="width:170px">First departure time to new Huanggang Port*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">KMB B10 (Kwai Tsui Estate PTI &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">KMB B11 (Tin Fu Bus Terminus &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">NLB B12 (Wu Kai Sha Station PTI &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">Citybus B13 (Siu Sai Wan (Island Resort) &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">4.45am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">Citybus B14 (Kai Tak (Olympic Avenue) &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.40am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">GMB 44B (Tuen Mun Station &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.45am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">GMB 44B1 (Tuen Mun Ferry Pier &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">5.45am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:415px">GMB 78 (Pat Heung Road (near Tai Lam Tunnel Bus-Bus Interchange) &ndash; New Huanggang Port)</td>
			<td style="width:170px">6am</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
*The first departures are advanced to tie in with the opening time of new Huanggang Port, and services will resume according to the Schedule of Service starting from the following day.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On that morning, the southbound first departures of the above franchised bus and GMB routes departing from the new Huanggang Port will also tie in with the commencement of clearance services at 6.30am, taking into account the estimated time required for passengers to complete clearance procedures and arrive at the PTI.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As for the short-haul CBC routes, they will be redirected to serve the new Huanggang Port after the last departures to and from the original Huanggang Port, operating according to their original service schedules:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:548px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Route</td>
			<td style="width:233px">First departure to new Huanggang Port (northbound)</td>
			<td style="width:217px">First departure to urban areas (southbound)*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Mong Kok</td>
			<td style="width:233px">6am</td>
			<td style="width:217px">6.40am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Yau Tsim</td>
			<td style="width:233px">6.45am</td>
			<td style="width:217px">6.45am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Kwun Tong</td>
			<td style="width:233px">6am</td>
			<td style="width:217px">6.45am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Wan Chai</td>
			<td style="width:233px">6.15am</td>
			<td style="width:217px">6.40am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:98px">Tsuen Wan</td>
			<td style="width:233px">6am</td>
			<td style="width:217px">6.45am</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
*Following the opening time of new Huanggang Port, subject to the time required for passengers to complete clearance procedures, the first departures may be slightly delayed.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Various PTOs will arrange sufficient vacant vehicles on standby at the new Huanggang Port PTI and the nearby public transport buffer area to pick up passengers arriving in Hong Kong via the new Huanggang Port.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
3) Traffic arrangements near new Huanggang Port<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Appropriate traffic signs and road markings have been installed on major roads connecting to the new Huanggang Port, such as San Sham Road and the Lok Ma Chau Bridge, to guide motorists to the new port. The Police will also be on-site to direct vehicles to the new Huanggang Port. Motorists should pay attention to on-site traffic signs and road markings, and follow the instructions of Police officers.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The spokesman said, &quot;We will closely monitor passenger demand in collaboration with PTOs and strengthen or adjust services when necessary. Passengers should refer to operator notices, websites, mobile apps, or pay attention to onboard broadcasts to understand route details, stop locations and service hours. Cross-boundary travellers who need to use the new Huanggang Port should plan their journeys early and reserve time to adapt to the new arrangements. The TD&#39;s Emergency Transport Co-ordination Centre will closely monitor the traffic and public transport conditions at the new port.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Members of the public and visitors may visit the TD&#39;s designated webpage <a href="https://www.td.gov.hk/en/transport_in_hong_kong/land_based_cross_boundary_transport/athp/index.html" target="_blank">&quot;Access to Huanggang Port&quot;</a> for details, or heed the latest traffic news via the TD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.td.gov.hk/" target="_blank">www.td.gov.hk</a>), the HKeMobility mobile app as well as radio and television broadcasts.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Crowd management and traffic control measures<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The HKPF has formulated detailed operational plans for the opening of the new Huanggang Port and its switchover period, and will implement crowd management measures, regulate traffic flow, and facilitate media activities on-site to ensure the smooth operation of the port and public safety.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The switchover plan will be executed in the small hours of October 12. The operational plans are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
1) Activation of Joint Operation Centre and implementation of crowd management plans<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On the night of the switchover, the HKPF will set up a Joint Operation Centre at the new Huanggang Port to monitor the crowd and traffic flow in real time. Noting that members of the public and bus enthusiasts may visit the Lok Ma Chau Control Point or the San Tin PTI to catch the first and last departures of buses, or to take photos of facilities prior to their cessation of operations, the Police will deploy officers to the above locations. Designated public activity areas, media areas, and waiting zones will be demarcated on ground. Crowd management measures will be implemented to ensure that these activities will not obstruct the normal operation of the port and the switchover arrangements.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Meanwhile, Police officers will also be stationed at the PTI on the second floor, as well as the passenger clearance halls on the fourth and fifth floors of the Huanggang Port. They will conduct joint crowd control operations alongside members of the Civil Aid Service and staff from the Government Property Agency to ensure good order upon the opening of the port.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Security Bureau will activate the Emergency Monitoring and Support Centre, and the New Huanggang Port Joint Command Post to closely monitor and co-ordinate the opening of the port, oversee and co-ordinate inter-departmental operations, and ensure a seamless switchover from the Lok Ma Chau Control Point to the new Huanggang Port.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
2) Traffic contingency plans and vehicle diversion measures<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; To ensure smooth traffic ahead of the switchover, Police officers have inspected surrounding roads prior to the implementation of the switchover plan with relevant departments to ensure road safety as well as clear and precise signage. During the switchover period, Police officers will step up patrols in the vicinity of San Tin Interchange, San Sham Road, and San Tin Highway to maintain smooth traffic in the surrounding areas.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; When the switchover plan is underway, the HKPF will place water-filled barriers at the entrance to the Lok Ma Chau Control Point and divert all Shenzhen-bound vehicles to the new Huanggang Port. Once it is confirmed that all northbound and southbound passengers and vehicles have departed the Lok Ma Chau Control Point, passenger clearance services at the Control Point will suspend operations.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In view of the potential traffic volume during the switchover period, the HKPF will, depending on the actual traffic conditions on-site, activate the Lok Ma Chau Control Point Northbound Vehicle Holding Area to prevent traffic queues from tail backing onto San Sham Road and San Tin Highway, which could otherwise cause widespread congestion. Meanwhile, police officers will also stand by at various locations around the port. In the event of traffic accidents or road blockages, they will immediately intervene to ensure the smooth overall operation of the new port.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026101000007_photo_1340845.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/10/P2026101000007_photo_1340845.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKSAR Government strongly disapproves of and rejects European Parliament's Recommendation on EU-China political relations]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000016.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/10/P2026101000016.htm</link><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 00:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) yesterday (October 9) expressed firm opposition to and strong dissatisfaction with, and strongly condemned the so-called Recommendation on EU-China political relations by the European Parliament, which made biased and untrue slanders in the section concerning Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the HKSAR Government stressed, &quot;The HKSAR is an inalienable part of the People&#39;s Republic of China. It is a local administrative region that enjoys a high degree of autonomy under &#39;one country, two systems&#39; and comes directly under the Central People&#39;s Government. The HKSAR Government will resolutely, fully and faithfully implement the Hong Kong National Security Law (HKNSL), the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance (SNSO) and other relevant laws so as to effectively prevent, suppress and impose punishment for acts and activities endangering national security, safeguard the lawful rights and interests enjoyed by Hong Kong people and other persons in the HKSAR in accordance with the law, and provide a solid foundation for developments in Hong Kong&#39;s economy, social life and other aspects. Its overarching goal is to ensure the steadfast and successful implementation of &#39;one country, two systems&#39;.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;The implementation of the HKNSL in the past six years or so has enabled the livelihood and economic activities of the Hong Kong community at large to swiftly resume to normal and the business environment to be restored and improved continuously. Besides maintaining its position as the world&#39;s freest economy, Hong Kong has risen to top spot in cross boundary wealth management worldwide and is ranked second in world competitiveness. The city is ranked third among international financial centres and comes first in the world in terms of funds raised through initial public offerings in 2025. Hong Kong remains high in the overall ranking and continues to be ahead of the western countries which often unreasonably criticise the rule of law and human rights situation of Hong Kong in the 2025 World Justice Project Rule of Law Index. These achievements are built upon the freedom and stability brought to Hong Kong society by the HKNSL, the SNSO, other relevant laws of the HKSAR, and the work of safeguarding national security.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;It is each and every sovereign state&#39;s inherent right, under international law and international relations based on the Charter of the United Nations, to enact laws safeguarding national security, and is also an international practice. The HKSAR Government strongly urges the European Parliament to immediately stop its slanders and smears against Hong Kong, as well as cease issuing in any form so-called recommendation that distort facts and spread alarmist narratives, and interfering in Hong Kong affairs, which are purely China&#39;s internal affairs. The HKSAR Government will, as always, safeguard independent judicial power and fully supports the Judiciary in exercising its judicial power independently, and at the same time steadfastly safeguard national sovereignty, security and development interests, and defend the dignity of the rule of law.&quot;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong publishing and printing industries showcased at Frankfurt Book Fair (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900647.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900647.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:47:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office, Berlin (HKETO Berlin) supported the participation of the Hong Kong Printers Association and Hong Kong Publishing Federation at the Frankfurt Book Fair from October 7 to 11 (Frankfurt time).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Pavilion, sponsored by the Cultural and Creative Industries Development Agency (CCIDA) under the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau, showcased outstanding Hong Kong publications and printed materials to an international audience. It also provided a platform for Hong Kong publishers and printers to promote their capabilities and explore business opportunities in international markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Frankfurt Book Fair is the largest international publishing and content trade fair in the world. Once a year, it brings together publishers, authors, and industry experts to develop new business opportunities, as well as exhibit the creativity and achievements of different countries and regions.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Head of Public Relations of HKETO Berlin, Ms Stephanie Pall, attended the opening ceremony of the Hong Kong Pavilion at the Frankfurt Book Fair on October 7. She thanked CCIDA for supporting the participation of the Hong Kong Printers Association and Hong Kong Publishing Federation at the Book Fair to showcase the top quality of Hong Kong&#39;s publishing and printing industries.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;Hong Kong continues to build on its reputation as a dynamic creative hub in Asia. Powered by an open flow of ideas, strong market agility, and a global perspective, local publishing and printing professionals offer compelling original content and world-class production capabilities for international partners,&quot; Ms Pall said.<br />
<br />
About HKETO Berlin<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HKETO Berlin is the official representative of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government in commercial relations and other economic and trade matters in Germany as well as Austria, Czechia, Hungary, Poland, the Slovak Republic, Slovenia and Switzerland.&nbsp;<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900647_photo_1340838.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900647_photo_1340838.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CHP investigates cluster of Carbapenemase-producing Enterobacteriaceae carrier cases]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900755.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900755.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:23:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;​The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health is today (October 9) investigating a cluster of Carbapenemase-producing Enterobacteriaceae (CPE) carrier cases involving three residents of a residential care home for the elderly (RCHE).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The CHP recently received a notification from the Hospital Authority (HA) that two residents living on the same floor of an RCHE in North Point had been admitted to a public hospital for treatment due to their underlying illnesses. Their admission screening samples tested positive for CPE upon laboratory testing and they were suspected to have been infected in the RCHE. The CHP immediately conducted epidemiological investigations, contact tracing as well as screenings, and subsequently identified one more infection case in the same RCHE. All three patients (two males and one female, aged between 74 and 94) are carriers. None of them exhibited symptoms associated with CPE infection. They are currently in stable condition.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The CHP&#39;s further investigation revealed that the source of infection for the aforementioned three carrier cases may have been two other previously identified CPE carrier cases on the same floor of the same RCHE. After inspecting the RCHE, CHP staff discovered that the RCHE had not implemented relevant infection prevention and control measures. The bacteria were highly likely to have been transmitted within the RCHE via staff, or contaminated environment and objects. The CHP reminded the staff of the RCHE to adhere to the guidelines and take necessary measures, including enhancing hand hygiene among staff and residents, and thoroughly cleaning and disinfecting the environment and objects. The CHP also recommended that the RCHE cohort the carriers in a single designated area.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The CHP will continue to investigate the cluster of cases and conduct screening at the RCHE concerned to determine whether there are any other CPE-carriers. The CHP will also continue to put the RCHE under medical surveillance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Enterobacteriaceae (such as Escherichia coli and&nbsp;Klebsiella) are common pathogens that can cause infections in various parts of the body, including urinary tract infections, intra-abdominal infections or blood infection. CPE are enterobacteriaceae that produce carbapenemase, an enzyme that can deactivate carbapenems and other beta-lactam antibiotics such as penicillins. These bacteria are commonly resistant to multiple antibiotics, limiting therapeutic options, and may render severe clinical infections difficult to treat. The range of diseases associated with CPE varies from asymptomatic carriage to potentially life-threatening or fatal infections. The level of risk depends on which part of the body is affected by the infection and the patient&#39;s general health.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The proper use of antibiotics and maintaining good personal and environmental hygiene, especially hand hygiene, are important for preventing the emergence and cross-transmission of multi-drug resistant organisms (MDROs), such as CPE. In addition, susceptible individuals such as the elderly, infants, young children, people with weakened immunity and pregnant women can reduce their risk of contracting MDROs by avoiding raw or undercooked foods.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Police College renews collaboration with three post-secondary education institutions to co-organise "DAE - Police Cadet Training" Programme (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900773.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900773.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:10:37 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Police College today (October 9) signed a second-phase Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) with the Caritas Institute of Community Education (CICE), the Hong Kong College of Technology (HKCT) and the Hong Kong Institute of Technology (HKIT) to jointly organise the full-time &quot;Diploma of Applied Education (DAE) - Police Cadet Training&quot; Programme for the three academic years from 2027/28 to 2029/2030, with a view to providing practical knowledge and preparatory training for persons interested in joining the Force. Students who successfully complete the programme and pass the Police Constable selection process may commence foundation training at the Police College upon graduation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Witnessed by the Assistant Commissioner of Police (Personnel), Dr Law Yuet-wing; the Director of the Hong Kong Police College, Ms Cheng Lai-ki, and the Secretary-General and Acting Head of the Diploma of Applied Education of the Federation for Self-financing Tertiary Education, Dr Ma Chee-keung, the Senior Superintendent of Police, School of Foundation Training, Ms Tsang Chiu-tong, signed the second-phase MOU with the Principal of the CICE, Dr Wong Man-wai, the Vice President of the HKCT, Ms Li Wai-chi, and the President of the HKIT, Dr Shi Mei-chun.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The &quot;Diploma of Applied Education (DAE) - Police Cadet Training&quot; Programme lasts one year with a total of 600 contact hours. The three education institutions will be responsible for 420 contact hours, consisting of 300 contact hours of core courses (Chinese Language, English Language and Mathematics) and two 60-hour complementary courses. The Hong Kong Police College will be responsible for the 180-hour &quot;Police Cadet Training&quot; elective cluster, which comprises three courses, namely &quot;Police Studies&quot;, &quot;Police Recruit Preparatory Training&quot; and &quot;Physical and Mindset Development&quot;.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Students who successfully complete the DAE programme would obtain qualification deemed comparable to the attainment of Level 2 standard in five subjects including Chinese and English Languages in the Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education Examination, which meets the academic entry requirements for Police Constable. The Hong Kong Police Force will conduct the Police Constable selection process during the programme to facilitate admission of selected candidates, who may commence Recruit Police Constable foundation training upon graduation.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Applicants of the programme must be i) Secondary 6 school leavers or ii) aged 21 or above, who are interested in joining the Force. To join the Hong Kong Police Force, one must also be a permanent resident of Hong Kong. The first round of application will open in December 2026. To apply, please make online registration at the programme website (<a href="https://www.dae.edu.hk/daepublic/dae_enrollmentguide.aspx" target="_blank">www.dae.edu.hk/daepublic/dae_enrollmentguide.aspx</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340841.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340841.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340842.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340842.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340843.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340843.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340844.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900773_photo_1340844.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CFS urges public not to consume two kinds of imported prepackaged salad and pesto products suspected to be contaminated with Salmonella]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900721.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900721.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:05:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department today (October 9) urged the public not to consume a kind of imported prepackaged salad and a kind of imported prepackaged pesto, as the products might have been contaminated with Salmonella, a pathogen. The trade should stop using or selling the affected batches of the products immediately if they possess them.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Product details are as follows:<br />
<br />
Product name: Nutrient Dense Nutty Super Wholefood Salad<br />
Brand: Marks &amp; Spencer<br />
Pack size: 285 grams<br />
Place of origin: United Kingdom<br />
Use-by date: October 8, 2026; October 9, 2026; October 10, 2026; October 11, 2026<br />
Importer: ALF Retail Hong Kong Limited<br />
<br />
Product name: Collections Pistachio Pesto<br />
Brand: Marks &amp; Spencer<br />
Pack size: 115 grams<br />
Place of origin: United Kingdom<br />
Use-by date: October 8, 2026; October 9, 2026; October 10, 2026; October 11, 2026; October 12, 2026; October 13, 2026<br />
Importer: ALF Retail Hong Kong Limited<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the CFS said, &quot;The CFS, through its Food Incident Surveillance System, noted notifications from the Food Standards Agency of the United Kingdom and the Food Safety Authority of Ireland that the above-mentioned products might have been contaminated by Salmonella and are being recalled. Upon learning of the incident, the CFS immediately followed-up the cases. A preliminary investigation found that the above-mentioned importer had imported and sold the affected products.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The importer concerned has stopped sales, removed from shelves the affected products and has initiated a recall according to the CFS&#39;s instructions. Members of the public may call its hotline at 8 0093 1457 during office hours for enquiries about the recall.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;Salmonella infection may cause fever and gastrointestinal upset such as vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhoea. The effects on infants, young children, the elderly and persons with a weak immune system could be more severe and may even lead to death,&quot; the spokesman said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CFS will alert the trade to the incident, and will continue to follow up and take appropriate action. The investigation is ongoing.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[225 more nomination forms for Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900744.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900744.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:46:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Returning Officers for the various subsectors of the 2026 Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received a total of 220 nomination forms for candidates and five nomination forms from designated bodies today (October 9). This brings to 685 the total number of nomination forms for candidates and 11 the total number of nomination forms from designated bodies received since nominations opened on October 7. The nomination period will end on October 20.<br />
