&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKSAR LegCo resolutely opposes and strongly condemns European Parliament's Recommendation on EU-China political relations]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900719.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900719.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:43:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Legislative Council Secretariat:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A spokesperson for the Legislative Council (LegCo) of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) today (October 9) expressed resolute opposition and severe condemnation to the Recommendation on EU-China political relations (the Recommendation) by the European Parliament, which made untrue and biased allegations in the section concerning Hong Kong. The spokesperson also urges the European Parliament to immediately stop slandering Hong Kong&#39;s development, smearing its rule of law, human rights and other conditions, as well as cease interfering in any form in Hong Kong affairs, which are purely China&rsquo;s internal affairs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The LegCo spokesperson pointed out that the HKSAR is an inalienable part of the People&#39;s Republic of China. Enacting laws to safeguard national security and continuously refining relevant enforcement mechanisms is a universally recognized international practice and an inherent duty. Facts have shown that the enactment and implementation of the Hong Kong National Security Law (HKNSL), the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance and other relevant laws have effectively safeguarded national security and restored stability to Hong Kong society. It has laid a solid foundation for Hong Kong&#39;s economic, livelihood and overall development. The rule of law and judicial independence of Hong Kong and the basic freedoms enjoyed by its residents have received more comprehensive protection. Furthermore, Hong Kong&#39;s business environment has continually achieved commendable results. Notably, Hong Kong was once again ranked as the world&#39;s freest economy recently, ranked second globally in world competitiveness, third worldwide as an international financial centre. Hong Kong&#39;s rule of law ranking remains high. These objective facts serve as the most powerful rebuttal to the European Parliament&#39;s so-called recommendation.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In fact, the European Union (EU) and its member states have also enacted or amended laws and regulations that restrict citizens&rsquo; rights and freedoms in the name of national security or anti-terrorism. They have likewise faced accusations over deteriorating human rights conditions. The European Parliament should not adopt double standards to smear the legislation enacted by the HKSAR to safeguard national security. LegCo strongly urges the European Parliament to cease issuing so-called recommendation on Hong Kong affairs that distort facts and spread alarmist narratives.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Regarding the case of Lai Chee-ying and the case involving 45 individuals convicted of conspiracy to commit subversion mentioned in the Recommendation, the LegCo spokesperson pointed out that Hong Kong is a society underpinned by the rule of law, where laws must be observed and offenders must be held accountable. Hong Kong courts handle all offenses endangering national security strictly in accordance with the law, conducting trials fairly and impartially, and releasing comprehensive judgments that meticulously articulate the reasoning behind verdicts and sentences. The courts&#39; convictions are well-grounded, measured and free from any interference. The European Parliament&#39;s recommendation, however, makes unreasonable demands for the release of several convicted individuals, placing politics above court judgments and repeatedly exerting political pressure on Hong Kong&#39;s judicial independence. This is a trampling on rule of law.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As pointed out by the spokesperson of the Chinese Mission to the EU, China-EU relations is at an important stage of development. Maintaining the sound and steady development of China-EU relations serves the common interests of both sides. The LegCo spokesperson stressed that rather than expending excessive time concocting recommendation full of biases regarding China and Hong Kong, the European Parliament should devote more effort to learn about China&#39;s actual circumstances, obtain a better understanding of the stability and prosperity of Hong Kong society following the implementation of the HKNSL, and recognize the benefits brought by China-EU economic and trade co-operation to the people of both sides.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The LegCo spokesperson urges the European Parliament to earnestly abide by principles of international law and basic norms governing international relations, fully respect China&#39;s sovereignty, stop interfering in China&#39;s internal affairs (including HKSAR affairs), stop inciting confrontation and take concrete actions to safeguard the overall interests of China-EU relations. This is the common wishes shared by the people of both China and the EU.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Construction Industry Safety Excellence Award recognises exemplary construction safety performance (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900301.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900301.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:01:13 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Labour Department (LD) and the Occupational Safety and Health Council (OSHC) jointly held the Construction Industry Safety Excellence Award 2026 (the Award) Forum and Award Presentation Ceremony today (October 9) to recognise organisations and frontline workers excelling in occupational safety and health (OSH) management and practices, and to further enhance the safety culture within the construction industry. The Award Presentation Ceremony was officiated by the Secretary for Labour and Welfare, Mr Chris Sun, and the Chairman of the OSHC, Dr David Mong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The LD and the OSHC launched the new Construction Industry Safety Excellence Award this year by integrating the Construction Industry Safety Award Scheme and the Construction Safety Promotional Campaign, which have been implemented for over 20 years. Supported and co-organised by various government departments, organisations, trade associations and labour unions, the new Award aims at promoting continuous improvement in OSH performance across the industry.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Speaking at the ceremony, Mr Sun said that the Government attaches great importance to the OSH of the construction industry and has carried out extensive preventive work. Based on a risk-based principle, the LD will continue to formulate and adjust strategies in a timely manner through a multipronged approach covering inspection and enforcement, publicity and promotion, education and training, as well as innovative technology applications to promote OSH culture and enhance OSH performance. He said that the new Construction Industry Safety Excellence Award can create a synergy effect, attracting more contractors, teams and workers to actively participate, as well as further driving the industry to continuously improve its OSH performance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The themes of this year&#39;s Award are &quot;Fall Prevention on Construction Sites&quot;, &quot;Safe Operation of Heavy Machinery&quot; and &quot;Good Housekeeping on Construction Sites&quot; with a total of nine award categories. The Award event received enthusiastic support from contractors, teams and workers which recorded over 800 entries. Awards were presented to 25 organisations, 23 teams and 97 workers. Another highlight of the Award event was the Forum held before the Award Presentation Ceremony in which representatives of the shortlisted organisations shared their valuable experiences in implementing workplace safety measures and promoting construction safety culture, encouraging industry members to learn from one another and promote good safety practices to other construction sites.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For details of the Award and the list of awardees, please visit the Award&#39;s website&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;(<a href="https://www.oshc.org.hk/eng/main/awards_campaigns/cisea_2026/" target="_blank">www.oshc.org.hk/eng/main/awards_campaigns/cisea_2026</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340833.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340833.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340837.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340837.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340834.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340834.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340835.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340835.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340836.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900301_photo_1340836.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tax Exemption (Wang Fuk Court) Order gazetted]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900697.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900697.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:50:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Chief Executive in Council has made the Tax Exemption (Wang Fuk Court) Order (the Order) pursuant to the Inland Revenue Ordinance (Cap. 112), and the Order is gazetted today (October 9). The Order waives the property owners and residents of Wang Fuk Court in Tai Po, as well as persons who died in the fire at Wang Fuk Court, from the balance of tax payable for the year of assessment (YA) 2024/25 and the tax payable for the YA 2025/26 (including provisional tax and final tax). The Order covers tax types including salaries tax, property tax, profits tax, and tax under personal assessment.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government announced the above tax waivers in <a href="https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202512/03/P2025120300817.htm" target="_blank">December last year</a> and <a href="https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202605/04/P2026050400392.htm" target="_blank">this May</a> in order to ease the financial burden on people affected by the fire.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Order will be tabled at the Legislative Council on October 14 for negative vetting.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CE thanks LegCo for passing and supporting motion on Hong Kong's First Five-Year Plan and Motion of Thanks in respect of 2026 Policy Address]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900576.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900576.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[　　The Chief Executive, Mr John Lee, today (October 9) expressed his gratitude to the Legislative Council (LegCo) for passing and supporting the motion on the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) (2026-2030) and the Motion of Thanks in respect of the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
　　Mr Lee said, &quot;The HKSAR Government formulated Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan, which proactively aligns with the National 15th Five-Year Plan, leading Hong Kong in better seizing development opportunities and better integrating into and serving the overall national development. This is of great significance. Hong Kong&rsquo;s First Five-Year Plan is a forward-looking, strategic, and directional guiding document, while the 2026 Policy Address serves as an annual action plan on the implementation of the Five-Year Plan. The two documents are closely interconnected, and during the formulation process, opinions from all sectors were widely gathered. This year&#39;s Policy Address is entitled &#39;A Strategic Vision for a Bright New Era, Driving Reform and Boosting Development, Unleashing Opportunities and Enhancing Livelihood&#39;. It sets out wide-ranging policy measures across various fields to take forward the initiatives for the first year in line with our five-year blueprint, which creates more development opportunities, enables the public to share economic dividends, and enhances social well-being.<br />
　　<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;I express my gratitude to LegCo Members for engaging in in-depth discussions and offering diverse views during the debate on Hong Kong&rsquo;s First Five-Year Plan and the Policy Address for three consecutive days. Their discussions covered areas including advancing the development of the &#39;four centres and one hub&#39;; leveraging Hong Kong&#39;s international advantages; expediting the development of the Northern Metropolis; deepening the development of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area and participating in high-quality co-operation under the Belt and Road Initiative; enhancing social well-being in a tangible way; as well as adopting a holistic approach to development and security. The Government will carefully analyse and study all views to ensure more effective implementation of relevant measures.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;My team and I will continue to work closely with the LegCo to ensure positive interaction between the executive authorities and the legislature, with mutual checks and balances and complementarity. I firmly believe that, under the principles of an executive-led system and &#39;patriots administering Hong Kong&#39;, the executive authorities and the legislature will continue to pool their strengths and work together to lead the community in better developing the economy and improving people&#39;s livelihood.&quot;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Local premieres of two Film Development Fund-supported films]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900570.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900570.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Film Development Council (HKFDC):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Two local films supported by the Film Development Fund (FDF), namely &quot;Bird of Paradise&quot; and &quot;Good Trip&quot;, will hold their local premieres on October 10 and October 13 respectively, showcasing the creative achievements of emerging Hong Kong filmmakers and the effectiveness of the FDF in nurturing film talent.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;Bird of Paradise&quot; is a winning film project in the Higher Education Institution Group of the 7th First Feature Film Initiative (FFFI) funded by the Government. Produced by Saville Chan, and directed and written by Joey Wu, the film stars Catherine Chau, Elaine Jin, Chan Charm-man, Yeung Nga-man and Jayden Cheung. It tells the story of a middle-aged housewife pursuing her dreams while shouldering family responsibilities. The film was selected for screening at the 28th Shanghai International Film Festival, the 25th New York Asian Film Festival and the 9th Malaysian International Film Festival, and will be officially released in Hong Kong on October 23.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Another FDF-supported film, &quot;Good Trip&quot;, has received funding support under the Directors&#39; Succession Scheme (DSS). Produced by Mabel Cheung, co-directed by Wong Hoi and Norris Wong, written by Norris Wong, and starring Nick Cheung and Chung Suet-ying, the film follows an art assistant who juggles responsibilities on the film set and at the hospital while caring for her father. The father and daughter embark on an unexpected road trip. The film will be officially&nbsp;released in Hong Kong on October 15.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Chairman of the HKFDC, Dr Wilfred Wong, said that &quot;Bird of Paradise&quot; is a heartwarming and inspiring film that demonstrates a woman&#39;s courage and perseverance in pursuing self-fulfillment, fully reflecting the creativity and insight of a new generation of filmmakers. &quot;Good Trip&quot; delicately captures the rapport and genuine affection between a father and daughter. The film&#39;s touching story, humorous dialogue, coupled with classic elements of Hong Kong cinema, is expected to resonate strongly with audiences.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Dr Wong added that the FDF has been nurturing local film talent through various funding schemes and injecting new momentum into the industry. Since its launch, the FFFI has completed eight editions, supporting 30 winning teams in producing their films and incubating 32 new directors. Meanwhile, the DSS enables emerging directors to gain practical experience through collaboration with veteran film practitioners. Dr Wong said he was pleased to see the Government&#39;s continued support for the development of the film industry through the FDF and its efforts to nurture more outstanding film talent for the long-term and sustainable development of Hong Kong cinema.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For more information about the FFFI and the DSS, please visit the HKFDC&#39;s website at&nbsp;<a href="https://www.fdc.gov.hk/en/applications_detail.php?id=2022042210252142851" target="_blank">www.fdc.gov.hk/en/applications_detail.php?id=2022042210252142851</a> and <a href="https://www.fdc.gov.hk/en/dss/index.html" target="_blank">www.fdc.gov.hk/en/dss/index.html</a>.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[DH launches Mental Recharge Station in support of World Mental Health Day 2026 and calls on public to participate in fostering inclusive community (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900637.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900637.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:10:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To echo World Mental Health Day 2026, the Department of Health (DH) today (October 9) launched a three-day mental health promotion event, the Mental Recharge Station, at MOSTown in Ma On Shan. Featuring free interactive experiences, games and workshops, the event encourages members of the public to pay more attention to mental health and listen more to those around them, so that everyone, including those in need, can find a &quot;mental recharge station&quot; where they can share. The DH appealed to members of the public to join the event over the weekend and work together to build an inclusive and caring community that better safeguards mental health.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The World Federation for Mental Health designates October 10 every year as World Mental Health Day to raise awareness of mental health issues and mobilise efforts to support people experiencing emotional or mental health distress. This year&rsquo;s theme is &quot;Lived Experiences Heard: Real Voices, Real Change&quot;, highlighting the importance of listening attentively to the personal experiences and needs of people experiencing emotional or mental health distress. It also seeks to promote understanding, dispel misconceptions and stigma and help them access appropriate support in the community.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Director of Health, Dr Ronald Lam said, &quot;Mental health is an integral part of overall health. The Chief Executive has recently announced the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) and the 2026 Policy Address, which clearly set out the need to strengthen community psychological and mental health support and implement 11 measures to promote mental health. We will make every effort to implement the relevant objectives and continuously enhance public awareness and overall mental health.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;He emphasised, &quot;Mental wellbeing is an essential part of overall health. Everyone may experience stress, changes in mood or psychological distress at different stages of life. While many people make a conscious effort to exercise regularly and watch their diet to maintain their physical health, they often neglect to look after their psychological wellbeing and emotions. Members of the public are encouraged to give equal importance to mental and physical health. In addition to developing healthy lifestyle habits and adopting positive emotional management practices, they should also show greater care for those around them. If family members, friends, colleagues or classmates experience sustained emotional distress, they can express concern and listen patiently, avoid criticism, dismissive remarks or stigmatising words or actions, and encourage those concerned to seek professional help early, such as through the &#39;18111 - Mental Health Support Hotline&#39;.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) Government attaches great importance to public mental health. Through an integrated and multidisciplinary approach, the Government has strengthened mental health promotion, prevention, early identification, timely intervention and support services. The HKSAR Government launched the &quot;18111 - Mental Health Support Hotline&quot; in December 2023, providing 24-hour one-stop emotional and mental health support and referral services. To further enhance the convenience and comprehensiveness of the service, four new functions were introduced in November last year, namely WhatsApp support, multi-language support, scheduled callback, and additional optional services. From its launch to September 30 this year, the hotline&#39;s professional staff have handled nearly approximately 352 900 calls and 6 250 WhatsApp messages, providing immediate support.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On the promotion and prevention front, the DH and the Advisory Committee on Mental Health jointly implement the &quot;Shall We Talk&quot; mental health promotion and public education initiative. Through a one-stop thematic website (<a href="https://www.shallwetalk.hk/en/" target="_blank">shallwetalk.hk</a>), media and social media platforms, as well as school and community activities, the initiative disseminates relevant health messages and enhances public understanding of mental health. As at the end of September this year, the thematic website had recorded more than 4.4 million visits, while activities for primary and secondary schools and tertiary institutions had reached more than 100 000 students. The Government also promotes mental health in the workplace through the Mental Health Workplace Charter to foster a positive workplace culture of care, inclusion, active listening and communication, and create an inclusive and supportive working environment for employees experiencing emotional distress.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, the Government launched the Healthy Mind Pilot Project (Pilot Project) in August 2024 to offer free initial mental health assessments at the community level and provide evidence-based psychological intervention for people experiencing mild to moderate psychological distress, so as to achieve early identification and early intervention to prevent further deterioration. The Pilot Project aims to detect people with psychological distress at an early stage and enhance their ability in managing their mental wellness through low-intensity psychological intervention. Those assessed as high-risk cases will be referred to relevant district professional service partners for multidisciplinary support services, so that they can receive more comprehensive and professional care and support in the community.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government will continue to work with different sectors of the community and stakeholders to strengthen mental health promotion, eliminate public stigma surrounding mental health and encourage people with mental health needs to seek help early, with a view to building a mental health-friendly community.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Mental Recharge Station will be held from today until October 11 (Sunday) from noon to 8pm daily. For more information on the Mental Recharge Station and mental health, please visit the <a href="https://www.shallwetalk.hk/en/" target="_blank">&quot;Shall We Talk&quot; thematic website</a>.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340823.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340823.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340824.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340824.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340825.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900637_photo_1340825.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by PSCST at opening ceremony of Hong Kong Performing Arts Expo 2026 (English only)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900616.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900616.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:08:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the speech by the Permanent Secretary for Culture, Sports and Tourism, Ms Vivian Sum, at the opening ceremony of the Hong Kong Performing Arts Expo 2026 today (October 9):<br />
<br />
郭永紅部長 (Director General of the Department of Publicity, Cultural and Sports Affairs of the Liaison Office of the Central People&#39;s Government in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, Mr Guo Yonghong), Kenneth (Chairman of the Hong Kong Arts Development Council, Mr Kenneth Fok), distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Good evening. On behalf of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government, I am pleased to welcome you all to the opening ceremony of the Hong Kong Performing Arts Expo (HKPAX) 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Two years ago, we launched the inaugural HKPAX with a clear vision to establish Hong Kong as a premier hub for performing arts exchanges and collaboration. Today, I am truly delighted to see that vision taking root and bearing fruit. The inaugural HKPAX not only successfully built a global platform for deepening international cultural exchanges and co-operation, it also effectively facilitated numerous touring agreements and collaborative projects between local and international organisations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This year, HKPAX returns with renewed momentum under the theme &quot;Passion in Motion&quot;. In alignment with the National 15th Five-Year Plan Outline and Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan, this edition empowers Hong Kong to harness its distinctive role as a &quot;super connector&quot; and expedites Hong Kong&#39;s development into an East-meets-West centre for international cultural exchanges. By hosting HKPAX, not only are we expanding the global reach of our mega arts and cultural events; we are also contributing to fortifying our nation&#39;s status as a cultural powerhouse.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A highlight of this year&#39;s edition is the Hong Kong Chapter, which is a dedicated platform for local artists and art groups to showcase their artistic visions and latest creations. Another highlight is one of this year&#39;s main venues, the East Kowloon Cultural Centre. Commissioned last year, this new venue specialises in performances that integrate arts and technology, reflecting our strategic focus on &quot;Arts Tech&quot;.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;HKPAX even extends beyond the theatre doors and spreads into the community. Just before the kick-off this evening, we have already rolled out an exciting four-day programme featuring free public performances at the iconic Hong Kong Cultural Centre Piazza, attracting locals and tourists alike.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government remains steadfast in strengthening our cultural ecosystem, nurturing creative talent, fostering artistic innovation and boosting international engagement. Backed by the unmatched advantage of &quot;one country, two systems&quot;, we will continue to leverage our extensive international connections to enhance our cultural soft power and tell the good stories of China and Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As we look among ourselves on this occasion, we see so many distinguished artists, representatives of arts organisations, presenters and industry leaders from every corner of the world. Your presence affirms the growing stature of HKPAX as a vital nexus for exchanges, dialogue and partnership within the international performing arts community. As you immerse yourselves in our rich line-up of programmes, I suggest you also take the opportunity to join the Greater Bay Area Performing Arts Tour to explore new horizons for touring and collaboration while experiencing the dynamic cultural landscape of Macao and Zhuhai.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In closing, I sincerely thank the Hong Kong Arts Development Council, all participating artists and arts groups, sponsors and partner organisations, and every individual involved for their dedication and hard work that have made this second edition of the Expo possible.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​I wish HKPAX 2026 every success. May our shared passion continue to inspire, connect and transform. Enjoy the extraordinary programmes and the vitality of our city beyond the Expo.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Incoming passenger convicted and jailed for importing duty-not-paid cigarettes and alternative smoking products (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900609.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900609.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:05:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​An incoming male passenger was sentenced to 11 months&#39; imprisonment and fined $2,000 by the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Courts today (October 9) for importing duty-not-paid cigarettes, failing to declare them to Customs officers, as well as importing alternative smoking products, in contravention of the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (DCO) and the Import and Export Ordinance (IEO).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs officers intercepted a 37-year-old Mainland male passenger arriving in Hong Kong from Tokyo, Japan, at Hong Kong International Airport yesterday (October 8) and seized 49 215 duty-not-paid cigarettes and 6 000 alternative smoking products, with an estimated market value of about $239,400 and a duty potential of about $162,700, from his personal baggage. The passenger was subsequently arrested.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs welcomes the sentence. The custodial sentence has imposed a considerable deterrent effect and reflects the seriousness of the offences.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs reminds members of the public that under the DCO, cigarettes are dutiable goods to which the DCO applies. Any person who imports, deals with, possesses, sells or buys illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the IEO, any person who imports an alternative smoking product into Hong Kong commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected illicit cigarette activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account (<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900609_photo_1340832.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900609_photo_1340832.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Judiciary alerts public to phishing email with fraudulent summons]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900606.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900606.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:04:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Judiciary:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Judiciary today (October 9) called on the public to stay vigilant against a phishing email sent from the email account &quot;soong.wing@biznetvigator.com&quot;. The email falsely claims that it was issued by the Registrar of the District Court, and was attached with a fraudulent summons purportedly issued by the Judiciary.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Judiciary clarified that the phishing email and the suspicious summons were not issued by the Judiciary. The case has been reported to the Police.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Members of the public are reminded to stay alert to suspicious emails and avoid opening them&nbsp;or following any instructions or requests from them. They should report the matter to the Police if in doubt.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Interdepartmental vegetation fire and mountain rescue exercise 2026 held (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900441.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900441.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An interdepartmental vegetation fire and mountain rescue exercise was held today (October 9). Participating departments and organisations comprised the Fire Services Department (FSD); the Hong Kong Police Force; the Government Flying Service; the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department; the Auxiliary Medical Service; the Civil Aid Service; the Home Affairs Department; the Social Welfare Department; and the Hospital Authority, mobilising a total of about 150 personnel.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The exercise was conducted in the area around Lion Rock Country Park, simulating a scenario where 25 hikers encountered a vegetation fire. The FSD deployed about 90 fire and ambulance personnel in total, as well as a large drone to drop water bombs for the first time to prepare for operational deployments during the Chung Yeung Festival. The FSD also applied various innovative technologies including the Patient Tagging System for Multiple Casualties Incidents, the Mountain Rescue Mobile App, viewshed analysis, the Mobile Phone Locator, along with AI to analyse fire conditions, locate persons awaiting rescue, and enhance search and rescue efficiency.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The exercise aimed to enhance the co-ordination, communication, command and control, and logistical support capabilities among different departments and organisations during large-scale hill fire and mountain rescue operations. It also helped test the effectiveness and co-operation of different emergency formations during firefighting and rescue operations, as well as the application of innovative technology and AI. Members of the Wong Tai Sin District Council and the Wong Tai Sin District Fire Safety Committee also attended the exercise as observers to assist in promoting hill fire prevention and safety awareness in mountaineering in the community.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FSD reminded the public that given the recent dry weather, should a vegetation fire occur, it could become fierce with the wind and spread rapidly. Therefore, members of the public are urged not to leave any ignition sources behind during grave sweeping or hiking activities and must evacuate to a safe place in the event of a fire.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340826.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340826.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340827.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340827.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340828.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340828.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340829.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340829.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340830.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340830.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340831.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900441_photo_1340831.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Auction for Victoria Park Chinese New Year Fair stalls concludes]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900573.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900573.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The open auction for stalls at the 2027 Victoria Park Chinese New Year (CNY) Fair concluded today (October 9). The total revenue was $8,651,552.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department said that a total of 400 stalls at the Fair, including 170 for regular size dry goods, 46 for large size dry goods, 180 for wet goods and four for fast food, were let out during the three-day open auction.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To summarise the results of the auction held during the past two days (October 8 and 9), the successful bids for the regular size dry goods stalls ranged from $8,540 to $64,000, which averaged out to $21,017. The highest bid of $64,000 was about 7.5 times the opening price of $8,540. The auction of regular dry goods stalls fetched a total of $3,572,920. For the large size dry goods stalls, the successful bids ranged from $22,000 to $50,000, which averaged out to $29,870. The highest bid of $50,000 was about 3.9 times the opening price of $12,810. The auction of large size dry goods stalls fetched a total of $1,374,000.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The three auction days for the Victoria Park CNY Fair stalls attracted about 1 320 participants in total.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auction for stalls of the Tat Tung Road Garden CNY Fair in Tung Chung will be held on Monday (October 12) at Assembly Hall, 2/F, Lai Chi Kok Government Offices, 19 Lai Wan Road, Lai Chi Kok, Kowloon. The auction session is scheduled from 9am until completion of the auction.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auctions for stalls of the CNY fairs in Fa Hui Park and Cheung Sha Wan Playground in Sham Shui Po and Kwun Tong Recreation Ground in Kwun Tong will be held at the above-mentioned venue from October 13 to 15. The auction sessions are scheduled from 9am to 12.30pm (AM session) and 2pm until completion of the auction (PM session).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auction for stalls of the To Kwa Wan Recreation Ground CNY Fair in Kowloon City will be held at the above-mentioned venue on October 16 (Friday). The auction session is scheduled from 9am until completion of the auction.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman reminded the successful bidders to comply with all stipulations and provisions as set out in the licence agreement. Otherwise, the department is entitled to terminate the agreement and the licensee shall immediately vacate the stall.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[20 persons arrested during anti-illegal worker operations (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900516.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900516.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; The Immigration Department (ImmD) mounted a series of territory-wide anti-illegal worker operations codenamed &quot;Puncture&quot;, &quot;Contribute&quot;, a joint operation with the Hong Kong Police Force and the Labour Department codenamed &quot;Powerplayer&quot;, joint operations with the Hong Kong Police Force codenamed &quot;Sahara&quot;, &quot;Windsand&quot;, &quot;Champion&quot;, and a joint operation with Hong Kong Customs, the Hong Kong Police Force, the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department and the Leisure and Cultural Services Department from October 2 to yesterday (October 8). During the anti-illegal worker operations, ImmD officers raided multiple target locations including restaurants, flats under renovation and beauty parlours, etc. Seventeen suspected illegal workers, two suspected employers and one overstayer were arrested. The arrested suspected illegal workers comprised 15 men and two women, aged 22 to 58. Among them, five men and one woman were holding recognisance forms which prohibit them from taking any employment. Two men, aged 48 and 64, were suspected of employing the illegal workers and were also arrested. Furthermore, the arrested overstayer was a man aged 49. An investigation into the suspected employers is ongoing, and the possibility of further arrests is not ruled out.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; An ImmD spokesman said, &quot;Any person who contravenes a condition of stay in force in respect of him or her shall be guilty of an offence. Also, visitors are not allowed to take employment in Hong Kong, whether paid or unpaid, without the permission of the Director of Immigration. Offenders are liable to prosecution and upon conviction face a maximum fine of $50,000 and up to two years&#39; imprisonment. Aiders and abettors are also liable to prosecution and penalties.&quot;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; The spokesman warned, &quot;As stipulated in section 38AA of the Immigration Ordinance, an illegal immigrant, a person who is the subject of a removal order or a deportation order, an overstayer or a person who was refused permission to land is prohibited from taking any employment, whether paid or unpaid, or establishing or joining any business. Offenders are liable upon conviction to a maximum fine of $50,000 and up to three years&#39; imprisonment. As stipulated in section 20(1)(a) of the Immigration Ordinance, the Chief Executive may make a deportation order against an immigrant, prohibiting the immigrant from being in Hong Kong at any time thereafter if the immigrant has been found guilty in Hong Kong of an offence punishable by imprisonment for not less than two years.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; The spokesman stressed that it is a serious offence to employ people who are not lawfully employable. Under the Immigration Ordinance, the maximum penalty for an employer employing a person who is not lawfully employable, i.e. an illegal immigrant, a person who is the subject of a removal order or a deportation order, an overstayer or a person who was refused permission to land, has been significantly increased from a fine of $350,000 and three years&#39; imprisonment to a fine of $500,000 and 10 years&#39; imprisonment to reflect the gravity of such offences. The director, manager, secretary, partner, etc, of the company concerned may also bear criminal liability. The High Court has laid down sentencing guidelines that the employer of an illegal worker should be given an immediate custodial sentence.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; According to the court sentencing, employers must take all practicable steps to determine whether a person is lawfully employable prior to employment. Apart from inspecting a prospective employee&#39;s identity card, the employer has the explicit duty to make enquiries regarding the person and ensure that the answers would not cast any reasonable doubt concerning the lawful employability of the person. The court will not accept failure to do so as a defence in proceedings. It is also an offence if an employer fails to inspect the job seeker&#39;s valid travel document if the job seeker does not have a Hong Kong permanent identity card. Offenders are liable upon conviction to a maximum fine of $150,000 and to imprisonment for one year. In that connection, the spokesman would like to remind all employers not to defy the law by employing illegal workers. The ImmD will continue to take resolute enforcement action to combat such offences.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Under the existing mechanism, the ImmD will, as a standard procedure, conduct an initial screening of vulnerable persons, including illegal workers, illegal immigrants, sex workers and foreign domestic helpers, who are arrested during any operation with a view to ascertaining whether they are trafficking in persons (TIP) and/or forced labour victims. When any TIP and/or forced labour indicator is revealed in the initial screening, the ImmD officers will conduct a full debriefing and identification by using a standardised checklist to ascertain the presence of TIP and/or forced labour elements. Identified TIP and/or forced labour victims will be provided with various forms of support and assistance, including urgent intervention, medical services, counselling, shelter or temporary accommodation and other supporting services. The ImmD calls on TIP and/or forced labour victims to report crimes to the relevant departments immediately.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; For reporting illegal employment activities, please call the dedicated hotline 185 185, fax at 2824 1166, email&nbsp;<a href="mailto:anti_crime@immd.gov.hk" target="_blank">anti_crime@immd.gov.hk</a>, or submit the &quot;Online Reporting of Immigration Offences&quot; form at&nbsp;<a href="https://www.immd.gov.hk/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.immd.gov.hk</a>.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900516_photo_1340821.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900516_photo_1340821.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Update on mosquito-borne diseases, dengue fever and chikungunya fever]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900420.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900420.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health today (October 9) announced the latest situation regarding dengue fever (DF) and chikungunya fever (CF). Although Hong Kong has entered autumn, the weather has yet to cool down and the rainy season has not yet ended, thus favourable for mosquito breeding. Members of the public are reminded to take appropriate measures to prevent mosquito bites and mosquito breeding, both in Hong Kong and when travelling to areas affected by mosquito-borne diseases, in order to prevent mosquito-borne diseases.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
Dengue fever<br />
----------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;From October 2 to yesterday, the CHP recorded one imported DF case. The patient had visited Vietnam during the incubation period.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Hong Kong has recorded 33 DF cases so far this year, comprising 31 imported cases and two local cases. A total of 59 DF cases were recorded last year (2025), all of which were imported cases. In 2024, 161 DF cases were recorded, comprising 156 imported cases and five local cases.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;According to the World Health Organization (WHO), the global incidence of DF has markedly increased over the past two decades, posing a substantial public health challenge. In 2024, the WHO recorded over 14 million DF cases, which was a record number. In 2025, over five million cases and over 4 000 DF-related deaths were reported in over 90 countries/territories. From January to August 2026, around 2.2 million cases and over 1 000 DF-related deaths have been recorded globally.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; DF has long been prevalent in many neighbouring tourist destinations. So far this year, Sri Lanka and Vietnam each recorded over 100 000 cases; Bangladesh and Cambodia each recorded over 80 000 cases; Indonesia and Malaysia each recorded over 70 000 cases; and Singapore recorded over 2 600 cases. Meanwhile, according to the Guangdong Provincial Disease Control and Prevention Administration, as of August 31, 2026, Guangdong Province has recorded 431 cases, of which 150 were recorded in August.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Detailed information on the latest DF situation in Hong Kong, as well as neighbouring and overseas countries and areas, has been uploaded to the CHP&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/files/pdf/df_imported_cases_and_overseas_figures_eng.pdf" target="_blank">website</a>.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Chikungunya fever<br />
-----------------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From October 2 to yesterday, the CHP recorded one imported CF case, involving a 73-year-old male. The patient had visited the Chinese Mainland during the incubation period. The CHP considered that he was infected during travel. The case was classified as an imported case. The patient had been admitted to the hospital for treatment in a mosquito-free environment. He recovered and discharged.&nbsp;His household contact has completed medical surveillance and is asymptomatic. The CHP&#39;s epidemiological investigation is ongoing. The case has been reported to the Chinese Mainland health authority.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Hong Kong has recorded five confirmed CF cases so far this year, including four imported cases and one local case. Hong Kong recorded a total of 82 confirmed CF cases last year. Among them, 11 were local cases and the rest were imported cases.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;According to the WHO, a total of 502 264 CF cases and 186 CF-related deaths were reported in 41 countries/territories last year. Cases were reported in the Americas, Africa, Asia and Europe. According to the Guangdong Provincial Disease Control and Prevention Administration, as of August 31, 2026, Guangdong Province has recorded 739 cases, with 496 recorded in August, a sharp increase from the 36 and 171 cases recorded in June and July respectively.<br />
<br />
Preventive measures to be taken by the public<br />
----------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The CHP reminds members of the public to maintain good environmental hygiene, and to take appropriate measures to prevent mosquito bites and mosquito breeding, both in Hong Kong and when travelling to areas affected by mosquito-borne diseases, in order to prevent mosquito-borne diseases. Mosquito-borne diseases such as DF and CF are endemic in various countries worldwide. The risk of importation persists. Members of the public should check the situation at their destinations before travelling abroad, especially when travelling to Southeast Asian countries. Additionally, whether travelling or not, members of the public should use DEET-containing insect repellents or other effective active ingredients properly to prevent mosquito bites, but the following precautions should be taken when using them:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>read the label instructions carefully first;</li>
	<li>apply right before entering an area with a risk of mosquito bites;</li>
	<li>apply on exposed skin and clothing;</li>
	<li>use DEET of up to 30 per cent for pregnant women and up to 10 per cent for children (for children who travel to countries or areas where mosquito-borne diseases are endemic or epidemic, and where exposure is likely, those aged 2 months or above can use DEET-containing insect repellents with a DEET concentration of up to 30 per cent);</li>
	<li>apply sunscreen first, then insect repellent;</li>
	<li>reapply only when needed and follow the instructions; and</li>
	<li>in addition to DEET, there are other insect repellents available on the market containing different active ingredients, such as IR3535 and picaridin. When using any insect repellent, the public should follow the usage instructions and precautions on the product label.</li>
</ul>
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) appeals to members of the public to continue to stay alert and work together to carry out mosquito prevention and control measures early, including inspecting their homes and surroundings to remove potential breeding grounds, changing water in vases and scrubbing their inner surfaces, removing water in saucers under potted plants at least once a week, and properly disposing of containers such as empty cans and lunch boxes. The FEHD also advises members of the public and property management agencies to keep drains free of blockage and level all defective ground surfaces to prevent the accumulation of water. They should also scrub all drains and surface sewers with an alkaline detergent at least once a week to remove any mosquito eggs.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;​The public should call 1823 in case of mosquito problems, and may visit the following pages for more information: the CF page of the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/healthtopics/content/24/6122.html" target="_blank">CHP</a>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.travelhealth.gov.hk/english/travel_related_diseases/chikungunyafever.html" target="_blank">Travel Health Service</a>, the latest&nbsp;<a href="https://www.travelhealth.gov.hk/english/outbreaknews/outbreaknews.html" target="_blank">Travel Health News</a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/features/38927.html" target="_blank">tips for using insect repellents</a>, and the CHP&nbsp;<a href="https://www.facebook.com/CentreforHealthProtection?_rdr" target="_blank">Facebook Page</a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.instagram.com/centreforhealthprotection/" target="_blank">Instagram Account</a>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<a href="https://www.youtube.com/c/ChpGovHkChannel" target="_blank">YouTube Channel</a>, and also the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.fehd.gov.hk/english/pestcontrol/risk-pest-mosquito.html" target="_blank">Mosquito Prevention and Control dedicated page</a>&nbsp;of the FEHD.<br />
&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Asia+ Festival's "Canvas of Sound" presents Middle Eastern music and improvisatory calligraphy for cross-disciplinary dialogue (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900466.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900466.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:20:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The fourth Asia+ Festival will present the &quot;Canvas of Sound&quot; on October 23 and 24, a cross-disciplinary music performance in which Pakistani-born visual artist Tazeen Qayyum will join Syrian-born musicians Feras Charestan and Basel Rajoub to combine Middle Eastern music with live Arabic calligraphy, creating a stirring piece that refreshes the mind.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Charestan brings out the shimmering tones of the qanun, while Rajoub draws the resonant depth of the duclar. Together with percussion and saxophone, they weave a musical tapestry of Middle Eastern music. Qayyum&#39;s calligraphy fuses Arabic, Persian and Urdu scripts. Improvising in response to melodies, she lets sound and brushwork echo one another, bringing four themes - hope, kindness, justice and peace - to life on canvas. Each work is created spontaneously during the performance, making every canvas unique, and will be displayed the following day.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Curated by the Aga Khan Music Programme, the gallery edition of the &quot;Canvas of Sound&quot; premiered in 2024 at the National Museum of Qatar in Doha. Its stage edition has received an overwhelming response at the 2025 Edinburgh International Festival, and will be performed in Hong Kong as its Asian premiere.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The &quot;Canvas of Sound&quot; will be held at 8pm on October 23 and 24 at the Sai Wan Ho Civic Centre Theatre. Tickets priced at $260 and $400 are available at URBTIX (<a href="https://www.urbtix.hk/" target="_blank">www.urbtix.hk</a>). For telephone bookings, please call 3166 1288 or use the mobile ticketing app URBTIX. A meet-the-artist session will be held after both performances.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, the programme features a range of fringe activities, including a display of canvases, a workshop and an outreach performance. For details, please refer to <a href="https://www.asiaplus.gov.hk/2026/en/canvas-of-sound" target="_blank">www.asiaplus.gov.hk/2026/en/canvas-of-sound</a>.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Presented by the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau and organised by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department, the Asia+ Festival is held annually from September to November. Themed &quot;Traditions Recast&quot;, this year&#39;s festival brings together over 30 countries and regions, and presents more than 100 performances and related activities, showcasing the artistic essence of Asia and countries and regions along the Belt and Road. For programme enquiries and ticketing concessions, please call 2370 1044 or visit <a href="https://www.asiaplus.gov.hk/2026/en/" target="_blank">www.asiaplus.gov.hk</a>.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340813.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340813.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340814.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340814.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340815.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340815.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340816.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900466_photo_1340816.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[RTHK signs Memorandum of Understanding with Shenzhen Media Group (with photos) ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900468.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900468.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK) and the Shenzhen Media Group (SZMG) today (October 9) signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) on co-operation in areas including programme resources, talent exchanges and programme production. The two broadcasters aim to jointly promote the further integration of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA), showcasing the latest developments and vibrancy of the GBA to the world, and telling the good stories of the country and the GBA.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Building on the MOU, the two broadcasters have agreed to collaborate across multiple areas to jointly strengthen the broadcasting influence in both cities. The co-operation agreements cover the following:<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(1) RTHK and SZMG will share a range of quality programmes produced by each side, including television dramas, history and culture documentaries, biographical documentaries and popular science programmes, enriching the choices of audiences in both cities;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(2) To plan the joint production of GBA-themed television and radio programmes covering culture, tourism and performing arts;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(3) To strengthen news and current affairs information sharing, with the two sides jointly conducting news coverage and producing feature programmes during the 2026 Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in Shenzhen; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;(4) To jointly produce special programmes celebrating the 30th anniversary of the establishment of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region in 2027 to promote people-to-people connectivity.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Director of Broadcasting, Ms Angelina Kwan, signed the MOU with the Party Secretary of the Party Committee and President of SZMG, Ms Shang Boying, in Shenzhen. Ms Kwan said the partnership fully harnesses the respective strengths of the broadcasting organisations in Shenzhen and Hong Kong, jointly providing richer and higher-quality broadcasting content for audiences in the GBA. RTHK will continue to expand its collaboration network with more Chinese Mainland media organisations and leverage Hong Kong&#39;s unique advantage of being closely connected with the Chinese Mainland and the world to tell the good stories of the country, the GBA, and Hong Kong on the global stage, enhancing international communication capacity.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition to signing MOUs with Chinese Mainland media organisations, RTHK is also seeking opportunities to broadcast more of its own programmes across various media platforms in the Chinese Mainland. Among them, the sports documentary &quot;Out of Bounds&quot; has successfully launched on Migu, the Mainland&#39;s largest online sports platform, as well as the online media platforms of several Mainland football associations. The programme offers audiences across China a glimpse into the daily lives, training, and matches of Hong Kong footballers playing for the Chinese Football Super League, illustrating how football connects different Chinese Mainland cities with Hong Kong. RTHK will continue to produce more quality programmes to foster cultural exchanges and people-to-people bonds between Hong Kong and the Chinese Mainland.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900468_photo_1340811.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900468_photo_1340811.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900468_photo_1340812.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900468_photo_1340812.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Remarks by S for S at media session on official opening of Huanggang Port (with video)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900495.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900495.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:06:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Following are the remarks by the Secretary for Security, Mr Tang Ping-keung, at a media session on the official opening of the Huanggang Port this afternoon (October 9):<br />
<br />
Reporter: How many travellers and vehicles does the Government expect to use the port on the first day of its opening? And what will be done to ensure that there will be a smooth opening amid the expected flock of visitors?&nbsp;<br />
<br />
Secretary for Security: Thank you for your questions. First of all, I think we have to look at the number of passengers and vehicles at the current port. For the current number of passengers, we have a daily average of about 40 000 and we have 65 000 at the highest, while the new port has the capacity to handle 200 000 passengers. On the first day, we already have that capacity. Regarding vehicles, we have an average of about 9 000 cars per day, and at the highest, we have about 12 000 cars. But now we have the capacity to handle up to 15 000 vehicles. That capacity will be utilised on the first day, so we have full confidence that we can handle the number of passengers and vehicles.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Regarding how to ensure there is a smooth operation of the port area, in fact, before its opening, we have done a lot of preparatory measures, including a lot of drills and exercises. We also have a stress test with up to 20 000 people per hour, which is about two or three times the maximum passenger flow currently we have. We will have maximum manpower on that day to handle unexpected numbers of passengers. We also have a robust system in handling passengers, public transport vehicles and private cars.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In fact, in the opening time, we also engage a lot of ambassadors that we call &quot;port ambassadors&quot;, who are familiar with the operation of the port area, to assist members of the public in using the facilities. So, we have full confidence in the smooth operation of the new port.<br />
<br />
(Please also refer to the Chinese portion of the remarks.)]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Online auctions of vehicle registration marks to be held from October to December 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900539.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900539.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Transport Department (TD) today (October 9) announced that six online auctions of vehicle registration marks will be held from October to December 2026 through the E-Auction platform (<a href="https://e-auction.td.gov.hk/" target="_blank">e-auction.td.gov.hk</a>). The auction list has been uploaded to the TD&#39;s <a href="https://www.td.gov.hk/en/public_services/vehicle_registration_mark_n/ca/index.html" target="_blank">website</a>.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Interested bidders can participate in the online auction only after they have successfully registered as E-Auction users. Members of the public may refer to the E-Auction website for details on registration and bidding procedures, as well as bidding and payment means. For enquiries, please call the E-Auction hotline (3583 3980) or email <a href="mailto:e-auction-enquiry@td.gov.hk" target="_blank">e-auction-enquiry@td.gov.hk</a>.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Online auction results will be uploaded to the E-Auction website after the closure of an auction session.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tentative issuance schedule for HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds and appointment of Primary Dealers under Infrastructure Bond Programme and Government Sustainable Bond Programme]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900511.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900511.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), as representative of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government (HKSAR Government), published today (October 9) a tentative issuance schedule for institutional Government Bonds (GBs) to be offered under the Infrastructure Bond Programme (IBP) and Government Sustainable Bond Programme (GSBP) for the six-month period from October 2026 to March 2027 (Annex 1). The GBs are denominated in Hong Kong dollars (HKD) and renminbi (RMB) and will be offered by way of competitive tendering. The tentative issuance schedule contains information on, among others, the tentative tenors, tender dates, issuance sizes, issue dates and methods of issuance of individual GB issues. Further details are set out in the Information Memorandum published on the Hong Kong Government Bonds website, <a href="https://www.hkgb.gov.hk/" target="_blank">www.hkgb.gov.hk</a>.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKMA also re-appoints 13 financial institutions as Primary Dealers under the institutional part of the IBP and GSBP to assist in the implementation of the GB tenders (Annex 2). Primary Dealers are also expected to provide two-way bid-and-offer quotations in the secondary market to facilitate GB trading. The latest appointment of Primary Dealers covers the two-year period from October 2026 to September 2028.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The information contained in the tentative issuance schedule, such as the tenors, tender dates, issuance sizes, issue dates and methods of issuance are tentative. The HKMA may make adjustments having regard to market conditions. A revised issuance schedule may be issued where appropriate. Details of individual issues, including details on the use of proceeds of the bonds, are to be confirmed and announced in the relevant tender notices prior to the scheduled tender dates.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Advisory Committee on Water Supplies fully supports Hong Kong's First Five-Year Plan to develop Hong Kong into international hub for high-quality development of country's water sector]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900352.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900352.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Advisory Committee on Water Supplies:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Advisory Committee on Water Supplies (ACWS) discussed at its meeting today (October 9) the major policy strategies and initiatives relating to water development set out in the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) (Hong Kong&#39;s Five-Year Plan) and the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address. Members unanimously expressed support for the relevant strategies and initiatives.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The ACWS is pleased to note the Government&#39;s commitment to developing Hong Kong into an international hub for high-quality development of the country&#39;s water sector, developing advanced standards for water-related affairs applicable to the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, and capitalising on Hong Kong as an international demonstration platform for Mainland water-related technologies, where pilot testing, transformation and application can be conducted. These initiatives will assist technologies and enterprises of the Mainland and Hong Kong in going global and provide the international water sector with high-quality and cost-effective water solutions. At the meeting, members offered suggestions to the Government on the relevant strategies and initiatives.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Chairman of the ACWS, Mr Edmund Leung, said, &quot;The Water Supplies Department (WSD) and the Drainage Services Department will jointly organise the inaugural Hong Kong Water Expo in April next year. The event will provide a new high-level platform for exchanges among water professionals around the world, promoting the close integration of strategic thinking, research and development achievements, and practical applications. It will also facilitate water-related technologies and enterprises of the Mainland and Hong Kong in expanding into international markets. We look forward to the WSD continuing its proactive efforts in taking forward the water-related directions set out in Hong Kong&#39;s Five-Year Plan and in developing Hong Kong into an international hub for high-quality development of the country&#39;s water sector.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Leung added that the ACWS will continue to provide professional advice to the Government on matters relating to water supplies and promote the continuous enhancement of Hong Kong&#39;s water supply services. The ACWS will fully support and align with Hong Kong&#39;s Five-Year Plan, actively contributing to the future development of both the nation and Hong Kong.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKICL alerts public of fraudulent websites]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900528.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900528.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:52:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Hong Kong Interbank Clearing Limited (HKICL) has recently noted two fraudulent websites purported to be from HKICL:
<ul>
	<li>tuikuanfpsnew.sbs</li>
	<li>tuikuanfpsnew.cfd</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;These fraudulent websites imitate as &quot;Buyer Online Protection&quot; to provide services including 1) refund to buyer, 2) unauthorised online transaction reporting, and 3) online transaction support, for online transaction over FPS payment. They intend to trick user into giving away personal document number and ID photo together with phone number for real name verification in order to get cash reward. In addition, they also trick user into providing bank name, bank account number and bank account holder&#39;s name to top up and withdraw money from their platforms&#39; virtual wallet. Further, they direct user to a fraudster impersonating as customer service personnel.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HKICL advises that these fraudulent websites have no affiliation with HKICL, or any business of HKICL. HKICL will not directly provide FPS service to individual members of the public or contact individual members of the public proactively under usual circumstance. The genuine official website addresses of HKICL are <a href="https://www.hkicl.com.hk/eng/" target="_blank">www.hkicl.com.hk</a> and <a href="https://fps.hkicl.com.hk/eng/fps/index.php" target="_blank">fps.hkicl.com.hk</a>.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;HKICL urges the public to beware of suspicious calls, websites or other communications, and to remain vigilant in protecting personal information. If members of the public receive any suspicious communication purportedly from HKICL, please call HKICL&#39;s general line at 2533 1111 to verify. Members of the public who suspect that they&nbsp;have become the victim of fraudulent acts should report promptly to the Police.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[AFCD conducts unattended dog capture operation in Yuen Long and Sha Tau Kok]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900500.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900500.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:51:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) conducted a special joint operation in the vicinity of Nam Sang Wai Road in Yuen Long and Luk Keng in Sha Tau Kok with the Police yesterday afternoon (October 8). During the operation, one unattended mongrel dog was captured and transferred to the Animal Management Centre for detention and observation. The captured dog was not microchiped.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The AFCD will check and verify lost-and-found records. If there is sufficient evidence, the keeper will be prosecuted. If the keeper cannot be traced, staff and veterinary officers at the Animal Management Centres will patiently observe the dog. Generally speaking, dogs that are confirmed to be in good health and of a gentle temperament will be transferred to the partner animal welfare organisations for sterilisation and arrangement for adoption by the public.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An AFCD spokesman stated that in a high-density urban environment, unowned and uncontrolled dogs may pose risks to public safety. Once dogs are allowed to roam unattended in public places, they may cause harm to members of the public or other animals. Furthermore, dogs that are not vaccinated against rabies pose a threat to public health and animal health. The AFCD will not release dogs without keepers back into the community to avoid posing public safety risks. In this regard, the AFCD adopts a risk-based strategy and will conduct special operations to capture unattended dogs at high-risk locations. The AFCD will also work closely with the Police to conduct joint special enforcement operations when necessary, requiring dog keepers to fulfil their responsibilities to safeguard community safety.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the Rabies Ordinance (Cap. 421) and the Rabies Regulation (Cap. 421A), all dogs over five months old must be microchipped, vaccinated against rabies and licensed. Dogs must thereafter be re-vaccinated and have their licences renewed at intervals of not more than three years. In addition, dogs in public places must be held on a leash or otherwise kept under effective control. Dogs must not be left unattended in public places, nor abandoned. Offenders are liable on conviction to a maximum fine of $10,000 for each offence, and a keeper who abandons a dog is liable to imprisonment for six months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The AFCD reminds members of the public that, if they encounter an unattended dog in a public place, they should remain calm and keep a safe distance from the dog. They should report the case by calling 1823. In an emergency - for example, if the dog is displaying aggressive behaviour or poses an immediate danger - members of the public should call the Police for assistance immediately. Members of the public who wish to learn more about the proper control of dogs and the responsibilities of keepers may visit the AFCD website (<a href="https://www.pets.gov.hk/english/index.html" target="_blank">www.pets.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Import of poultry meat and products from areas in Canada suspended]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900432.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900432.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department announced today (October 9) that in view of a notification from the World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH) about outbreaks of highly pathogenic H5N1 avian influenza in the Rural Municipality of Woodlands in the Province of Manitoba and the County of Vermilion River in the Province of Alberta in Canada, the CFS has instructed the trade to suspend the import of poultry meat and products (including poultry eggs) from the above-mentioned areas with immediate effect to protect public health in Hong Kong.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A CFS spokesman said that according to the Census and Statistics Department, no poultry meat or eggs were imported into Hong Kong from Canada in the first six months of this year.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The CFS has contacted the Canadian authority over the issue and will closely monitor information issued by the WOAH and the relevant authorities on the avian influenza outbreaks. Appropriate action will be taken in response to the development of the situation,&quot; the spokesman said.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FEHD releases first batch of gravidtrap indexes for Aedes albopictus in October (with photos) ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900520.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900520.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:43:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) today (October 9) released the first batch of gravidtrap indexes and density indexes for Aedes albopictus in October, covering 17 survey areas, as follows:&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:500px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">District</td>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Survey Area</td>
			<td colspan="2" rowspan="1">October 2026</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>First Phase Gravidtrap Index&nbsp;</td>
			<td>First Phase Density Index</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Central and Western</td>
			<td>Central and Admiralty</td>
			<td>0.0%</td>
			<td>N/A</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Kennedy Town and Shek Tong Tsui</td>
			<td>5.6%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="3">Eastern</td>
			<td>Chai Wan</td>
			<td>1.6%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>North Point and Quarry Bay</td>
			<td>3.3%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Shau Kei Wan and Sai Wan Ho</td>
			<td>7.1%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Southern</td>
			<td>Deep Water Bay and Repulse Bay</td>
			<td>1.7%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Wan Chai</td>
			<td>Tin Hau and Causeway Bay</td>
			<td>2.0%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Wan Chai</td>
			<td>0.0%</td>
			<td>N/A</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Kwun Tong</td>
			<td>Lam Tin and Sau Mau Ping</td>
			<td>9.4%</td>
			<td>1.2&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Yau Tong and Cha Kwo Ling</td>
			<td>3.4%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Wong Tai Sin</td>
			<td>Diamond Hill and Tsz Wan Shan</td>
			<td>1.7%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Wong Tai Sin East</td>
			<td>2.1%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">North</td>
			<td>Fanling North</td>
			<td>2.3%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Fanling South</td>
			<td>4.7%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Sha Tin</td>
			<td>Sha Tin East</td>
			<td>8.2%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Tsuen Wan</td>
			<td>Sheung Kwai Chung</td>
			<td>1.9%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Tuen Mun</td>
			<td>Tuen Mun South</td>
			<td>5.5%</td>
			<td>1.0&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The FEHD continues to carry out a series of mosquito prevention and elimination measures, including:
<ul>
	<li>interdepartmental collaboration: convening special meetings of the interdepartmental task forces on anti-mosquito work to co-ordinate relevant departments and stakeholders (such as construction site contractors, schools and property management companies) in strengthening mosquito control work at venues;</li>
	<li>precise risk assessments: carrying out intensive and targeted mosquito control work at locations with more serious mosquito infestations, including stepping up inspections, eliminating mosquito breeding and potential breeding grounds, applying larvicides, conducting fogging operations to eradicate adult mosquitoes, and placing mosquito trapping devices at appropriate locations;</li>
	<li>promotion and education: organising exhibitions with relevant departments and stakeholders, and distributing leaflets and posters to enhance public awareness of mosquito control and prevention; and</li>
	<li>rapid alert mechanism: notifying residential estates that have subscribed to the gravidtrap Rapid Alert System, and advising property management companies and residents to stay vigilant and work together in taking mosquito prevention and elimination measures.</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Public participation is crucial to the effective control of mosquito problems. The FEHD appeals to members of the public to continue to work together in strengthening personal mosquito control measures, including:
<ul>
	<li>tidying up their premises and checking for any accumulation of water inside the premises;</li>
	<li>removing all unnecessary water collections and eliminating the sources;</li>
	<li>checking household items (those placed in outdoor and open areas in particular), such as refuse containers, vases, air conditioner drip trays, and laundry racks to prevent stagnant water;</li>
	<li>changing water in flower vases and scrubbing their inner surfaces thoroughly, and removing water in saucers under potted plants at least once a week;</li>
	<li>properly covering all containers that hold water to prevent mosquitoes from accessing the water;</li>
	<li>properly disposing of articles that can contain water, such as disposable meal boxes and empty cans; and</li>
	<li>scrubbing drains and surface sewers with alkaline detergent at least once a week to remove any mosquito eggs.</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Starting in August 2025, following the completion of the surveillance of individual survey areas, and once the latest gravidtrap index and the density index are available, the FEHD has been disseminating relevant information through press releases, its website and social media. It aims to allow members of the public to quickly grasp the mosquito infestation situation and strengthen mosquito control efforts, thereby reducing the risk of chikungunya fever (CF) transmission.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following recommendations from the World Health Organization and taking into account the local situation in Hong Kong, the FEHD sets up gravidtraps in districts where mosquito-borne diseases have been recorded in the past, as well as in densely populated places such as housing estates, hospitals and schools to monitor the breeding and distribution of Aedes albopictus mosquitoes, which can transmit CF and dengue fever. At present, the FEHD has set up gravidtraps in 62 survey areas of the community, with a surveillance period of two weeks. During the surveillance period, the FEHD will collect the gravidtraps once a week. After the first week of surveillance, the FEHD will immediately examine the glue boards inside the retrieved gravidtraps for the presence of adult Aedine mosquitoes to compile the Gravidtrap Index (First Phase) and Density Index (First Phase). At the end of the second week of surveillance, the FEHD will instantly check the glue boards for the presence of adult Aedine mosquitoes. Data from the two weeks of surveillance will be combined to obtain the Area Gravidtrap Index and the Area Density Index. The Gravidtrap and Density indexes for Aedes albopictus in different survey areas, as well as information on mosquito prevention and control measures, are available on the department&#39;s webpage&nbsp; (<a href="https://www.fehd.gov.hk/english/pestcontrol/dengue_fever/Dengue_Fever_Gravidtrap_Index_Update.html#" target="_blank">www.fehd.gov.hk/english/pestcontrol/dengue_fever/Dengue_Fever_Gravidtrap_Index_Update.html#</a>).&nbsp;<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340817.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340817.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340818.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340818.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340819.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340819.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340820.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900520_photo_1340820.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[DH continues to clamp down on illegal smoking in no smoking areas]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900498.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900498.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Tobacco and Alcohol Control Office (TACO) of the Department of Health (DH) continued to clamp down on illegal smoking in no smoking areas by conducting another enforcement operation, codenamed &quot;Cloudcrush&quot;, in Causeway Bay and Tsim Sha Tsui last night (October 8) and issued a total of five fixed penalty notices (FPNs).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; During the operation, officers from TACO (including plainclothes officers) carried out inspections and enforcement action at bars in the vicinity of Causeway Bay and Tsim Sha Tsui areas and issued a total of five FPNs to persons illegally smoking waterpipes and cigarette, as well as possessing an e-cigarette capsule. TACO&#39;s investigation is ongoing, and prosecution may also be taken against operators of the bars that are suspected of aiding and abetting smoking offences. TACO will also refer the cases to the Liquor Licensing Board for appropriate follow-up action.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Under the Smoking (Public Health) Ordinance (Ordinance), conducting a smoking act in a statutory no smoking area (such as indoor areas of bars or restaurants) is prohibited. The Ordinance also prohibits the possession of specified alternative smoking products (ASPs) (including e-cigarette capsules, liquids, heat sticks and herbal cigarettes) in public places. Any person contravening the above provisions is liable to a fixed penalty of $3,000. Moreover, where smoking products (including waterpipes) are sold, in bars or otherwise, the restrictions on the promotion and sale of smoking products stipulated in the Ordinance apply. Offenders are liable on summary conviction to a maximum fine of $50,000. Venue managers of statutory no smoking areas are empowered by the Ordinance to request a smoking offender cease the act; if the offender is not co-operative, the manager may contact the Police for assistance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In addition, under the Criminal Procedure Ordinance, any person who aids, abets, counsels or procures the commission by another person of any offence shall be guilty of the same offence.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; During the inspections conducted from January 2024 to date, the DH issued a total of over 300 fixed penalty notices for smoking waterpipe tobacco illegally in indoor no smoking areas, in addition to around 150 summonses to staff of the bars for relevant offences including aiding and abetting smoking in indoor no smoking areas.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;Members of the public must not try or continue consuming ASPs or waterpipes based on the misconception that these products contain less harmful substances. On the contrary, ASPs have been proven to emit numerous toxic substances that can lead to addiction, illness, or even death. A waterpipe is also a smoking product, and its combustion of fuel (e.g. charcoal) releases carbon monoxide. A typical one-hour waterpipe smoking session exposes the user to 100 to 200 times the volume of smoke inhaled from a single conventional cigarette. Moreover, sharing a waterpipe apparatus increases the risk of transmitting infectious diseases, such as tuberculosis. People should seek medical attention immediately if they suspect they are developing symptoms of carbon monoxide poisoning,&quot; said the Head of TACO, Dr Manny Lam.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;He stressed that TACO will continue to closely monitor and rigorously enforce the law against illegal activities such as waterpipe smoking in no smoking areas, illegal sales and promotions of smoking products, as well as illegal imports of ASPs.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Dr Lam urged smokers to quit smoking as early as possible for their own health and that of others. Members of the public may call the DH&#39;s Integrated Smoking Cessation Hotline on 1833 183, or visit <a href="https://www.taco.gov.hk/t/english/index.html" target="_blank">TACO&#39;s website</a> for information on smoking cessation.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Results of monthly survey on business situation of small and medium-sized enterprises for September 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900336.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900336.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Census and Statistics Department (C&amp;SD) released today (October 9) the results of the Monthly Survey on Business Situation of Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs) for September 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The current diffusion index (DI) on business receipts amongst SMEs decreased from 44.1 in August 2026 in the contractionary zone to 43.7 in September 2026, whereas the one-month&rsquo;s ahead (i.e. October 2026) outlook DI on business receipts was 47.4. Analysed by sector, the current DIs on business receipts for some surveyed sectors dropped in September 2026 as compared with previous month, particularly for the real estate (from 48.5 to 45.4) and restaurants (from 44.0 to 41.3).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The current DI on new orders for the import and export trades decreased from 44.8 in August 2026 to 44.3 in September 2026, whereas the outlook DI on new orders in one month&rsquo;s time (i.e. October 2026) was 47.7.<br />
<br />
Commentary<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A Government spokesman said that current business sentiment among SMEs eased somewhat in September. The current diffusion index on business receipts for SMEs edged down from the preceding month, while the outlook diffusion index remained relatively stable, with improvement recorded in the import and export trades, retail trade and wholesale trade sectors. The overall diffusion index on employment also improved slightly.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Looking ahead, the continued solid growth in the Hong Kong economy should support business confidence, though the ongoing uncertainties in the external environment may still affect near-term sentiment. The Government will continue to closely monitor the situation.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
Further information<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Monthly Survey on Business Situation of Small and Medium-sized Enterprises aims to provide a quick reference, with minimum time lag, for assessing the short-term business situation faced by SMEs. SMEs covered in this survey refer to companies with fewer than 50 persons engaged. Respondents were asked to exclude seasonal fluctuations in reporting their views. Based on the views collected from the survey, a set of diffusion indices (including current and outlook diffusion indices) is compiled. A reading above 50 indicates that the business condition is generally favourable, whereas that below 50 indicates otherwise. As for statistics on the business prospects of prominent companies in Hong Kong, users may refer to the publication entitled &quot;Report on Quarterly Business Tendency Survey&quot; released by the C&amp;SD.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The results of the survey should be interpreted with care. The survey solicits feedback from a panel sample of about 600 SMEs each month and the survey findings are thus subject to sample size constraint. Views collected from the survey refer only to those of respondents on their own companies rather than those on the respective sectors they are engaged in. Besides, in this type of opinion survey on expected business situation, the views collected in the survey are affected by the events in the community occurring around the time of enumeration, and it is difficult to establish precisely the extent to which respondents&#39; perception of the business situation accords with the underlying trends. For this survey, main bulk of the data were collected around the last week of the reference month.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;More detailed statistics are given in the &quot;Report on Monthly Survey on the Business Situation of Small and Medium-sized Enterprises&quot;. Users can browse and download the publication at the website of the C&amp;SD (<a href="https://www.censtatd.gov.hk/en/EIndexbySubject.html?pcode=B1080015&amp;scode=300" target="_blank">www.censtatd.gov.hk/en/EIndexbySubject.html?pcode=B1080015&amp;scode=300</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Users who have enquiries about the survey results may contact Industrial Production Statistics Section of the C&amp;SD (Tel: 3903 7246; email: <a href="mailto:sme-survey@censtatd.gov.hk">sme-survey@censtatd.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Grading of beach water quality released]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900341.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900341.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Environmental Protection Department (EPD) today (October 9) released the latest grading of water quality for 39 gazetted beaches (see Note 1) and one non-gazetted beach (see Note 2).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
