</table>
&nbsp;<br />
Grade 2 beaches are:
<table border="0" style="width:520px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Anglers&#39; Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Golden Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Approach Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Hoi Mei Wan Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Big Wave Bay Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Lido Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Butterfly Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Ma Wan Tung Wan Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Cafeteria Old Beach</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Silver Mine Bay Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Casam Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Silverstrand Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Castle Peak Beach</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Ting Kau Beach*</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Clear Water Bay Second Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">Turtle Cove Beach</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:23px; width:302px">Deep Water Bay Beach*</td>
			<td style="height:23px; width:293px">&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
&nbsp;<br />
<a target="_blank">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</a>Compared with the grading released last week,<a target="_blank"> </a>Kadoorie Beach, Kiu Tsui Beach and Kwun Yam Beach have been upgraded from Grade 2 to Grade 1.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the present grading system, beaches are classified into four grades, namely Good (Grade 1), Fair (Grade 2), Poor (Grade 3) and Very Poor (Grade 4), according to the level of E. coli in the water. Grades are calculated on the basis of the geometric mean of the E. coli counts on the five most recent sampling occasions.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;While the ratings represent the general water quality at the beaches, an EPD spokesman reminded members of the public that water quality could be temporarily affected during and after periods of heavy rain. Bathers should avoid swimming at beaches for up to three days after a tropical cyclone or heavy rainfall.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A summary of beach grades is published weekly before the weekend. The latest beach grades based on the most current data may be obtained from the EPD&#39;s website on Beach Water Quality (<a href="https://www.epd.gov.hk/epd/beach" target="_blank">www.epd.gov.hk/epd/beach</a>) or the beach hotline, 2511 6666. Members of the public can also obtain the latest daily water quality forecast information for all beaches that are open for swimming through the EPD&#39;s dedicated webpage on the beach water quality forecast (<a href="https://www.epd.gov.hk/en/BWQForecast" target="_blank">www.epd.gov.hk/en/BWQForecast</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Note 1: Lifesaving services are provided by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department at 29 gazetted beaches, marked with an asterisk (*) above, from September 1. For details, please refer to the Leisure and Cultural Services Department&#39;s Special Announcement (<a href="https://www.lcsd.gov.hk/en/beach/index.html" target="_blank">www.lcsd.gov.hk/en/beach/index.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Note 2: This refers to Discovery Bay Tai Pak Beach which has no lifesaving service.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Draft Mai Po and Fairview Park Outline Zoning Plan approved]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900268.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900268.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Chief Executive in Council has approved the draft Mai Po and Fairview Park Outline Zoning Plan (OZP).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The approved OZP provides a statutory land use planning framework to guide the development and redevelopment within the Mai Po and Fairview Park area&quot;, a spokesman for the Town Planning Board said today (October 9).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The planning scheme area, covering about 1 148 hectares, is bounded by San Tin Highway, the San Tin Technopole and the Ngau Tam Mei area including the Ngau Tam Mei New Development Area to the east, Shenzhen River to the north, Mai Po Nature Reserve to the west and Nam Sang Wai area to the south.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved OZP has incorporated amendments shown on the draft Mai Po and Fairview Park OZP No. S/YL-MP/9, which mainly involve (i) rezoning a site at Kam Pok Road from &quot;Residential (Group D)&quot; to &quot;Residential (Group C)1&quot; for a private residential development with commercial use, transport lay-by, neighbourhood elderly centre and open space; and (ii) upzoning a site at Wo Shang Wai zoned &quot;Other Specified Uses&quot; annotated &quot;Comprehensive Development to include Wetland Restoration Area&quot; to facilitate a private residential development with a 100-place residential care home for the elderly and a wetland restoration area.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Notes and Explanatory Statement of the OZP have been amended to reflect the above amendments. Opportunity is also taken to update the general information of various land use zonings and the planning circumstances, where appropriate.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved Mai Po and Fairview Park OZP No. S/YL-MP/10 is available for public inspection during office hours at (i) the Secretariat of the Town Planning Board, (ii) the Planning Enquiry Counters, (iii) the Fanling, Sheung Shui and Yuen Long East District Planning Office, (iv) the Yuen Long District Office, and (v) the San Tin Rural Committee.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Copies of the approved OZP are available for sale at the Map Publications Centre in North Point. The electronic version of the OZP can be viewed on the Town Planning Board&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.tpb.gov.hk/" target="_blank">www.tpb.gov.hk</a>).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Draft Hung Shui Kiu and Ha Tsuen Outline Zoning Plan approved]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900262.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900262.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Chief Executive in Council has approved the draft Hung Shui Kiu and Ha Tsuen Outline Zoning Plan (OZP).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The approved OZP provides a statutory land use planning framework to guide the development and redevelopment within the Hung Shui Kiu and Ha Tsuen area,&quot; a spokesman for the Town Planning Board said today (October 9).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The planning scheme area, covering an area of about 707 hectares, is bounded by the Tin Shui Wai New Town to the east, Castle Peak Road to the south, knolls of Yuen Tau Shan to the west and Lau Fau Shan Road/hill slope along Deep Bay Road to the north.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved OZP has incorporated amendments shown on the draft Hung Shui Kiu and Ha Tsuen OZP No. S/HSK/3, which mainly involve (i) rezoning the area from &quot;Other Specified Uses&quot; annotated &quot;Port Back-up, Storage and Workshop Uses&quot; to &quot;Other Specified Uses&quot; annotated &quot;Industry Park&quot; to facilitate the development of Hung Shui Kiu Industry Park; (ii) rezoning three sites in Planning Area 4B from &quot;Residential (Group B)3&quot; to &quot;Residential (Group A)6&quot; for private residential developments; (iii) rezoning the southern site in Planning Area 34E from &quot;Government, Institution or Community&quot; (&quot;G/IC&quot;) to &quot;G/IC(1)&quot; for the reprovisioning of the divisional fire station and ambulance depot cum staff quarters; (iv) revision to the building height restriction from three storeys to 50 metres above Principal Datum for &quot;G/IC&quot; zone to the east of Sha Chau Lei for the redevelopment of Pok Oi Hospital Yeung Chun Pui Care and Attention Home; and (v) rezoning a site to the south of Tuen Ma Line Tin Shui Wai Station from &quot;Comprehensive Development Area&quot; to &quot;Residential (Group A)7&quot; to reflect the completed residential development.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Notes and Explanatory Statement of the OZP have been amended to reflect the above amendments. Opportunity is also taken to update the general information of various land use zonings and the planning circumstances, where appropriate.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved Hung Shui Kiu and Ha Tsuen OZP No. S/HSK/4 is available for public inspection during office hours at (i) the Secretariat of the Town Planning Board, (ii) the Planning Enquiry Counters, (iii) the Tuen Mun and Yuen Long West District Planning Office, (iv) the Yuen Long District Office, (v) the Tuen Mun District Office, (vi) the Ha Tsuen Heung Rural Committee, and (vii) the Ping Shan Heung Rural Committee.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Copies of the approved OZP are available for sale at the Map Publications Centre in North Point. The electronic version of the OZP can be viewed on the Town Planning Board&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.tpb.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.tpb.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Draft Tsuen Wan Outline Zoning Plan approved]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900240.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900240.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Chief Executive in Council has approved the draft Tsuen Wan Outline Zoning Plan (OZP).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The approved OZP provides a statutory land use planning framework to guide the development and redevelopment within the Tsuen Wan area&quot;, a spokesman for the Town Planning Board said today (October 9).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The planning scheme area, covering about 743 hectares, includes Tsuen Wan Valley and its adjoining areas and forms part of the Tsuen Wan New Town. The area extends from Wo Yi Hop Road in the east to Yau Kom Tau in the west.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved OZP has incorporated amendments shown on the draft Tsuen Wan OZP No. S/TW/40, which mainly involve (i) rezoning a site at Fu Yung Shan from &quot;Green Belt&quot; (&quot;GB&quot;) and &quot;Village Type Development&quot; to &quot;Residential (Group B)9&quot; for a private residential-cum-residential care home for the elderly and public vehicle park development; (ii) rezoning a site in Yau Kom Tau from &quot;GB&quot; and &quot;Government, Institution or Community&quot; (&quot;G/IC&quot;) to &quot;&quot;Residential (Group B)10&quot; (&quot;R(B)10&quot;) for a private residential development with social welfare facilities; and (iii) rezoning a strip of land to the north of the &quot;R(B)10&quot; zone from &quot;GB&quot; to &quot;G/IC&quot; for rationalising the zoning boundaries.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Notes and Explanatory Statement of the OZP have been amended to reflect the above amendments. Opportunity is also taken to update the general information of various land use zonings and the planning circumstances, where appropriate.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The approved Tsuen Wan OZP No. S/TW/41 is available for public inspection during office hours at (i) the Secretariat of the Town Planning Board, (ii) the Planning Enquiry Counters, (iii) the Tsuen Wan and West Kowloon District Planning Office, (iv) the Tsuen Wan District Office and (v) the Tsuen Wan Rural Committee.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Copies of the approved OZP are available for sale at the Map Publications Centre in North Point. The electronic version of the OZP can be viewed on the Town Planning Board&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.tpb.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.tpb.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Effective Exchange Rate Index]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900204.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900204.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The effective exchange rate index for the Hong Kong dollar on Friday, October 9, 2026 is 100.4&nbsp;(down 0.1 against yesterday&#39;s index).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKSAR LegCo firmly rejects and strongly condemns false allegations against Hong Kong in Trafficking in Persons Report 2026 of Department of State of US]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900444.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900444.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Legislative Council Secretariat:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A spokesperson for the Legislative Council (LegCo) of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) today (October 9) expressed firm opposition to and strong condemnation of the completely false, slanderous and biased content regarding HKSAR&#39;s efforts to tackle trafficking in persons (TIP) in the Trafficking in Persons Report 2026 (Report) issued by the Department of State of the United States (US). LegCo firmly supports the statement issued by the HKSAR Government to set the record straight and clarify fallacies.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The LegCo spokesperson stressed that the rating of Hong Kong at Tier 3 in the Department of State of the US&#39;s so-called Report is completely groundless and an attempt to smear Hong Kong&#39;s good reputation. The content of the Report deviate grossly from the actual situation in Hong Kong and the international community&#39;s positive evaluation of the city. In fact, as pointed out by the HKSAR Government, TIP has never been a prevalent problem in Hong Kong, nor is there any sign that Hong Kong is being actively used by syndicates as a destination or transit point for TIP.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In its statement, the HKSAR Government forcefully rebutted the allegations with objective figures, demonstrating the proactive and multi-pronged strategy to combat TIP activities it adopted, as well as the substantial resources in tackling TIP and enhancing the protection for foreign domestic helpers in Hong Kong. Among these efforts, the HKSAR Government conducted around 12 100 initial screenings in 2025 (a 7 per cent increase over last year) and identified 15 TIP victims, all of whom were Hong Kong residents falling prey to employment fraud in Southeast Asian countries. The HKSAR Government&#39;s swift actions in effectively assisting these residents to return safely to Hong Kong, identifying them as TIP victims, thoroughly investigating these cases and prosecuting the people involved highlight Hong Kong&#39;s firm determination to combat TIP and safeguard the lives, safety and human rights of citizens. As an integral part of the HKSAR&#39;s governing team, LegCo will, as always, render full support to the HKSAR Government in combating TIP activities through policy advice and legislative work.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The LegCo spokesperson pointed out that the selective citation of data in the so-called Report and the unwarranted criticisms against Hong Kong&#39;s victim identification efforts are deeply hypocritical and reflect double standards, especially when studies and reports in the US have highlighted systemic underreporting of TIP cases and shortcomings by law enforcement agencies in the US. Yet, the Department of State of the US made wanton accusations against Hong Kong&#39;s rigorous work. Hong Kong will never accept such politically-motivated selective targeting.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The LegCo spokesperson also pointed out that the so-called Report made untruthful remarks regarding the Hong Kong National Security Law and the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance, completely disregarding the laws safeguarding national security in Hong Kong have explicitly stipulated that human rights shall be respected and protected in safeguarding national security. The rights and freedoms, including the freedoms of speech, of the press, of publication, of association, of assembly, of procession and of demonstration, enjoyed by Hong Kong people under the Basic Law and the provisions of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights as applicable to the HKSAR are protected in accordance with the law. Relevant court cases have clearly shown that laws safeguarding national security target only a very small number of organisations and individuals that endanger national security. Normal activities of any law-abiding persons or organisations will not unwittingly violate the law.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The LegCo spokesperson urges the US to respect facts, stop issuing false reports filled with political bias, abide by international law and the basic norms governing international relations, and immediately cease interfering in Hong Kong affairs, which are purely China&#39;s internal affairs. The US should also refrain from using double standards to smear and vilify the actual situation in the HKSAR and its long-standing efforts to combat TIP, so as to avoid undermining mutual trust and co-operation in the international community&#39;s fight against TIP.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Special traffic arrangements for Chung Yeung Festival]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900493.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900493.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:47:59 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To facilitate grave-sweeping activities during the Chung Yeung Festival period, the Police today (October 9) reminded the public that special traffic arrangements will be implemented in phases in the following districts from today until November 8 to ensure smooth vehicular traffic movements and pedestrian safety.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Chai Wan<br />
---------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
A. Temporary prohibited zone<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The section of Cape Collinson Road east of Lin Shing Road i.e. the left-turning movement from Lin Shing Road to Cape Collinson Road leading to the Chinese Permanent Cemetery and its vicinity will be temporarily designated as private cars and light goods vehicles prohibited zone from 7am to 3pm daily from today until November 8. All private cars and light goods vehicles, except those with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport, will be prohibited from entering into the prohibited zone.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Road closures<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Phase 1 - From 7am to 5pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 19, 24, 25 and 31 and November 1 (Sundays, public holidays and specific weekends before and after Chung Yeung Festival), except for those vehicles specified below:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
- Lin Shing Road southbound, except franchised buses, green minibus (GMB) routes 16A, 16M, 16X and 18M, taxis, hearses, funeral vehicles, vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport and vehicles gaining access to Wan Tsui Estate and Lin Tsui Estate;<br />
- Lin Shing Road northbound, except GMB route 18M, hearses, funeral vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport;<br />
- The section of Cape Collinson Road east of Lin Shing Road (i.e. the left&shy;turning movement from Lin Shing Road to Cape Collinson Road leading to the vicinity of the Chinese Permanent Cemetery), except GMB route 18M, taxis, hearses, funeral vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport;<br />
- The section of Cape Collinson Road west of Lin Shing Road, except franchised buses, GMB routes 16A, 16M and 16X, taxis, hearses, funeral vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport;<br />
- The slip road leading from Cape Collinson Road to Gardens of Remembrance and the Crematorium, except hearses and vehicles carrying passengers to service at the Crematorium; and<br />
- The slip road leading to the Chai Wan Chinese Permanent Cemetery.<br />
<br />
Phase 2 - From 7am to 5am on the day of Chung Yeung Festival (October 18), except for those vehicles specified below:<br />
<br />
- Lin Shing Road, except franchised buses, GMB routes 16A, 16M and 16X, hearses, funeral vehicles, vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport and vehicles making access to Wan Tsui Estate and Lin Tsui Estate;<br />
- The section of Cape Collinson Road east of Lin Shing Road (i.e. the left&shy;turning movement from Lin Shing Road to Cape Collinson Road leading to the vicinity of the Chinese Permanent Cemetery), except hearses, funeral vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport;<br />
- The section of Cape Collinson Road west of Lin Shing Road, except franchised buses, GMB routes 16A, 16M and 16X, hearses, funeral vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport;<br />
- The slip road leading from Cape Collinson Road to Garden of Remembrance and the Crematorium, except hearses and vehicle carrying passengers to service at the Crematorium;<br />
- The slip road leading to the Chai Wan Chinese Permanent Cemetery; and<br />
- Wan Tsui Lane (except vehicles making access to Hing Wah Plaza).<br />
<br />
C. Traffic diversions<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In connection with road closures and traffic control, traffic diversions will be implemented on following roads:<br />
<br />
- Vehicles along the section of Wan Tsui Road eastbound between Fei Tsui Road and Lin Shing Road cannot turn right to Lin Shing Road southbound during the implementation of road closures under Phases 1 and 2; and<br />
- The section of Cape Collinson Road between the slip road leading to Cape Collinson Crematorium and Shek O Road will be converted as one-way westbound during the implementation of road closures under Phases 1 and 2, and during the operation period of CTB route 388.<br />
<br />
D. No parking<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In connection with the road closures under Phases 1 and 2, no parking will be permitted on the following roads:<br />
<br />
- Wan Tsui Road;<br />
- Lin Shing Road;<br />
- Cape Collinson Road; and<br />
- The section of Shek O Road between Tai Tam Gap Correctional Institution and Tai Tam Road.<br />
<br />
Pok Fu Lam<br />
-----------------<br />
<br />
A. Traffic direction<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The section of Consort Rise between Victoria Road and Bisney Road will be converted to one-way southbound by the Police from 7am to 5.30pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24 and 25 depending on the traffic conditions in the vicinity of the cemeteries.<br />
<br />
B. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;4 metered parking spaces (meter nos. 3941A, 3941B, 3942A and 3942B) and 3 motorcycle parking spaces at Victoria Road near Consort Rise will be temporarily suspended from 7am on October 17 to 5pm on October 19.<br />
<br />
Aberdeen<br />
-------------<br />
<br />
A. Road closures<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Peel Rise in the vicinity of Aberdeen Chinese Permanent Cemetery and the slip road leading from Shek Pai Wan Road to Aberdeen Chinese Permanent Cemetery will be closed to all vehicular traffic by the Police depending on the traffic conditions in the vicinity of the cemetery during the following periods:<br />
<br />
- From 7.30am to 5.30pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 19, 24 and 25; and<br />
- From 7.30am to 6.30pm on October 18.<br />
<br />
Peak Area<br />
---------------<br />
<br />
A. Road closures<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Lugard Road, Harlech Road and Mount Austin Road will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic from 9am to 11pm daily on October 18 and 19 (except for access by local residents or vehicles with permits). Affected vehicles on Peak Road northbound heading for Lugard Road, Harlech Road and Mount Austin Road will be diverted to Peak Road southbound via the slip road next to the carpark entrance of the Peak Galleria.<br />
<br />
B. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The metered parking spaces, motorcycle parking spaces and disabled parking spaces at Mount Austin Road will be temporarily suspended from 9am to 11pm daily on October 18 and 19.<br />
<br />
Tseung Kwan O<br />
---------------------<br />
<br />
A. General closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On October 11, 17, 19, 24 and 25, and November 1 and 8, the access road leading from Ko Chiu Road to the Tseung Kwan O Chinese Permanent Cemetery (the Cemetery) will be closed to all vehicular traffic from 5.30am to 6pm daily (except for emergency vehicles, KMB Special Route No. 14S, hearses, vehicles in funeral processions and vehicles with valid entry permits issued by the Cemetery).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Full closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On October 18, full closure will be implemented on the access road leading from Ko Chiu Road to the Cemetery between 5.30am and 8pm except for emergency vehicles.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Grave sweepers may make use of the BMCPC Footpath opposite to MTR Tiu Keng Leng Station or the access road on Ko Chiu Road to access the Cemetery. As there are staircases on the BMCPC Footpath, wheelchair users should make use of the access road on Ko Chiu Road.<br />
<br />
Diamond Hill<br />
------------------<br />
<br />
I. Between 7am and 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24 and 25<br />
<br />
A. Road closure<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following roads will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for those vehicles specified below):<br />
<br />
- The access road leading to the Diamond Hill Crematorium, the unnamed road between Diamond Hill Funeral Parlour and Hammer Hill Substation, and the access road adjacent to the Diamond Hill Funeral Parlour leading to Sze Chen Hall (except emergency vehicles, hearses and vehicles taking part in funeral processions); and<br />
- The section of Yuk Wah Street east of Tsz Wan Shan Road and Yuk Hok Lane (except private cars, taxis, emergency vehicles and police vehicles).<br />
<br />
B. Traffic diversions<br />
<br />
- Vehicles entering/leaving Fung Shing Street or Fu Shan Estate Carpark shall use Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill only;<br />
- Vehicles entering/leaving Grand View Garden Carpark shall use Po Kong Village Road northbound uphill only; and<br />
- Vehicles on Po Kong Village Road eastbound could route via the easternmost traffic lane of Tsz Wan Shan (South) Bus Terminus onto Yuk Wah Street westbound.<br />
<br />
C. Banning of right-turn vehicular movements<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following roads will be prohibited from right-turning:<br />
<br />
- From Fung Shing Street westbound onto Po Kong Village Road northbound uphill;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road northbound uphill into Fung Shing Street eastbound;<br />
- From Grand View Garden Carpark onto Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill into Grand View Garden Carpark;<br />
- From Fu Shan Estate Carpark onto Po Kong Village Road northbound uphill;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road northbound uphill into Fu Shan Estate Carpark;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill into Po Kong Village Road School Village Carpark;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road School Village Carpark onto Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill;<br />
- From Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill onto unnamed road opposite to Po Leung Lane;<br />
- From unnamed road opposite to Po Leung Lane onto Po Kong Village Road southbound downhill; and<br />
- From Yuk Wah Crescent onto Yuk Wah Street eastbound.<br />
<br />
D. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The metered parking spaces and the motorcycle parking spaces on the unnamed road between the Diamond Hill Funeral Parlour and Hammer Hill Substation and the motorcycle parking spaces in Yuk Wah Street near Yuk Hok Lane will be temporarily suspended.<br />
<br />
E. Designation of temporary pick up/set down points<br />
<br />
- Temporary taxi pick up/ set down point will be designated at Fung Shing Street westbound near Fu Yan House, Fu Shan Estate; and<br />
- Temporary taxi and private car pick up/ set down point will be designated at Yuk Wah Street westbound near Yuk Hok Lane.<br />
<br />
II. Between 7am and 6pm daily on October 11, 18, 19 and 25<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Po Kong Village Road School Village Carpark will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except private cars, taxis, emergency vehicles and police vehicles) to provide a temporary taxi and private car set down point.<br />
<br />
Cheung Sha Wan<br />
-------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following special traffic and transport arrangements will be implemented in the vicinity of the Saint Raphael&rsquo;s Catholic Cemetery from 7am to 7pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24 and 25:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
A. Road closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Wing Ming Street and the unnamed road between Wing Ming Street and the Saint Raphael&rsquo;s Catholic Cemetery will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for hearses, vehicles taking part in funeral processions, vehicles access by the Cemetery staff, emergency vehicles and vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Temporary traffic arrangement<br />
<br />
- Parking will be prohibited in the section of Yu Chau West Street between Castle Peak Road and King Lam Street;<br />
- Passenger pick-up or set-down will be prohibited in the section of Yu Chau West Street near Wing Ming Street (outside 83 Wing Hong Street); and<br />
- &ldquo;No Stopping&rdquo; and &ldquo;No Parking&rdquo; will be strictly enforced on Ching Cheung Road.<br />
<br />
C. Suspension of on-street parking spaces<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;All on-street parking spaces (including metered parking spaces, disabled parking spaces and motorcycle parking spaces) in Yu Chau West Street, Tai Nan West Street, a section of King Lam Street between Yu Chau West Street and Tai Nan West Street and a section of Wing Hong Street between Yu Chau West Street and Tai Nan West Street will be temporarily suspended.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Tuen Mun<br />
--------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(I) Castle Peak<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following special traffic and transport arrangements will be implemented in Tuen Mun from 7am to 7pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31 and November 1 for Chung Yeung Festival:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
A. Road closures<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following sections of roads will be intermittently closed to all vehicular traffic (except emergency vehicles and those authorised by police officers on-site):<br />
<br />
- Tsing San Path; and<br />
- The access roads leading to Ching Chung Koon and Ching Chung Sin Yuen.<br />
<br />
B. Removal of illegally parked vehicles<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Any vehicles found illegally parked at the following locations will be prosecuted and towed away by the Police without prior notice (except emergency vehicles and those authorised by police officers on-site):<br />
<br />
- San Fuk Road;<br />
- The section of Tsing Chung Koon Road between Tsing Chung Path and San Fuk Road;<br />
- The section of Tsing Lun Road between Tsun Wen Road and the Northern vehicle entrance of Tuen Mun Hospital; and<br />
- The section of Tsun Wen Road southbound between Tsing Lun Road and San Fuk Road.<br />
<br />
C. Traffic diversions<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following section of roads will be intermittently implemented with traffic diversions (except emergency vehicles and those authorised by police officers on-site):<br />
<br />
- The section of Tsing Chung Koon Road between Tsing Lun Road and San Fuk Road will be converted to one-way northbound traffic; and<br />
- The section of San Fuk Road between Leung Shun Street and Tsing Chung Koon Road will be converted to one-way westbound traffic.<br />
<br />
D. Provision of temporary passenger pick-up and drop-off points<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Temporary loading and unloading points will be provided at Tsing Chung Koon Road northbound opposite to Castle Peak Hospital, Tsing Chung Koon Road northbound opposite to Tuen Mun Hospital and Tsing Lun Road westbound outside Tuen Mun Hospital.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(II) Tsang Tsui<br />
&nbsp;<br />
A. Road closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The section of Nim Wan Road between Yung Long Road and Tsang Tsui Columbarium access road will be closed to all vehicular traffic from 7am to 7pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31 and November 1, except for the access of the following vehicles:<br />
<br />
- Franchised buses and MTR buses;<br />
- Taxis;<br />
- Vehicles gaining access to and from West New Territories Landfill and T&middot;PARK; and<br />
- Vehicles with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport.<br />
<br />
Kwai Tsing<br />
---------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following traffic arrangements will be implemented from 7am to 5pm daily on October 11, 18 and 19:<br />
<br />
A. Traffic arrangements<br />
<br />
- The section of Wing Tak Street southbound between the entrance of City Point and Wing Shun Street, Wing Yin Street and the section of Wing Shun Street between Wing Tak Street and Kwai Hei Street will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for hearses, franchised buses, taxi, green minibuses and emergency vehicles);<br />
- The section of Wing Kei Road between Kwai Hei Street and Wing Kin Road will be converted into one-way southbound. Vehicles will not be allowed to enter Wing Kei Road from Kwai Hei Street;<br />
- Wing Hau Street will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles);<br />
- Temporary taxi stands will be provided at the following locations:<br />
(i)&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Wing Kin Road near its junction with Wing Kei Road;<br />
(ii) Kwai Hei Street near its junction with Wing Shun Street;<br />
(iii) Wing Shun Street near its junction with Wing Yin Street; and<br />
(iv) Wing Shun Street near its junction with Yi Lok Street.<br />
- Thirty metered parking spaces at Wing Shun Street (Nos. 20578AB, 20579AB, 20580AB, 20581AB, 20582AB, 20583AB, 20584AB, 20585AB, 20586AB, 20587AB, 20588AB, 20589AB, 20590AB, 20599AB and 20600AB) will be temporarily suspended during the time specified below:<br />
(i)&nbsp; &nbsp; From 7am to 5pm on October 11; and<br />
(ii) From 7am on October 18 to 5pm on October 19.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The above arrangement will be implemented on any other dates if traffic situation so requires.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, Wing Yin Street and Wing Hau Street will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for hearses, franchised buses and emergency vehicles) from 7am to 5pm on October 10, 17, 24, 25 and 31.<br />
<br />
Lantau Island<br />
------------------<br />
<br />
A. Special traffic arrangements<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A section of closed road of Tung Chung Road between Shek Mun Kap Road and South Lantau Road will be temporarily opened for all vehicles to access from 6am to 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18 and 19.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;All other roads or access roads in South Lantau remain closed 24 hours daily. All motor vehicles, except emergency vehicles and those with valid Lantau Closed Road Permits, are banned from entering the closed road.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Special transport arrangements<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On October 18, apart from the bus routes serving Tung Chung Road, NLB Route No. 34S (Tung Chung Tat Tung Road Bus Terminus &ndash; Tung Chung Cemetery Area No. 18 (Circular)) will be operated from 8.45am to 1.15pm at a frequency of 30 to 45 minutes and NLB Route No. 7S (Mui Wo Ferry Pier &ndash; Lai Chi Yuen Cemetery) will be operated from 9am to 2.45pm at a frequency of 30 minutes.<br />
<br />
Sha Tin<br />
-----------<br />
<br />
(I) Po Fook Memorial Hall and Fu Shan Crematorium in Tai Wai<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 7am to 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, the following special traffic arrangements will be implemented:<br />
<br />
A. Road closure<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The section of Lower Shing Mun Road between Yau On Street and Fu Shan Crematorium, Yau On Street and Yau Ting Street will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles, hearses, vehicles taking part in funeral processions, New Territories GMB Route No. 64K and vehicles of residents of Pak Tin Area 2 to 5 and Lakeview Garden).<br />
<br />
B. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;All parking spaces on Yau On Street and Lower Shing Mun Road will be suspended.<br />
<br />
(II) Po Fook Hill in Sha Tin<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 8am to 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and 1 November, the western section of Pai Tau Street from its junction with Sheung Wo Che Road will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles).<br />
<br />
(III) To Fung Shan<br />
<br />
A. Road closure<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 9am to 5pm daily on October 11, 17, 18, 19, 24 and 25, the northern section of To Fung Shan Road from its junction with Pak Lok Path will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles and vehicles serving residents and persons working in To Fung Shan).<br />
<br />
B. Traffic direction<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 9am to 5pm daily on October 11, 17, 18, 19, 24 and 25, the section of To Fung Shan Road between Chung Ling Road and Pak Lok Path will be converted into one-lane two-way traffic.<br />
<br />
(IV) Shek Mun Columbarium<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 7am to 7pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, On Hing Lane will be closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles, Sha Tin Transfer Station vehicles and vehicles of persons working in Shek Mun Columbarium).<br />
<br />
Tsuen Wan<br />
---------------<br />
<br />
A. Road closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The section of Lo Wai Road from Lo Wai Pai Lau to Yuen Yuen Institute will be designated as closed road to all vehicular traffic except franchised buses (scheduled service Route No. 32P), taxis, public light buses (scheduled service New Territories Route No. 81), emergency vehicles and vehicles with valid closed road permits from 8am to 6pm on October 11 and 18.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the above road closure, all metered parking spaces on Lo Wai Road near Yi Pei Chun Road, former Lo Wai Bus Terminus and Lo Wai Road outside Yuen Yuen Institute will be suspended from 8am to 6pm on October 11 and 18.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Wo Hop Shek<br />
-------------------<br />
A. Road closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 6am to 5pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31 and November 1, the following road sections will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles and those vehicles specified below):<br />
<br />
- Kiu Tau Road (except for hearses, coaches conveying people attending services at the crematorium/cemetery, vehicles of the undertakers and franchised buses);<br />
- Ming Yin Road (except for franchised buses); and<br />
- All access roads within Wo Hop Shek Cemetery.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 6am to 5pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, 7 and 8, the following road section will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles):<br />
<br />
- Wo Ka Lau Road.<br />
<br />
B. Temporary prohibited zones<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 6am to 5pm daily on November 7 and 8, the following road sections will be temporarily designated as prohibited zones for private cars, buses, light buses, goods vehicles, motor cycles and motor tricycles (except for emergency vehicles and those vehicles specified below):<br />
<br />
- Kiu Tau Road (except for hearses, coaches conveying people attending services at the crematorium/cemetery, vehicles of the undertakers and franchised buses);<br />
- Ming Yin Road (except for franchised buses); and<br />
- All access roads within Wo Hop Shek Cemetery.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;All private cars, buses, light buses, goods vehicles, motor cycles and motor tricycles, except those vehicles specified above or those with permits issued by the Commissioner for Transport, will be prohibited from entering into the prohibited zones.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Members of the public may drive to Wo Hop Shek Cemetery after reopening of the roads/the end time of temporary prohibited zones until closure of the cemetery (i.e. 7.30pm) on the above-mentioned days.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
C. Banning of turning movements<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;All vehicles will be temporarily prohibited from making the following turning movements on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31 and November 1:<br />
<br />
- Right turning movements from Pak Wo Road northbound near Fung Ying Seen Koon onto Pak Wo Road southbound; and<br />
- Right turning movements from Pak Wo Road southbound near MTR Fanling Station into the car park on Pak Wo Road near Fung Ying Seen Koon.<br />
<br />
D. Suspension of car parks and parking spaces<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following car parks and parking spaces will be suspended (except emergency vehicles and those vehicles specified below) from 7am to 5pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, 7 and 8:<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />
- Car park at Wo Hing Road near Wah Ming Road roundabout (urban and NT taxis may access to the car park to pick up and set down passengers);<br />
- Car park at Pak Wo Road near Fung Ying Seen Koon (except for vehicles with valid disabled person&rsquo;s parking permits or parking certificates for drivers who carry people with mobility disabilities issued by the Transport Department to use the disabled parking space and the public buses operating the Residents&rsquo; Service Route No. NR112 (Dawning View, Fanling - Fung Ying Seen Koon) to pick up and set down passengers);<br />
- Parking spaces at Wah Ming Lane; and<br />
- All parking spaces at Ming Yin Road between Wo Hop Shek Cemetery Office and Kiu Tau Road.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Depending on the actual situation, the Police may exercise discretion to allow vehicles carrying the elderly and passengers with disabilities to pick up and set down at the car park at Pak Wo Road near Fung Yin Seen Koon to facilitate interchange with the bus services to Wo Hop Shek Cemetery.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Sandy Ridge<br />
------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
A. Opening of Frontier Closed Area from Lo Wu Station to Lo Wu Station Road<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Frontier Closed Area from Lo Wu Station to Lo Wu Station Road will be temporarily opened to the members of public for grave sweeping from 6am to 6pm daily between October 10 and November 8 (both dates inclusive).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
B. Road closure<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(i) From 6am to 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, 7 and 8, Lo Wu Station Road from its junction with Man Kam To Road to a point about 280 metres west of the same junction (near the management office of the Sandy Ridge Cemetery) will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles, vehicles of Lo Wu Village residents and vehicles that are allowed to park inside the carpark of MTR Lo Wu Station).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(ii) From 6am to 6pm daily on October 10, 11, 17, 18, 19, 24, 25 and 31, and November 1, 7 and 8, Sha Ling Road will be temporarily closed to all vehicular traffic (except for emergency vehicles and vehicles of Sha Ling residents).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Any vehicles found illegally parked within the precincts mentioned above will be towed away without prior warning, and may be subject to ticketing.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Police will implement appropriate arrangements subject to the prevailing traffic and crowd conditions. Grave sweepers are advised to use public transport services as far as possible. Motorists should exercise patience and tolerance, and follow the instructions of police officers on site.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inspection of aquatic products imported from Japan]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900484.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900484.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to the Japanese Government&#39;s plan to discharge nuclear-contaminated water at the Fukushima Nuclear Power Station, the Director of Food and Environmental Hygiene issued a Food Safety Order which prohibits all aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds originating from the 10 metropolis/prefectures, namely Tokyo, Fukushima, Ibaraki, Miyagi, Chiba, Gunma, Tochigi, Niigata, Nagano and Saitama, from being imported into and supplied in Hong Kong.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For other Japanese aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds that are not prohibited from being imported into Hong Kong, the Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department is conducting comprehensive radiological tests to verify that the radiation levels of these products do not exceed the guideline levels before they are allowed to be supplied in the market.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From noon on October 8 to noon today (October 9), the CFS conducted tests on the radiological levels of 272 food samples imported from Japan, which were of the &quot;aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt&quot; category. No sample was found to have exceeded the safety limit. Details can be found on the CFS&#39;s thematic website titled &quot;Control Measures on Foods Imported from Japan&quot; (<a href="https://www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html" target="_blank">www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) has also tested 52 samples of local catch for radiological levels. All the samples passed the tests. Details can be found on the AFCD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html" target="_blank">www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Observatory (HKO) has also enhanced the environmental monitoring of the local waters. No anomaly has been detected so far. For details, please refer to the HKO&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html" target="_blank">www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From August 24, 2023, to noon today, the CFS and the AFCD have conducted tests on the radiological levels of 266 449 samples of food imported from Japan (including 165 871 samples of aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt) and 56 760 samples of local catch respectively. All the samples passed the tests.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by SITI at AWS Future Builders: The AI Career Dialogue (English only) (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900455.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900455.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the speech by the Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Professor Sun Dong, at the AWS Future Builders: The AI Career Dialogue today (October 9):<br />
<br />
Dr Vogels (Vice President and Chief Technology Officer of Amazon.com, Dr Werner Vogels), Chris (Managing Director of AWS Hong Kong, Mr Chris So), Terry (Chief Executive Officer of the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks, Mr Terry Wong), distinguished guests, and young talents,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Good afternoon. It gives me great pleasure to join you all here at Hong Kong Science Park for Future Builders: The AI Career Dialogue. Today&#39;s event brings together university students, start-ups and technology leaders to exchange ideas, explore opportunities and consider how we can equip the next generation to thrive in an AI-enabled world.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Artificial Intelligence is no longer a technology of the distant future. It is transforming how businesses operate and how industries innovate. The Hong Kong SAR (Special Administrative Region) Government is seizing this opportunity to cement Hong Kong&#39;s status as a world-class innovation and technology (I&amp;T) hub. We are promoting AI as a core industry for Hong Kong&#39;s future development and empowering different sectors of the economy through AI technology. A key to this vision is our &quot;AI for All&quot; strategy championed in the 2026 Policy Address. AI should not be an exclusive club for tech giants or isolated research labs, but an open floor for the general public, university students, young researchers and professionals for their daily life and professional empowerment. It should also support our workforce and SMEs (small and medium-sized enterprises) in applying AI to meet practical needs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In this process, partnerships amongst the Government, industry, academia and investment sectors are important. This is why we are thrilled to see global tech champions like Amazon Web Services (AWS) collaborating with the Hong Kong Science Park, our ecosystem catalyst. Through Hong Kong Science Park&#39;s &quot;AI for All&quot; Inclusive Programme, we are giving our talents the practical runway to transform bold AI concepts into real-world solutions that benefit our entire community.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; To the university students, young innovators, and aspiring entrepreneurs here today, I would like to leave you with one thought. As generative AI flattens the technical learning curve, your true competitive edge lies in your creativity, critical thinking, problem-solving grit, and your ambition to solve human challenges. The future does not belong to AI, but to the builders who co-pilot with AI to amplify human ingenuity and create value for others.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On our part, the HKSAR Government is building a vibrant, sustainable I&amp;T ecosystem - from infrastructure and research capabilities to talent development and commercialisation. Meanwhile, we are also promoting the responsible use and orderly application of AI across industries, so that innovation can progress in a way that commands the confidence of businesses and the wider community. Hong Kong has a strong research foundation, international connectivity, and a deep pool of talents we need to seize the opportunities ahead. Most importantly, we have young people like you today who are ready to learn and experiment.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Before I close, I want to extend my gratitude to AWS for organising today&#39;s event and to Hong Kong Science Park for nurturing our innovation and technology ecosystem. In particular, my greetings also go to Dr Werner Vogels - for your coming to Hong Kong to ignite the minds of our future builders. I wish today&#39;s dialogue every success, and all of you an inspiring and rewarding afternoon.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Thank you very much.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900455_photo_1340822.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100900455_photo_1340822.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs detects case involving precious metals and stones dealer carrying out specified cash transactions without Category B registration]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900448.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900448.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:16:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Customs yesterday (October 8) detected a case involving a local company that conducted cash transactions of watches containing precious metals and stones valued at over HK$120,000, while not being a Category B registrant under the Dealers in Precious Metals and Stones Regulatory Regime. An investigation is ongoing.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing Ordinance (Cap. 615), unless exempted, any person who is seeking to carry on a business of dealing in precious metals and stones and engage in any transaction(s) (whether making or receiving a payment) with a total value at or above HK$120,000 in Hong Kong is required to register with Hong Kong Customs.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In particular, no person other than a Category B registrant may carry out a cash transaction with a total value at or above HK$120,000 in the course of business of dealing in precious metals and stones. Any dealer who is not a Category B registrant, who claims to be a Category B registrant, claims to be authorised to carry out, or carries out any cash transaction(s) with a total value at or above HK$120,000, commits an offence and is liable on conviction to a maximum fine of HK$100,000 and imprisonment for six months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs reminds dealers in precious metals and stones that they must obtain the relevant registration before they can carry out any cash or non-cash transaction(s) with a total value at or above HK$120,000.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For the forms, procedures and guidelines to submit applications for registration, please visit the website for Dealers in Precious Metals and Stones Registration System (<a href="https://www.drs.customs.gov.hk/index?request_locale=en" target="_blank">www.drs.customs.gov.hk</a>) or Customs&#39; webpage (<a href="https://www.customs.gov.hk/en/service-enforcement-information/anti-money-laundering/supervision-of-dealers-in-precious-metals-and-ston/index.html" target="_blank">www.customs.gov.hk/en/service-enforcement-information/anti-money-laundering/supervision-of-dealers-in-precious-metals-and-ston/index.html</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected transactions involving precious metals and stones with a total value at or above HK$120,000 conducted without the required registration to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Mandatory insurance coverage on motor and employees' compensation extended to Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900298.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900298.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In connection with the official opening of the Huanggang Port on October 12, the Government has entered into a market agreement with 45 insurers to extend the scope of coverage of their existing mandatory motor and employees&#39; compensation insurance policies to the Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area (HKPA). The agreement covers insurance policies required under section 4 of the Motor Vehicles Insurance (Third Party Risks) Ordinance (Cap. 272) and section 40 of the Employees&#39; Compensation Ordinance (Cap. 282) respectively. The effective date is July 31, 2026, i.e. the day the Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area Ordinance (Cap. 659) came into operation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The market agreement was signed between the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau (FSTB) and individual insurers to expand the geographical limit under the existing mandatory motor and employees&#39; compensation insurance policies to cover the HKPA without incurring any extra cost to policyholders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesperson for the FSTB said that this proactive collaboration of the FSTB with the Insurance Authority (IA) and the insurance industry eliminates potential public confusion by reaching a market agreement prior to the opening of the HKPA. This pragmatic arrangement is beneficial to the public in ensuring policyholders&#39; continuous compliance with the mandatory insurance requirements while minimising the administrative burden on stakeholders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area Ordinance came into operation on July 31, 2026. Insurance policies, being private contracts, however are not automatically extended to cover the HKPA upon the commencement of the Ordinance. The Government has thus taken the initiative to negotiate, via the IA, with the insurance industry to extend the coverage of the mandatory insurance policies of motor and employees&#39; compensation to include the HKPA at no additional premium to be paid by policyholders. This arrangement is similar to that made in 2007 for the Shenzhen Bay Port Hong Kong Port Area.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FSTB expressed its sincere gratitude to the IA, the Hong Kong Federation of Insurers and the insurance industry for their strong support and collaboration throughout the process.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The public may refer to the website of the FSTB (<a href="https://www.fstb.gov.hk/fsb/en/business/other_matters/index.html" target="_blank">www.fstb.gov.hk/fsb/en/business/other_matters/index.html</a>) for the details of the aforementioned market agreement and the list of insurers which have signed the agreement.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Company responsible officer fined $51,000 for contravening Employment Ordinance ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900356.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900356.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:50:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The responsible officer of Hong Kong Blockchain Development Company Limited was prosecuted by the Labour Department (LD) for violating the requirements under the Employment Ordinance (EO). The responsible officer pleaded guilty at the Eastern Magistrates&#39; Courts today (October 9) and was fined $51,000. The responsible officer was also ordered to pay an outstanding sum of about $2,147,000 to the employees concerned.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The company wilfully and without reasonable excuse contravened the requirements of the EO, failing to pay three employees&#39; wages and payment in lieu of notice within seven days after the expiry of the wage periods and termination of employment contracts, totalling about $1,927,000. The company also failed to pay the awarded sum of about $2,147,000 to the three employees within 14 days after the date set by the Labour Tribunal (LT). The responsible officer was prosecuted and convicted for his consent, connivance or neglect in the above offences.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The ruling will disseminate a strong message to all employers, directors and responsible officers of companies that they have to pay wages, termination payments and sums awarded by the LT or the Minor Employment Claims Adjudication Board to employees within the statutory time limit stipulated in the EO,&quot; a spokesman for the LD said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The LD will not tolerate these offences and will spare no effort in enforcing the law and safeguarding employees&#39; statutory rights,&quot; the spokesman added.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Appointments to Board of West Kowloon Cultural District Authority announced]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900230.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900230.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government today (October 9) announced the appointments made by the Chief Executive to the Board of the West Kowloon Cultural District Authority (WKCDA). The 12 non-official members are appointed for a two-year term from October&nbsp;23, 2026, to October&nbsp;22, 2028.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The list of appointees is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Mr Abraham Chan Lok-shung<br />
Mr Vincent Cheng Wing-shun<br />
Dr Roy Chung Chi-ping<br />
Mr Benjamin Hung Pi-cheng<br />
Mr Leo Kung Lin-cheng<br />
Mr Liu Che-ning<br />
Dr Pang Yiu-kai<br />
Mr Paulo Pong Kin-yee<br />
Ms Cleresa Wong Pie-yue*<br />
Mr Anthony Wu Ting-yuk<br />
Ms Catherine Yen Kai-shun<br />
Mr Cliff Zhang Kun*<br />
Secretary for Culture, Sports and Tourism or alternate representative<br />
Secretary for Development or alternate representative<br />
Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury or alternate representative<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(*new appointee)<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The non-official members appointed are experienced in their respective areas of expertise, including arts and culture, tourism, engineering, business and finance, law and community service. They will provide the necessary knowledge and valuable advice in helping the Board to discharge its statutory duties and oversee the development and operation of the West Kowloon Cultural District (WKCD).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;We would like to thank the outgoing members - Mr Andy Hei Kao-chiang and Ms Yolanda Ng Yuen-ting - for their efforts and contributions. We welcome the new appointees - Ms Cleresa Wong Pie-yue and Mr Cliff Zhang Kun, and look forward to continuing to work closely with the WKCDA Board,&quot; said a spokesman for the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The WKCDA Ordinance (Cap. 601) provides that the Board is the governing and executive body of the WKCDA. It is responsible for the planning, development, operation and maintenance of the arts and cultural facilities and related facilities in the WKCD.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKFA's "Close Encounters with Master Filmmakers: Movie Talks" explores humanistic cinematic works of director Jacob Cheung (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100800382.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100800382.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Film Archive (HKFA) will present the third edition of the programme series &quot;Close Encounters with Master Filmmakers: Movie Talks&quot; in November, screening six of Jacob Cheung&#39;s classic films selected by the director himself. Cheung will attend post-screening talks to share his creative journey alongside film critic Thomas Shin, cinematographer Wong Ping-hung, and screenwriter Polly Yeung. The screening programme is part of the celebration of the 25th anniversary of the HKFA.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the 1980s, Cheung made a strong directorial debut with &quot;Lai Shi, China&#39;s Last Eunuch&quot; (1988), a critical and commercial success, before following it up with &quot;Beyond the Sunset&quot; (1989), which won the Hong Kong Film Awards for Best Film and Best Screenplay. The selected films vary in subject matter and technique, but are all filled with Cheung&#39;s sense of humanistic care. Two of them illustrate how individuals caught in hardship navigate their circumstances, including &quot;Lai Shi, China&#39;s Last Eunuch&quot;, where the protagonist is evicted from the imperial palace shortly after his castration following the fall of the dynasty, and &quot;Always on My Mind&quot; (1993), an absurdist comedy about a news anchor with terminal cancer who colludes with television executives to sensationalise his illness in order to secure his family&#39;s financial future.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A number of the selected works display Cheung&#39;s interest in the female perspective. &quot;Beyond the Sunset&quot; explores the generational divide stemming from mindset and cultural differences through the rift between the protagonist and her daughter, who has been living abroad for many years. &quot;Intimates&quot; (1997) contrasts the life journeys of two generations of women - one who is still nursing a broken heart, and the past of her father&#39;s nanny. In one of Anita Mui&#39;s iconic cinematic performances &quot;Midnight Fly&quot; (2001), she starred as a woman in conflict yet marked by a strange mutual understanding with her husband&#39;s mistress.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Adapted from an online novel, &quot;Rest on Your Shoulder&quot; (2011) tells the story of a woman who transforms into a butterfly and finds the cure for a pandemic in the insect world, with a fresh spin added to humanism by infusing it with fantasy.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Tickets priced at $60 will be available from October 11 (Sunday) at URBTIX (<a href="https://www.urbtix.hk" target="_blank">www.urbtix.hk</a>). For telephone bookings, please call 3166 1288. For programme details, please visit the HKFA website at <a href="https://www.filmarchive.gov.hk/en/web/hkfa/2026/jacob-cheung/pe-event-2026-jacob-cheung.html" target="_blank">www.filmarchive.gov.hk/en/web/hkfa/2026/jacob-cheung/pe-event-2026-jacob-cheung.html</a> or call 2739 2139.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340766.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340766.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340767.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340767.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340768.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340768.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340769.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340769.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340771.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340771.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340770.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/09/P2026100800382_photo_1340770.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Research Grants Council to present public lecture on "Child Development, Learning, and Family Interventions"]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900291.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900291.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the University Grants Committee:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Pursuant to the Government&#39;s initiative to bolster STEAM (science, technology, engineering, arts and mathematics) education at the primary and secondary school levels, as well as to step up efforts in identifying and nurturing local STEAM talent, the Research Grants Council (RGC) will organise a series of public lectures from October to November. Various topics including cultural preservation with artificial intelligence, online pricing, children education, and universe exploration will be covered. Admission is free on a first-come, first-served basis. All are welcome.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The second lecture titled &quot;Child Development, Learning, and Family Interventions&quot; will be held on October 17 (Saturday). The lecture will be conducted in Cantonese. Details are as follows:<br />
<br />
Time: 3pm to 5pm<br />
Venue: Lecture Theatre, G/F, Hong Kong Central Library<br />
Live Broadcast: YouTube channel of the Hong Kong Public Libraries<br />
(<a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCJUipdvSACZjO1ldoO3YQBA" target="_blank">www.youtube.com/channel/UCJUipdvSACZjO1ldoO3YQBA</a>)<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Session 1: &quot;How to Effectively Engage and Promote Children&#39;s Academic Learning: Findings from Hong Kong Families&quot;<br />
------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Children in Hong Kong face significant academic pressure from a young age, such as early literacy instruction in kindergarten and participation in after-school tutoring in primary school, leading to significant economic and emotional stress for parents. The family, as one of the proximal micro-ecosystems for children&#39;s learning and development, plays a vital role in their academic learning, development, and mental well-being. This lecture, presented by Research Assistant Professor in the Department of Applied Social Sciences at the Hong Kong Polytechnic University Dr Catrina Liu draws on several studies conducted in Hong Kong to discuss the characteristics of local home learning environment and activities, as well as their relationships with children&#39;s Chinese, English, and mathematics abilities, providing empirical guidance on how parents can effectively participate in and enhance their children&#39;s learning process.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Session 2: &quot;All-round Autism Care: An Overarching Intervention Approach for Autistic Children and Their Parents&quot;<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This lecture, presented by Professor in the Department of Educational Psychology at the Chinese University of Hong Kong Professor Catherine <a>So</a>&nbsp;will introduce the research team&#39;s signature RABI&reg; intervention model, highlighting its contributions to innovation and technological application, as well as its social and educational impact on key stakeholders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The RGC has been regularly organising public lectures since 2009, featuring various distinguished scholars as speakers. These lectures aim to promote research knowledge from Hong Kong&#39;s tertiary institutions and raise public awareness of the significance and value of local research work.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For enquiries, please call 2524 3987 or visit the University Grants Committee webpage (<a href="https://www.ugc.edu.hk/eng/rgc/about/events/lectures/lectures.html" target="_blank">www.ugc.edu.hk/eng/rgc/about/events/lectures/lectures.html</a>). Additionally, members of the public can register for the RGC&#39;s email subscription service at&nbsp;<a href="https://www.ugc.edu.hk/eng/rgc/about/subscribe" target="_blank">www.ugc.edu.hk/eng/rgc/about/subscribe</a>&nbsp;to receive regular updates.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Pro-Chancellor of Hong Kong Metropolitan University appointed]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900231.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900231.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:03:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government announced today (October 9) that the Chief Executive, in his capacity as the Chancellor of the Hong Kong Metropolitan University (HKMU), has accepted the recommendation of the HKMU Council and appointed Mr Michael Wong Yick-kam as the Pro-Chancellor of the HKMU with effect from November 1, 2026, for a term of three years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Secretary for Education, Dr Choi Yuk-lin, welcomed the appointment. &quot;Mr Wong is a well-respected community leader and has been devoted to public services. He was first appointed to the HKMU Council as a member in 2008 and subsequently served as the Treasurer of the Council from 2009 to 2014, the Deputy Chairman from 2014 to 2016, and the Chairman from 2016 to 2022. He has demonstrated exceptional leadership in advancing the university&#39;s development into a full-fledged comprehensive university in Hong Kong. I am confident that with Mr Wong&#39;s wealth of experience, he will lead the HKMU Court to continue providing insightful advice for the HKMU&#39;s long-term development,&quot; she said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Dr Choi also paid tribute to Dr Eddy Fong, the outgoing Pro-Chancellor of the HKMU. &quot;Dr Fong has served the HKMU for more than two decades. First appointed to the HKMU Council as member in 2003, he went on to serve as the Council Chairman from 2009 to 2016. In 2023, he was appointed as the Pro-Chancellor, serving in that position to date. The HKMU&#39;s significant achievements over the years owe much to Dr Fong&#39;s contributions,&quot; she said.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; According to the Hong Kong Metropolitan University Ordinance, the Chancellor may, on the recommendation of the Council, appoint a Pro-Chancellor. On the authority of the Chancellor, the Pro-Chancellor may exercise the powers and perform the duties of the Chancellor, including conferring degrees and other academic awards in the name of the university.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[United Nations Sanctions (South Sudan) Regulation 2019 (Amendment) Regulation 2026 gazetted]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900287.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900287.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Government today (October 9) gazetted the United Nations Sanctions (South Sudan) Regulation 2019 (Amendment) Regulation 2026 (Amendment Regulation), which came into operation today.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The Amendment Regulation amends the United Nations Sanctions (South Sudan) Regulation 2019 to give effect to certain decisions relating to sanctions in the United Nations Security Council (UNSC) Resolution 2821 in respect of South Sudan,&quot; a Government spokesman said.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The amendments renew the arms embargo, travel ban and financial sanctions.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government has long been implementing fully the sanctions imposed by the UNSC. The Amendment Regulation aims to give effect to the instructions by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs for fulfilling the international obligations of the People&#39;s Republic of China as a Member State of the United Nations.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Notice on the Official Opening and Operation of the Huanggang Port]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900281.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900281.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is a notice jointly issued by the Security Bureau of the Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region and the Office of Port of Entry and Exit of the Shenzhen Municipal People&#39;s Government today (October 9):<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Pursuant to the &quot;Official Reply of the State Council concerning the Agreement on the Commissioning of the Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area and Related Extended Areas&quot;, the Huanggang Port Hong Kong Port Area and Related Extended Areas were commissioned beginning at 00:00 hours of July 31, 2026, and are subject to the jurisdiction of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (&ldquo;HKSAR&rdquo;) in accordance with the laws of the HKSAR. The official opening date is to be determined by the governments of Guangdong and Hong Kong.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Acceptance inspection of the new port building of the Huanggang Port has been completed. Following the completion of a series of system tests, trials and emergency drills by both Hong Kong and Shenzhen, it has been confirmed that the port is ready for opening. As agreed by the governments of Guangdong and Hong Kong, the Huanggang Port will officially open and commence operation at 6:30am of October 12, 2026, providing 24-hour clearance services for passengers and passenger vehicles. At the same time, passenger clearance services between the temporary port building of the Huanggang Port in Shenzhen and the Lok Ma Chau Control Point in Hong Kong will be suspended.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Government appoints members to Hospital Authority]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900278.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900278.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government announced today (October 9) the reappointment of one serving member and the appointment of one new member to the Hospital Authority (HA).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The serving member reappointed, Professor David Shum Ho-keung, continues to serve as a HA member in his capacity of the Dean of the Faculty of Health and Social Sciences of the Hong Kong Polytechnic University for a period of two years from November 1, 2026, to October 31, 2028.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The new member, Revd Canon Peter Douglas Koon Ho-ming, will be appointed for a period of two years from October 12, 2026, to October 11, 2028.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Revd Canon Koon is an incumbent member of the Legislative Council representing the Election Committee Constituency and the Chairperson of the Hong Kong Council of Social Service. He is also a former member of the Hospital Governing Committee of Alice Ho Miu Ling Nethersole Hospital.<a target="_blank"><br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</a><br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Secretary for Health, Professor Lo Chung-mau, welcomed the new member to the HA and also expressed gratitude to the serving member reappointed.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HA comprises a Chairman, 22 non-official members, three public officers and one principal officer from the HA. The membership list to be effective from November 1, 2026, is as follows:<br />
<br />
Chairman<br />
------------<br />
Mr Henry Fan Hung-ling<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Non-official Members<br />
---------------------------<br />
Dr Rex Auyeung Pak-kuen<br />
Dr Henry Chan Hin-lee<br />
Professor Anthony Chan Tak-cheung<br />
Ms Priscilla Chau Pui-ying<br />
Professor Philip Chiu Wai-yan<br />
Ms Winnie Chiu Wing-kwan<br />
Ms Anita Fung Yuen-mei<br />
Ms Tennessy Hui Mei-sheung<br />
Mr Jat Sew-tong<br />
Mr Ko Yiu-cheung<br />
Revd Canon Peter Douglas Koon Ho-ming<br />
Mr Matthew Kwok Pui-ho<br />
Mr John Kwong Ka-sing<br />
Mr Victor Lam Wai-kiu<br />
Mrs Sylvia Lam Yu Ka-wai<br />
Professor Lau Chak-sing<br />
Professor David Shum Ho-keung<br />
Mr Henry Tong Sau-chai<br />
Mr Anthony Tsang Hin-fun<br />
Dr Thomas Tsang Ho-fai<br />
Mr Wan Man-yee<br />
Professor Janet Wong Yuen-ha<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Public Officers<br />
-------------------<br />
Permanent Secretary for Health, Mr Thomas Chan<br />
Director of Health, Dr Ronald Lam<br />
Deputy Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury (Treasury) Miss Shirley Lau<br />
&nbsp;<br />
HA Principal Officer<br />
--------------------------<br />
Chief Executive of the HA, Dr Libby Lee]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[SmartPLAY system to temporarily suspend service for system maintenance]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900218.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900218.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 9) that the SmartPLAY system will be temporarily suspended for system maintenance during the following periods:&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) from 11pm on October 13 to 6am the following day (October 14); and<br />
(2) from 11pm on October 15 to 6am the following day (October 16).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Users who plan to use the system are advised to use it before or after these periods.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[EMSD announces test results of LPG quality in September 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900210.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900210.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Electrical and Mechanical Services Department (EMSD) today (October 9) announced that the department collected nine liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) samples from auto-LPG filling stations and LPG terminals on a random basis in September 2026 for laboratory tests. The results show that the LPG quality of all these samples complied with auto-LPG specifications.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The detailed test results are available on the EMSD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/en/gas_safety/lpg_vehicle_scheme/publications/general/results_of_lpg_sample_analysis/index.html" target="_blank">www.emsd.gov.hk/en/gas_safety/lpg_vehicle_scheme/publications/general/results_of_lpg_sample_analysis/index.html</a>). Enquiries can also be made to the EMSD&#39;s hotline on 2333 3762.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, the EMSD has been vetting independent third-party test reports submitted by LPG supply companies for each shipment to ensure that the quality of imported LPG complies with the specified requirements.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Rates and Government rent due October 30]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100700531.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100700531.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Demands for rates and/or Government rent for the quarter from October to December 2026 have been issued, and payment should be made by October 30, 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Payment can be made:<br />
<br />
(1) by using autopay, the Faster Payment System (FPS), PPS, Internet banking or bank automated teller machines (ATMs);<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) by uploading an e-Cheque/e-Cashier Order via the <a href="https://www.payecheque.gov.hk/" target="_blank">Pay e-Cheque portal</a>;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) by sending a crossed cheque to the Treasury, PO Box No. 28000, Sham Shui Po Post Office, Hong Kong (mail with insufficient postage will be rejected); or<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(4) in person at any post office or designated convenience store in Hong Kong (i.e. 7-Eleven, Circle K or U select). For the addresses and opening hours of post offices, please call the Hongkong Post enquiry hotline on 2921 2222 or visit its <a href="https://www.hongkongpost.hk/en/home/index.html" target="_blank">website</a>.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If payers have not received the demands, they may obtain replacement demands or enquire as to the amount payable by (i) visiting the <a href="https://www.rvd.gov.hk/en/electronic_services/account_enquires.html" target="_blank">Rating and Valuation Department (RVD)&#39;s website</a>; (ii) calling 2152 0111; (iii) faxing 2152 0113; or (iv) visiting the RVD, 15/F, Cheung Sha Wan Government Offices, 303 Cheung Sha Wan Road, Kowloon.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;There will be no rates concession for this quarter. The total amount due is shown on the demand. Non-receipt or late receipt of the demand does not alter the requirement that the total amount due must be paid by October 30, 2026. A surcharge of 5 per cent will be imposed for late payment. A further surcharge of 10 per cent will be levied on the amount (including the 5 per cent surcharge) which remains unpaid six months after the last day for payment.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For payment by autopay, the rates and/or Government rent will be debited from payers&#39; bank accounts on October 30, 2026. Payers should ensure that there are sufficient funds in their bank accounts to meet the payments on that date until settlement.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To support environmental protection, payers are encouraged to utilise the RVD&#39;s free <a href="https://ebill1.rvd.gov.hk/app/ebill/?locale=en" target="_blank">eRVD Bill service</a> to receive e-bills and make payments, and to settle bills by autopay or other means of e-payment (e.g. FPS, PPS, Internet banking, e-Cheque/e-Cashier Order or ATMs) to save queuing time. Application forms for autopay can be obtained by downloading from the <a href="https://www.rvd.gov.hk/doc/en/forms/rvd1008.pdf" target="_blank">RVD&#39;s website</a>, visiting the RVD, District Offices and banks or by calling 2152 0111.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at several beaches]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900225.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900225.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 10:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today&nbsp;(October 9) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Stanley Main Beach, Shek O Beach and Big Wave Bay Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island; and Trio Beach, Hap Mun Bay Beach, Silverstrand Beach and Clear Water Bay Second Beach in Sai Kung District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Government to let by tender three sites in Kowloon and New Territories to brownfield operators]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900172.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900172.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 09:51:43 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Lands Department (LandsD) announced today (October 9) that a total of three government sites in Kwun Tong, Tuen Mun and Yuen Long will be let by way of short-term tenancy through tender to eligible brownfield operators affected by the land resumption and clearance exercises before or within the fourth quarter of 2029 under relevant government development projects.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Brownfield operators, which were invited to participate in previous relevant tenders for a short-term tenancy two or more times after the completion of clearance exercises of relevant government development projects, will be excluded from this tender.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The tender commences today and will close on November 9. Details of the three sites and the list of relevant government development projects involved are described in a Government Notice gazetted today.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the prevailing arrangements, business operators affected by government development projects will be offered statutory compensation in accordance with the law, or ex-gratia allowances as an alternative, subject to the fulfilment of relevant eligibility criteria.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesperson for the LandsD said that in order to facilitate brownfield operators&#39; relocation of businesses or operations, and to optimise the utilisation of land resources, the department has been identifying suitable vacant sites on government land to let through tender by way of a short-term tenancy.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The three sites to be let through tender are suitable for a wide range of uses including construction industry-related open storage, godown and workshop. They are also suitable for other industrial use, open storage, godown, workshop, recovery and recycling or reprocessing business.&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesperson stressed that the LandsD will continue to identify vacant sites suitable for use by those brownfield operators required to be displaced, and these sites will be let through tender by way of a short-term tenancy.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Man charged by Police National Security Department]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900232.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900232.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 09:44:54 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The National Security Department (NSD) of the Hong Kong Police Force took enforcement action on July 9 in Eastern District and arrested a 52-year-old local man for an offence of &quot;knowingly publishing publications that had a seditious intention&quot;, in contravention of Section 24 of the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Investigation revealed that the arrested man was suspected of repeatedly publishing posts with seditious intentions on online social platforms, with content provoking hatred towards the Central Authorities, the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) Government and the Judiciary; and inciting others to attempt to procure the alteration of any matter established in accordance with the law in the HKSAR.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The NSD laid a charge against the man with one count of &quot;knowingly publishing publications that had a seditious intention&quot; in the morning of October 7. The case was mentioned at the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Court in the afternoon on the same day.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Police remind members of the public that &quot;knowingly publishing publications that had a seditious intention&quot; is a serious offence. Offenders shall be liable to imprisonment for seven years on first conviction. Members of the public are urged not to defy the law.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKSAR Government firmly rejects US Trafficking in Persons Report 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900068.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/09/P2026100900068.htm</link><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 03:11:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) strongly reprimands and resolutely refutes the baseless, slanderous remarks about Hong Kong in the Trafficking in Persons Report 2026 (Report) of the Department of State of the United States today (October 9).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the HKSAR Government said, &quot;We vehemently oppose and firmly reject the categorically false and groundless remarks in the so-called Report on Hong Kong&#39;s efforts to tackle trafficking in persons (TIP). The rating of Hong Kong at Tier 3 is entirely unfounded and represents a blatant attempt to tarnish Hong Kong&#39;s sterling reputation. TIP has never been a prevalent problem in Hong Kong, and there has never been any sign that Hong Kong is being actively used by syndicates as a destination or transit point for TIP.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman stressed, &quot;The HKSAR Government has all along been proactive in the fight against TIP, adopting a comprehensive multi-pronged strategy and devoting significant recurrent resources to combatting TIP and enhancing the protection for foreign domestic helpers (FDHs) in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;In 2025, the HKSAR Government continued to spare no effort in conducting initial screenings for all persons vulnerable to TIP risks, including illegal immigrants, sex workers, illegal workers, FDHs, imported workers and other suspected victims, who had come to the attention of the departments concerned. The HKSAR Government conducted around 12 100 initial screenings in 2025 (a 7% increase compared with the year prior) and identified 15 TIP victims, all of whom were Hong Kong residents falling prey to employment fraud in Southeast Asian countries. Our efforts in returning those residents to Hong Kong safely, identifying them as TIP victims, thoroughly investigating the cases and rigorously prosecuting individuals implicated exemplify our commitment to tackling the scourge of TIP. The Report&#39;s baseless attacks on Hong Kong&#39;s victim screening efforts are deeply hypocritical and reflect of serious double standards, especially when studies and reports within the United States have highlighted systematic underreporting of human trafficking cases by domestic law enforcement in the United States.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman further pointed out, &quot;That the Report has, year after year, continued to fabricate criticisms about our efforts to tackle TIP and the quality of our screenings exposes its true nature as a politically-motivated, despicable smear campaign designed to mislead the international community.&quot;<br />
<br />
Protecting Foreign Domestic Helpers<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKSAR Government recognises the significant contribution of FDHs to our society, and attaches great importance to protecting the some 380 000 FDHs in Hong Kong. The Employment Ordinance (Cap. 57) (EO) provides the same comprehensive protection for the employment rights and benefits of FDHs as local workers. In addition to legal safeguards, FDHs are entitled to additional benefits that are usually not available to local workers through a Government-stipulated Standard Employment Contract. These additional benefits include free accommodation, free food (or food allowance in lieu), free medical treatment and free passage to/from their home countries.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the EO, an employment agency (EA) is only allowed to charge job seekers, including FDHs, a commission of not more than 10 per cent of their first month&#39;s salary upon successful placement. Any EA contravening labour laws is liable to prosecution and licence revocation upon conviction. The Labour Department (LD) has also promulgated a Code of Practice for Employment Agencies (CoP) to stipulate additional standards for EAs beyond legal requirements, including prohibiting EAs from involving in the financial affairs of FDHs. Non-compliance with the CoP may result in revocation or non-renewal of an EA&#39;s licence.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In 2025, the dedicated Foreign Domestic Helpers Division of the LD sustained its comprehensive support for FDHs through a one-stop portal available in 13 languages, a dedicated 24-hour hotline, a wide range of publications on their rights and benefits, as well as advice on employment matters and assistance in seeking help from relevant authorities. The LD also stepped up its collaboration with the Consulates General (CGs) of FDH source countries, non-governmental organisations and other stakeholders to strengthen outreach and publicity efforts, promote FDHs&#39; awareness of employment rights and financial literacy, and follow up on case referrals received from these partners to provide timely assistance and support. Meanwhile, the LD continued to join hands with the Police to advise FDHs against engaging in money laundering or other illicit activities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Immigration Department (ImmD)&#39;s FDH Special Investigation Section also continues to scrutinise FDH visa applications to proactively identify potential TIP victims and cases of exploited FDHs. Where appropriate, the ImmD will render assistance to those FDHs who have been abused or exploited. To facilitate an FDH established to be a victim of a criminal case to stay in Hong Kong to assist in investigation or act as a witness in legal proceedings, ImmD will waive the fee for his/her extension of stay. Also, the ImmD takes into account records of past abuse and exploitation in considering future applications for employing FDHs from employers. If there is indeed evidence of such abuse or exploitation, the concerned employer&#39;s future applications will normally not be approved.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Building on its earlier initiative of producing a briefing video available in English, Tagalog and Bahasa Indonesia to increase FDHs&#39; awareness, with a view to preventing them from falling victim to exploitation and to encouraging them to contact the authorities when in need, ImmD pursued new collaborations with the CGs of Indonesia and the Philippines in the past year, hosting themed seminars for newly-arrived FDHs in Hong Kong to enhance their understanding of the Immigration Ordinance (Cap. 115) and raise their awareness of issues relating to TIP.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In response to the Report&#39;s unsubstantiated criticism of the &quot;two-week rule&quot;, the spokesman said, &quot;The &#39;two-week rule&#39;, under which an FDH shall leave Hong Kong upon completion of employment contract or within two weeks from the date of early contract termination, whichever is the earlier, aims to allow sufficient time for FDHs to prepare for their departure. Also, the rule allows sufficient flexibility to cater for exceptional circumstances and does not preclude FDHs from working in Hong Kong again after returning to their places of origin. Exploitation of FDHs is never tolerated in Hong Kong. Any FDHs who have been abused or exploited could apply for change of their employers in Hong Kong without first returning to their places of origin. FDHs abused or exploited should not feel inhibited from lodging complaints against their employers.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The Report&#39;s unjustified critique of the &#39;two-week rule&#39; is the exemplification of an absurd double standard. While ignoring the rule&#39;s legitimate purpose as a means to maintain effective immigration control and calling for its abolition, the Report remained conspicuously silent on the United States&#39; visa regime, which likewise mandates the departure of migrant workers upon termination of employment under stipulated timelines.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman added, &quot;The &#39;live-in requirement&#39; underpins the long-established government policy of ensuring employment priority for the local workforce. As in many other jurisdictions, importation of workers in Hong Kong is allowed only when there is proven manpower shortage in specific trades that cannot be met by local workers. The legality of the &#39;live-in requirement&#39; was upheld by the Court of Appeal in a ruling handed down in 2020 in an appeal of a judicial review.&quot;<br />
<br />
Comprehensive Legal Framework, Robust Enforcement and Training<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In response to the Report&#39;s repeated insistence that Hong Kong should enact dedicated trafficking legislation, the spokesman said, &quot;The HKSAR Government&#39;s current legislative framework, which comprises over 50 legal provisions against various TIP conducts, provides a comprehensive package of safeguards comparable to dedicated TIP laws found in some jurisdictions. In fact, this legal framework has served Hong Kong well. The Court of Final Appeal has also affirmed in various rulings that the HKSAR Government had no positive obligation to enact and maintain a criminal offence specifically criminalising the relevant activities prohibited under Article 4 of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights (BOR 4), and that it would have a wide margin of discretion in the manner of compliance with its obligations under BOR 4. Every jurisdiction should have its latitude to determine the best legal framework to tackle the issue and there is simply no one-size-fits-all solution. The Report&#39;s arbitrary fixation on dedicated legislation as the universal gold standard blindly focuses on form rather than substance.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Law enforcement agencies (LEAs) in Hong Kong have all along remained vigilant in investigation and enforcement, and pursued prosecution with full rigour. In 2025, LEAs continued to investigate job scam cases where victims were lured to Southeast Asian countries and detained to engage in illegal work and prosecute those involved in court. Further, they secured successful convictions against individuals implicated in trafficking cases, with prison terms handed down by the courts. The Police also continued to commit significant efforts to combating vice activities including prostitution in 2025. A total of 108 anti-vice operations were mounted, an increase of 6% as compared with 2024. A total of 203 persons were arrested for the offence of keeping a vice establishment or prostitution-related crimes under the Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 200).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As regards the protection of FDHs, the Police arrested 37 FDH employers in relation to physical or sexual abuse in 2025. Separately, 49 FDH employers were prosecuted for aiding and abetting their FDHs in breaching the latter&#39;s conditions of stay, with 38 employers convicted. In parallel, the LD continued to take robust enforcement actions against EAs that contravened the EO, the Employment Agency Regulations (Cap. 57A) or the CoP. In 2025, the LD conducted over 1 700 inspections to EAs providing placement services for FDHs. In the same year, an EA providing placement services for FDHs was convicted for unlicensed operation, while another was denied licence renewal because a related person of the licensee had been convicted of an offence involving dishonesty within the preceding five years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Educating frontline officers of our LEAs and the LD on TIP, including detection, investigation, prosecution and victim assistance continued to be a top priority. In 2025, around 2 400 officers from various government bureaux and departments attended TIP-related training, marking an 18% increase over that in 2024. The HKSAR Government also continued to deliver sharing sessions on combatting TIP for frontline staff working at the airport to enhance their awareness of TIP.<br />
<br />
Safeguarding National Security<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Regarding the Report&#39;s unfounded remarks on the Hong Kong National Security Law and the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance, the spokesman added, &quot;Since Hong Kong&#39;s return to the motherland, human rights in the city have always been robustly guaranteed constitutionally by both the Constitution and the Basic Law. The Hong Kong National Security Law and the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance clearly stipulate that human rights shall be respected and protected in safeguarding national security. The rights and freedoms, including the freedoms of speech, of the press and of publication, and the freedoms of association, of assembly, of procession and of demonstration, enjoyed by Hong Kong people under the Basic Law and the provisions of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights as applicable to the HKSAR are protected in accordance with the law. Laws safeguarding national security only target a very small number of organisations and individuals that endanger national security. Law-abiding persons will not unwittingly violate the law.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKSAR Government has been steadfastly combating TIP in an all-out and highly proactive manner. A high-level steering committee, established in March 2018, provides policy steer on actions against TIP and protection of FDHs. An Action Plan to Tackle TIP and to Enhance Protection of FDHs in Hong Kong has been promulgated by the steering committee, with multi-faceted, comprehensive, strategic and targeted measures all firmly put in place by the end of 2019 to cover victim identification, investigation, enforcement, prosecution, victim protection and support, prevention, and partnership with different stakeholders.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[164 more nomination forms for Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800631.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800631.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 20:37:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Returning Officers for the various subsectors of the 2026 Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received a total of 160 nomination forms for candidates and four nomination forms from designated bodies today (October 8). This brings to 465 the total number of nomination forms for candidates and six the total number of nomination forms from designated bodies received since nominations opened yesterday (October 7). The nomination period will end on October 20.<br />
