&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Increase in visitor arrivals during Golden Week boosts business across industries]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800615.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800615.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 19:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The interdepartmental working group on festival arrangements, led by the Chief Secretary for Administration, Mr Chan Kwok-ki, today (October 8) announced that the seven-day National Day Golden Week of the Mainland (October 1 to 7) concluded smoothly yesterday (October 7). A total of around 1.6 million inbound visitors were recorded. Among them, Mainland visitors accounted for more than 1.41 million, which exceeds the number of Mainland visitors recorded at the eight-day National Day Golden Week of the Mainland last year. The arrangements for the reception of visitors proceeded in an orderly manner, with bustling crowds creating a lively atmosphere in the city that boosted business across various industries.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan said, &quot;This year&#39;s National Day Golden Week of the Mainland offered a variety of vibrant activities. The National Day fireworks display, the National Day Raceday and other events held on October 1 were well received by members of the public and visitors. At the same time, the Government and different sectors of the community launched a rich array of special offers and activities, covering public transport; culture, arts and leisure; as well as dining and consumption, to celebrate the 77th anniversary of the founding of the People&#39;s Republic of China with the public. These activities effectively attracted crowds and stimulated consumption, with retail sector indicating that consumer spending in various retail categories saw single-digit to double-digit growth year-on-year, while the catering sector also indicated that businesses in tourist areas increased by about 10 per cent. The Government will continue to work hand in hand with the industry to promote diversified tourism products and projects, creating an all-round and in-depth tourism experience for visitors and continuously injecting momentum into the local economy.&quot;<br />
<br />
Visitor flow, situation at control points, and traffic and public transport arrangements<br />
　　<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the seven-day National Day Golden Week of the Mainland, the daily average number of Mainland visitors was around 202 000. The arrival of Mainland visitors peaked on October 1, with around 277 000 visitors. During the Golden Week, the Lok Ma Chau Spur Line Control Point received the highest number of Mainland visitors, followed by the Express Rail Link West Kowloon Control Point.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To cope with the increased passenger flows during the Golden Week, relevant departments deployed additional manpower. The Immigration Department opened additional counters, temporary counters and vehicle clearance kiosks in advance at land boundary control points (BCPs) to facilitate passenger and vehicular flows. The Security Bureau activated the Emergency Monitoring and Support Centre during the holidays to closely monitor and co-ordinate public order across various districts, including various BCPs. The overall operation of the control points and traffic were smooth and orderly.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Emergency Transport Co-ordination Centre of the Transport Department (TD) operated round the clock at an escalated tier to holistically monitor traffic and public transport conditions across the city and at BCPs, major stations and various tourist hotspots. The TD steered local and cross-boundary public transport operators (PTOs) to step up their carrying capacity, including enhancing MTR train services to and from Lo Wu/Lok Ma Chau Stations, strengthening Express Rail Link train services to and from Futian, Shenzhenbei and Beijingxi Stations, enhancing cross-boundary coach services, increasing the frequency of bus services connecting land BCPs and increasing the capacity of inner-harbour ferry services for the convenience of residents and visitors. PTOs also deployed additional staff to maintain order and reserved sufficient vehicles and manpower to further enhance services when necessary. Overall traffic conditions were generally smooth during the Golden Week.<br />
<br />
Mega events and major tourist attractions<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The National Day Fireworks Display over Victoria Harbour on October 1 attracted over 378 000 spectators. The event concluded with effective crowd control arrangements and was well-received by locals and tourists. The National Day Raceday at Sha Tin Racecourse attracted nearly 30 000 attendees, including over 9 300 tourists. The Mid-Autumn Lantern Carnival 2026 and International Lantern Spectacular held at Victoria Park saw an enthusiastic response. The event period was extended twice, drawing a total of around 730 000 visitors.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Visitors to Hong Kong during the National Day Golden Week were spread across different tourist attractions in the city, including theme parks, the Peak, Ngong Ping, Avenue of Stars, etc. Overall visitor flow was high and crowd management work was smooth and effective.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At East Dam of the High Island Reservoir in Sai Kung, various departments took measures to control traffic, increase the frequency of minibuses, publish visitor flow information, and strengthen cleaning services as needed according to the plan formulated before the Golden Week. As regards the public transport service, upon the TD&#39;s advanced deployment and co-ordination, the operator of Green Minibus route No. 9A enhanced services plying the route between Pak Tam Chung and East Dam of High Island Reservoir in response to passenger demand and traffic conditions during the Golden Week. Overall services were generally smooth. Moreover, the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) piloted a reservation system for the Po Pin Chau section and deployed additional staff at the viewing platform, as well as using drones for patrols and public announcements. Visitors complied with on-site staff instructions, and the pedestrian flow was smooth and orderly.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As for the campsites at Ham Tin Wan, Sai Wan and Long Ke Wan, the AFCD and the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) conducted joint operations to keep the campsites and the Ham Tin Public Toilet clean, and deployed staff to conduct day-and-night patrols, with stationed personnel reminding campers of camping etiquette and regulations. The usage, hygiene and orderliness of the various campsites were generally good. The AFCD also strengthened publicity and education along Sections 1 and 2 of the MacLehose Trail and at designated campsites nearby.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At Sharp Island and the Shui Hau sandflat on Lantau Island, the AFCD and other departments strengthened patrols, using drones to assist in monitoring coastal areas. They also conducted public education activities jointly with WWF-Hong Kong and the Ocean Park Conservation Foundation respectively, to promote marine conservation messages to visitors. To strengthen coral conservation at Sharp Island, the AFCD deployed additional staff to remind visitors not to damage, disturb, feed or collect marine life. Snorkelling and kayaking guides were also deployed to patrol nearshore areas and remind snorkellers to enter and exit the water via coral-friendly routes to avoid accidentally stepping on corals.<br />
<br />
Inbound tour groups and hotel occupancy rates<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In terms of Mainland inbound tour groups, around 1 140 Mainland inbound tour groups brought over 41 000 visitors to Hong Kong during the seven-day National Day Golden Week, with around 60 per cent engaged in overnight itineraries. The general order was good, and the overall hotel occupancy rate reached 90 per cent.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan was pleased to note that the series of festive events and special offers in celebration of the National Day were well received. He expressed gratitude to relevant government departments, organisations, and industry partners for their collaborative efforts and close co-ordination in receiving visitors, providing citizens and visitors with a joyful National Day holiday.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Second-day auction results for Victoria Park Chinese New Year Fair stalls]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800584.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800584.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 19:28:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A total of 92 regular size dry goods stalls and 46 large size dry goods stalls were all successfully let on the second-day auction for stalls at the 2027 Victoria Park Chinese New Year Fair today (October 8).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Around 750 people attended the auction at the Assembly Hall, 2/F, Lai Chi Kok Government Offices, 19 Lai Wan Road, Lai Chi Kok, Kowloon, from 9am to about 6pm today, a spokesman for the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The average bid price for the regular size dry goods stalls was $23,175, with the successful bids ranging from $18,000 to $64,000. The highest bid, $64,000, was about 7.5 times the opening price of $8,540.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The average bid price for the large size dry goods stalls was $29,870, with the successful bids ranging from $22,000 to $50,000. The highest bid, $50,000, was about 3.9 times the opening price of $12,810.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auction for the remaining 78 regular size dry goods stalls at the 2027 Victoria Park Chinese New Year Fair will be held at 9am tomorrow (October 9) at the same venue.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman reminded the successful bidders to comply with all the stipulations and provisions as set out in the licence agreement. Otherwise, the department is entitled to terminate the agreement and the licensee shall immediately vacate the stall.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs detects two drug trafficking cases involving incoming passengers at airport (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800556.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800556.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 19:20:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Hong Kong Customs yesterday (October 7) and today (October 8) detected two drug trafficking cases involving incoming passengers at Hong Kong International Airport and seized about 2 kilograms of suspected cocaine and about 5.2kg of suspected cannabis buds, with a total estimated market value of about $2.55 million.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In the first case, a 24-year-old Mainland male passenger arrived in Hong Kong from Penang, Malaysia, yesterday. During customs clearance, Customs officers found about 2kg of suspected cocaine inside his carry-on baggage. He was subsequently arrested.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In the second case, a 55-year-old Mainland female passenger arrived in Hong Kong from Bangkok, Thailand, today. During customs clearance, Customs officers found about 5.2kg of suspected cannabis buds inside her carry-on baggage. She was subsequently arrested.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The arrested persons in the two cases have been charged with one count of trafficking in a dangerous drug respectively. The two cases will be&nbsp;brought up at the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Courts tomorrow (October 9).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue to step up enforcement against drug trafficking activities through intelligence analysis. The department also reminds members of the public to stay alert and not to participate in drug trafficking activities for monetary return. They must not accept hiring or delegation from another party to carry controlled items into and out of Hong Kong. They are also reminded not to carry unknown items for other people.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue to apply a risk assessment approach and focus on selecting passengers from high-risk regions for clearance to combat transnational drug trafficking activities.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the Dangerous Drugs Ordinance, trafficking in a dangerous drug is a serious offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $5 million and life imprisonment.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected drug trafficking activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account (<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800556_photo_1340775.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800556_photo_1340775.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800556_photo_1340776.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800556_photo_1340776.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Brussels ETO brings Hong Kong's cinematic memories back to life at Spain Moving Images Festival (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100600299.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100600299.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 19:12:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in Brussels (Brussels ETO) joins hands with the Film Development Fund and the Cultural and Creative Industries Development Agency (CCIDA) to support the screening of Hong Kong films at the ninth Spain Moving Images Festival (SMIFest), which is currently taking place in Madrid, Spain, from September 3 to October 18 (Madrid time).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;SMIFest features a special programme on Hong Kong&#39;s &quot;Neon Years&quot;, showcasing 10 classic Hong Kong films from 1980 to 1999. The selected films are &quot;A Better Tomorrow&quot;, &quot;Boat People&quot;, &quot;City on Fire&quot;, &quot;Comrades: Almost a Love Story&quot;, &quot;Fallen Angels&quot;, &quot;Made in Hong Kong&quot;, &quot;My Heart is That Eternal Rose&quot;, &quot;The Killer&quot;, &quot;Throw Down&quot; and &quot;Twin Dragons&quot;.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Brussels ETO hosted a networking reception on October 7 (Madrid time), which brought together members of the international film and cultural communities. Speaking at the reception, Assistant Representative of the Brussels ETO Mr Paul Leung praised the remarkable achievements of Hong Kong cinema in the 1980s and 1990s, as well as its lasting legacy and influence on the global film landscape.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Leung also highlighted the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government&#39;s continuous efforts to promote the film industry and the new initiatives to support its development just announced in the 2026 Policy Address by the Chief Executive, such as the &quot;Film + Tourism&quot; promotion. In addition, the Support Unit for Non-local Film Productions of CCIDA will provide one-stop services for overseas film crews filming in Hong Kong. He concluded by inviting the audience to stay tuned for more Hong Kong movies inspired by the city&#39;s unique culture in the coming years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;SMIFest is Madrid&#39;s premier festival dedicated to Asian audiovisual experimentation, art-house cinema and video art. It promotes emerging filmmakers and artists from China, Japan, South Korea, India and Southeast Asia, alongside retrospectives of pioneering and classic works.&nbsp;<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600299_photo_1340777.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600299_photo_1340777.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600299_photo_1340778.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600299_photo_1340778.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Cluster of Influenza A cases in Kowloon Hospital]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800536.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800536.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 19:10:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hospital Authority.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman for the Kowloon Hospital made the following announcement today (October 8):<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Eight patients (aged 47 to 90) in a male rehabilitation ward have presented with symptoms of fever or respiratory symptoms since October 2. Appropriate viral tests were arranged for the patients, and their test results were positive for Influenza A. One patient is currently hospitalised at Queen Elizabeth Hospital for other medical needs. All patients are in stable condition.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Infection control measures have already been stepped up according to established guidelines. All other patients in the ward concerned are under close surveillance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The cases have been reported to the Hospital Authority Head Office and the Centre for Health Protection for necessary follow-up.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKICL alerts public of fraudulent websites]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800440.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800440.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 18:33:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Interbank Clearing Limited (HKICL) has recently noted two fraudulent websites purported to be from HKICL:
<ul>
	<li>tk.hkfps.sbs</li>
	<li>tuikuan.fpshk.shop</li>
</ul>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;These fraudulent websites imitate as &quot;Buyer Online Protection&quot; to provide services including 1) refund to buyer, 2) unauthorised online transaction reporting, and 3) online transaction support, for online transaction over FPS payment. In addition, they also trick user into providing bank name, bank account number and bank account holder&#39;s name to top up and withdraw money from their platforms&#39; virtual wallet. Further, they direct user to a fraudster impersonating as customer service personnel.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HKICL advises that these fraudulent websites have no affiliation with HKICL, or any business of HKICL. HKICL will not directly provide FPS service to individual members of the public or contact individual members of the public proactively under usual circumstance. The genuine official website addresses of HKICL are <a href="https://www.hkicl.com.hk/" target="_blank">www.hkicl.com.hk</a> and <a href="https://fps.hkicl.com.hk/" target="_blank">fps.hkicl.com.hk</a>.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HKICL urges the public to beware of suspicious calls, websites or other communications, and to remain vigilant in protecting personal information. If members of the public receive any suspicious communication purportedly from HKICL, please call HKICL&#39;s general line at 2533 1111 to verify. Members of the public who suspect that they&nbsp;have become the victim of fraudulent acts should report promptly to the Police.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Scam alert related to banks]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800438.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800438.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 18:23:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) wishes to alert members of the public to the press release issued by the bank listed below relating to fraudulent websites, internet banking login screens, phishing emails or other scams, which has been reported to the HKMA. Hyperlink to the press release is available on the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/smart-consumers/beware-of-fraudsters/#fraudulent-bank-websites-phishing-emails-and-similar-scams" target="_blank">HKMA website</a>.<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:564px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Bank</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Type of scam</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Chong Hing Bank Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website and internet banking login screen</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKMA wishes to remind the public that banks will not send SMS or emails with embedded hyperlinks which direct them to the banks&#39; websites to carry out transactions. They will not ask customers for sensitive information, such as login passwords or one-time password, by phone, email or SMS (including via embedded hyperlinks).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Anyone who has provided his or her personal information, or who has conducted any financial transactions, through or in response to the scams concerned, should contact the relevant bank with the information provided in the corresponding press release, and report the matter to the Crime Wing Information Centre of the Hong Kong Police Force at 2860 5012.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[September's monthly gravidtrap index for Aedes albopictus mosquitoes continues to drop]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800460.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800460.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 17:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) today (October 8) announced that the monthly gravidtrap index (MGI) for Aedes albopictus mosquitoes in September was 5.2 per cent, lower than the 6.6 per cent in August. This reflects the continued efforts by the FEHD and relevant government departments to intensify mosquito prevention and elimination operations, resulting in the improvement of the overall mosquito infestation situation this month.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In September, among the 62 survey areas, the area gravidtrap index (AGI) in one area exceeded the alert level of 20 per cent. The decreases in the MGI and the AGI are attributable to the continuous and intensified mosquito prevention and elimination operations by various government departments and stakeholders, and may also have been affected by factors such as weather. Overall, the MGI for Aedes albopictus mosquitoes in September was 5.2 per cent, at Level 2 (indicating the distribution of Aedes albopictus mosquitoes in the survey areas was fairly extensive). Relevant departments and stakeholders will persistently intensify mosquito prevention and elimination operations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Moreover, the monthly density index for Aedes albopictus in September was 1.2, which represented that an average of 1.2 Aedes albopictus adults were found in the Aedes-positive gravidtraps, indicating that the number of adult Aedes albopictus was not abundant in the survey areas. The gravidtrap and density indexes for Aedes albopictus in different survey areas, as well as information on mosquito prevention and control measures, are available on the department&#39;s website at <a href="https://www.fehd.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.fehd.gov.hk</a>.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the FEHD said, &quot;The FEHD continues to monitor mosquito infestation and conduct risk assessments in all districts, promptly taking appropriate and targeted mosquito prevention and elimination measures. The FEHD, together with relevant departments and stakeholders, has implemented rapid response measures and targeted deployments, and has continued to intensify mosquito prevention and control work with relevant government departments in areas under their purview, including eliminating mosquito breeding places, applying larvicides, conducting fogging operations to eradicate adult mosquitoes, and placing mosquito trapping devices at suitable locations. The FEHD has also conducted site inspections with relevant departments, and provided them with professional advice and technical support to assist them in formulating and implementing effective anti-mosquito measures swiftly. At the same time, the FEHD has strengthened publicity and education.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FEHD continues to conduct a three-phase Anti-mosquito Campaign this year. The third phase of the Anti-mosquito Campaign was launched on August 3 and will run until October 23. During the period, the district offices of the FEHD will target residences, schools, construction sites, public housing estates, hospitals, illegal cultivation sites, waterfront public and private cargo working areas, land boundary control points, typhoon shelters and cross-boundary ferry terminals to remove accumulated water and carry out mosquito prevention and control work. To further enhance the effectiveness of mosquito control, the FEHD and relevant government departments have launched the first and second phases of the All-out Anti-mosquito Operations on March 25 and April 30 respectively. The departments will continue with the work of phase one, including eliminating potential mosquito breeding places and arranging for necessary structural repairs to minimise mosquito breeding places, and will consistently carry out the work of phase two by conducting regular ultra-low volume fogging operations at places with mosquito infestation to control the mosquitoes. The FEHD calls on property management entities to continue actively participating in the All-out Anti-mosquito Operations.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FEHD currently makes regular announcements on gravidtrap indexes for Aedes albopictus on its webpage. To allow more citizens to quickly grasp the mosquito infestation situation, the FEHD has strengthened information dissemination by promptly announcing the latest gravidtrap indexes through press releases and social media.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To reduce the risk of transmission of chikungunya fever (CF) and dengue fever (DF), the FEHD continues to step up mosquito prevention and control measures across all districts and conduct vector investigations and targeted mosquito control operations within a 250-metre radius of the residences of patients and the places that patients had visited during the infectious period. Actions include removing mosquito breeding grounds, applying larvicides to stagnant water that cannot be cleared, and carrying out ultra-low volume fogging operations in adult mosquito habitats such as densely wooded areas, dark and secluded places, and abandoned structures to eliminate adult mosquitoes.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FEHD appeals to members of the public to continue to stay alert and work together to carry out mosquito prevention and control measures early, including inspecting their homes and surroundings to remove potential breeding grounds, changing water in vases and scrubbing their inner surfaces, removing water in saucers under plant pots at least once a week, and properly disposing of containers such as empty cans and disposable meal boxes. The FEHD also advises members of the public and estate management bodies to keep drains free of blockage and level all defective ground surfaces to prevent the accumulation of water. They should also scrub all drains and surface sewers with an alkaline detergent at least once a week to remove any mosquito eggs.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Aedes albopictus is a kind of mosquito that can transmit DF and CF. DF and CF are commonly found in countries in tropical and subtropical regions, and the risk of importation persists. Members of the public should check the situation at their destinations before travelling abroad, especially when travelling to Southeast Asian countries. If travellers feel unwell after returning to Hong Kong, they should seek medical advice immediately and provide their travel history to the doctor.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[AFCD launches series of special operations to capture unattended dogs at high-risk locations]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800467.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800467.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 17:05:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) announced today (October 8) that a new phase of special operations will be launched. In the coming four months, priority will be given to high-risk locations where dog attacks on passers-by and congregations of stray dogs have occurred, to strengthen the capturing of unattended dogs. Depending on the situation, the AFCD will continue the operations to safeguard public safety.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the AFCD said, &quot;In a high-density urban environment, unowned and uncontrolled dogs may pose risks to public safety. Once dogs are allowed to roam unattended in public places, they may cause harm to members of the public or other animals. Dogs that have not been vaccinated against rabies also pose a threat to public health and animal health. To this end, the AFCD adopts a risk-based strategy and will prioritise conducting special operations to capture unattended dogs at high-risk locations. The AFCD will also work closely with the Police to conduct joint special enforcement operations when necessary, requiring dog keepers to fulfil their responsibilities properly to safeguard community safety.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman added that after capturing the dogs, the AFCD will check whether the dogs have been microchipped and hold a valid dog licence, and will contact the keepers based on the registration information and verify lost-and-found records. If there is sufficient evidence, the keepers will be prosecuted. If the keepers cannot be traced, staff and veterinary officers at the Animal Management Centres of the AFCD will patiently observe the dogs. Those that are confirmed to be in good health and of a gentle temperament will be transferred to the AFCD&#39;s partner animal welfare organisations for sterilisation and arrangement for adoption by the public. The spokesman emphasised that the AFCD will not release dogs without keepers back into the community to avoid posing public safety risks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the Rabies Ordinance (Cap. 421) and the Rabies Regulation (Cap. 421A), all dogs over five months old must be microchipped, vaccinated against rabies and licensed. Dogs must thereafter be re-vaccinated and have their licences renewed at intervals of not more than three years. In addition, dogs in a public place must be held on a leash or otherwise kept under effective control. Dogs must not be left unattended in public places, nor abandoned. Offenders are liable on conviction to a maximum fine of $10,000 for each offence, and a keeper who abandons a dog is liable to imprisonment for six months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public who wish to learn more about the proper control of dogs and the responsibilities of keepers may visit the AFCD website (<a href="https://www.pets.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.pets.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Interest rate of fourth interest payment for Silver Bond Series due 2027]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800423.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800423.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 16:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Monetary Authority, as representative of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government, announced today (October 8) the relevant per annum interest rate for the fourth interest payment of Silver Bond Series due 2027 (Issue Number: 03GB2710R) (the Bonds) issued under the Infrastructure Bond Programme.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the Issue Circular dated September 30, 2024, for the Bonds, the fourth interest payment of the Bonds is scheduled to be made on October 23, 2026, and the relevant interest rate is scheduled to be determined and announced on October 8, 2026, as the higher of the prevailing Floating Rate and Fixed Rate.&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On October 8, 2026, the Floating Rate and Fixed Rate are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
Floating Rate: +1.80 per cent (Annex)<br />
Fixed Rate: +4.00 per cent<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Based on the Floating Rate and Fixed Rate set out above, the relevant interest rate for the fourth interest payment is determined and announced as 4.00 per cent per annum.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs conducts interdepartmental anti-illicit cigarette publicity campaigns in North District (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700280.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700280.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:53:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Hong Kong Customs yesterday (October 7) conducted joint anti-illicit cigarette publicity campaigns with members of the North District Council, the Tobacco and Alcohol Control Office (TACO) of the Department of Health, the Housing Department (HD) and the Hong Kong Police Force at Choi Yuen Estate and Ching Ho Estate in Sheung Shui. Customs also publicised the Duty Stamp System.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Customs officers patrolled the housing estates and introduced to residents Customs&#39; enforcement actions against illicit cigarettes, and reminded them that the maximum penalty for offences related to duty-not-paid cigarettes has been increased to a $2 million fine and seven-years&#39; imprisonment. Customs also explained to estate security personnel how to deal with suspected illicit cigarette activities.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Customs officers also introduced to members of the District Council, residents, and newspaper and cigarette retailers the Duty Stamp System to be implemented in Hong Kong. The three-month Pilot Run for the Duty Stamp System launched by Customs concluded early this year. The department will continue to maintain close communication with all stakeholders and optimise the design and implementation details of the system. Customs expects the Duty Stamp System to achieve the ultimate goal of effective distinguishment of duty-paid cigarettes from duty-not-paid ones, and to combat &quot;cheap whites&quot;.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Customs will continue to strengthen publicity and education to raise public awareness of anti-illicit cigarettes. If public rental housing units are found to be involved in illicit cigarette crimes, Customs will notify the HD for follow-up action after the conclusion of court proceedings. Customs reminds members of the public not to buy or sell illicit cigarettes or distribute illicit cigarette leaflets to avoid creating a criminal record that could affect their future.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (Cap. 109), anyone involved in dealing with, possession of, selling or buying illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years. Members of the public are urged to report any suspected illicit cigarette activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080, its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In accordance with the Smoking (Public Health) Ordinance (Cap. 371), no person shall distribute any smoking product advertisement (including any promotional leaflet). Any person who contravenes the regulation is liable to a maximum fine of $50,000. Members of the public are also urged to report any suspected activities of illicit cigarette leaflet distribution to TACO&#39;s hotline 2961 8823.&nbsp;<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340772.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340772.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340773.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340773.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340774.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700280_photo_1340774.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inspection of aquatic products imported from Japan]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800417.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800417.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:50:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to the Japanese Government&#39;s plan to discharge nuclear-contaminated water at the Fukushima Nuclear Power Station, the Director of Food and Environmental Hygiene issued a Food Safety Order which prohibits all aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds originating from the 10 metropolis/prefectures, namely Tokyo, Fukushima, Ibaraki, Miyagi, Chiba, Gunma, Tochigi, Niigata, Nagano and Saitama, from being imported into and supplied in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For other Japanese aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds that are not prohibited from being imported into Hong Kong, the Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department is conducting comprehensive radiological tests to verify that the radiation levels of these products do not exceed the guideline levels before they are allowed to be supplied in the market.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From noon on October 7 to noon today (October 8), the CFS conducted tests on the radiological levels of 124 food samples imported from Japan, which were of the &quot;aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt&quot; category. No sample was found to have exceeded the safety limit. Details can be found on the CFS&#39;s thematic website titled &quot;Control Measures on Foods Imported from Japan&quot; (<a href="https://www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html" target="_blank">www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) has also tested 50 samples of local catch for radiological levels. All the samples passed the tests. Details can be found on the AFCD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html" target="_blank">www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Observatory (HKO) has also enhanced the environmental monitoring of the local waters. No anomaly has been detected so far. For details, please refer to the HKO&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html" target="_blank">www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From August 24, 2023, to noon today, the CFS and the AFCD have conducted tests on the radiological levels of 265 995 samples of food imported from Japan (including 165 599 samples of aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt) and 56 708 samples of local catch respectively. All the samples passed the tests.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Effective Exchange Rate Index]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800166.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800166.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The effective exchange rate index for the Hong Kong dollar on Thursday, October 8, 2026 is 100.5 (same as yesterday&#39;s index).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Correctional officers stop remand person in custody from attacking staff member]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800390.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800390.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Correctional officers at Siu Lam Psychiatric Centre stopped a female remand person in custody from attacking a staff member inside a hospital ward today (October 8).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At 10.20am today, a 50-year-old female remand person in custody suddenly became emotional and attacked a correctional officer inside a hospital ward. Officers at the scene immediately stopped the assailant and called for reinforcement.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During the incident, the officer being attacked sustained an injury to her face. She did not need to be sent to a public hospital after examination and treatment by the institution Medical Officer. The assailant did not sustain any injuries.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The case has been reported to the Police for investigation.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The assailant was remanded for the offence of robbery in 2026.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Police National Security Department arrests one man and one woman]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800391.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800391.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:22:33 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The National Security Department of the Hong Kong Police Force arrested a 38-year-old man and a 38-year-old woman in Kowloon on October 6. They were suspected of contravening the offence of &quot;doing with a seditious intention an act that had a seditious intention&rdquo; under Section 24 of the Safeguarding National Security Ordinance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The two arrested persons, including a male licensed insurance broker, were suspected of operating pages on various social media platforms and publishing seditious posts that incite hatred against the constitutional order of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region. The arrested man also profited by selling insurance products. Prior to the operation, Police obtained court search warrants to search the residences of the arrested persons and seize relevant exhibits. The two arrested persons will be released on bail for further investigation.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The acts concerned are suspected of contravening the offence of &quot;doing with a seditious intention an act or acts that had a seditious intention&quot;, which is a serious offence. On first conviction, offenders shall be liable to imprisonment for seven years.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Police will continue to ensure that laws are observed and strictly enforced to bring offenders to account, so as to prevent, suppress, and punish any acts or activities that endanger national security in accordance with the law.<br />
&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Four owners fined over $110,000 in total for not complying with removal orders]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800337.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800337.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Four owners were fined $116,160 in total yesterday (October 7) upon conviction at the Kowloon City Magistrates&#39; Courts for failing to comply with removal orders issued under the Buildings Ordinance (BO) (Cap. 123).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The first case involved an unauthorised structure with area of about 15 square metres on the flat roof of a composite building on Yan Oi Street, Wong Tai Sin. As the unauthorised building works (UBW) was carried out without prior approval and consent from the Buildings Department (BD), a removal order was served on two co-owners under section 24(1) of the BO. Failing to comply with the removal order, they were prosecuted by the BD, and were fined $53,250 in total yesterday, of which $38,160 was the fine for the number of days that the offence continued, upon conviction at the court on May 6.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The second case involved an unauthorised structure with area of about 32 sq m on the flat roof of a residential building on Fuk Wing Street, Sham Shui Po. As the UBW was carried out without prior approval and consent from the BD, a removal order was served on two co-owners under section 24(1) of the BO. Failing to comply with the removal order, they were prosecuted by the BD and were fined $62,910 in total, of which $47,820 was the fine for the number of days that the offence continued upon conviction at the court yesterday.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the BD said today (October 8), &quot;UBWs may lead to serious consequences. Owners must comply with removal orders without delay. The BD will continue to take stringent enforcement action against owners who fail to comply with removal orders, including instigation of prosecution, to ensure building and public safety.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Failure to comply with a removal order without reasonable excuse is a serious offence under the BO. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $200,000 and one year&#39;s imprisonment, and a further fine of up to $20,000 for each day that the offence continues.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tokyo ETO promotes Hong Kong's I&T development under Hong Kong's First Five-Year Plan in Korea (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100500589.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100500589.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in Tokyo (Tokyo ETO), in collaboration with Invest Hong Kong (InvestHK) and the Hong Kong Trade Development Council (HKTDC), held a networking luncheon for innovation and technology (I&amp;T) companies in Seoul, Korea, today (October 8) through the Economic and Trade Express&nbsp;platform&nbsp;to promote Hong Kong&#39;s strategic development in innovation technology and provide a platform for Korean companies to share their experiences of establishing and developing their businesses in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Speaking at the luncheon, the Principal Hong Kong Economic and Trade Representative (Tokyo), Ms Gillian Lam, said that given Hong Kong&#39;s unique position under the &quot;one country, two systems&quot; principle, the city offers an ideal platform for Korean companies to develop and scale their I&amp;T businesses, as well as to access the Chinese Mainland and global markets.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ms Lam said that advancing I&amp;T is one of Hong Kong&#39;s highest strategic priorities for future growth, as set out in the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) (Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan). In the I&amp;T sector, Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan sets out a clear strategic roadmap to accelerate Hong Kong&#39;s development into an international I&amp;T centre, including the development of three major I&amp;T parks and five key research and development institutions.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; She added that the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government is also committing substantial resources to advancing this ambition, including a HK$10 billion I&amp;T Industry-Oriented Fund, which will support the commercialisation and industrialisation of technological achievements. Together with contributions from the market, the fund is expected to leverage at least HK$40 billion.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Participants who joined the three-day mission to Hong Kong in late August and early September also shared their experiences of visiting Hong Kong&#39;s flagship I&amp;T hubs and strategic infrastructure during the luncheon. Tokyo ETO encouraged participants who have yet to visit Hong Kong to experience first-hand Hong Kong&#39;s development as an international I&amp;T and business hub.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tokyo ETO will continue to work closely with regional partners, including InvestHK and the HKTDC, to foster I&amp;T partnerships and deepen ties between Korea and Hong Kong.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340762.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340762.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340763.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340763.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340764.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340764.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340765.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100500589_photo_1340765.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Employer fined for violation of safety legislation ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800344.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800344.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:48:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Chun Yuen Engineer (HK) Company Limited was fined $60,000 at the Tuen Mun Magistrates&#39; Courts today (October 8) for violating the Occupational Safety and Health Ordinance. The prosecution was launched by the Labour Department.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The case involved a fatal accident that occurred on August 1, 2025, at a residential building in Tuen Mun. A worker, while draining dyed water on the roof alone for a seepage test of waterproofing works, got his left arm trapped in a drainage opening, causing him to drown as his face had been submerged in the water for some time. He was rushed to hospital in an unconscious state and passed away 13 days later.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Fatal traffic accident in Pat Heung]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800335.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800335.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:59:50 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Police are investigating a fatal traffic accident in Pat Heung yesterday (October 7) in which a man died.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At about 9.26pm yesterday, a motorcycle driven by a man was travelling along Route Twisk towards Tsuen Wan. When approaching the lamp post H2915, it was reportedly hit by the side mirror of a light goods vehicle travelling in the opposite traffic lane. The motorcyclist lost his balance and fell on the ground. The light goods vehicle did not stop and left the scene.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; Sustaining hand and leg injuries, the 35-year-old male motorcyclist was rushed to Pok Oi Hospital in conscious state. He was later certified dead at 0.04am today (October 8).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Investigations by the Special Investigation Team of Traffic, New Territories North are under way.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Anyone who witnessed the accident or has any information to offer is urged to contact the investigating officers on 3661 3800.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hongkong Post announces sale of philatelic products from various postal administrations (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800232.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800232.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hongkong Post announced today (October 8) that selected philatelic products issued by China Post, Macao Post and Telecommunications, and the postal administrations of Australia, the Isle of Man, Liechtenstein, New Zealand and the United Kingdom will be available for sale from October 15 (Thursday).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;China Post has released stamps on the theme of &quot;The Investitures of the Gods (II)&quot;. This second set focuses on pivotal chapters in the campaign against King Zhou of Shang, showcasing the grand epic scale and rich ensemble cast of the Investitures of the Gods universe through classic tales of duels among celestial beings, clashes between armies, and shifting allegiances. The entire stamp set is crafted with Chinese traditional gongbi painting and heavy colours, inheriting the precise and refined artistic tradition of Song dynasty paintings. It employs multilayered colour applications with comprehensive texturing and dyeing techniques, and features a rigorous composition and elegant colour scheme. The design preserves the aesthetics of classical painting while incorporating modern visual expression, recreating in miniature the fantastical splendour and heroic spirit of the Investitures of the Gods universe.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Macao Post and Telecommunications has released stamps on the theme of the &quot;Macao 2026 Specialized World Stamp Exhibition - A Journey through Day and Night II&quot;. This stamp issue focuses on six time periods of the Chinese timekeeping system, namely Wu, Wei, Shen, You, Xu, and Hai, capturing the enchanting scenery of Macao&#39;s islands - from the brilliance of high noon to the stillness of the night - in miniature artworks. The stamp palette transitions from dazzling daylight to the mellow tones of nightfall, as the narrative of light and shadow shifts from radiant intensity to serene calm.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, Isle of Man Post released stamps on the theme of &quot;Manx Heritage Transport: Buses &amp; Horse Trams&quot;. The presentation pack features all six Manx Heritage Transport stamps with detailed notes on the history of bus and horse tram services on the Isle of Man.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The selected philatelic products will be available for sale at the Hongkong Post online shopping platform ShopThruPost (<a href="https://shopthrupost.hongkongpost.hk/?lang=E" target="_blank">shopthrupost.hongkongpost.hk</a>) starting from 9am on October 15, while stocks last. These products will also be available at the PostShop of the General Post Office, Tsim Sha Tsui Post Office and Sha Tin Central Post Office on the same day. Details of each product, the prices and sales quotas are set out in the Appendix. For further information, members of the public may visit the Hongkong Post Stamps website (<a href="https://stamps.hongkongpost.hk/en/home/index.html" target="_blank">stamps.hongkongpost.hk</a>) or call the Hongkong Post Philatelic Bureau hotline at 2785 5711.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340749.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340749.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340750.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340750.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340751.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340751.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340753.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340753.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340754.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340754.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340755.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340755.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340756.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340756.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340757.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340757.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340758.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340758.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340759.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340759.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340760.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340760.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340761.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800232_photo_1340761.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Public rental housing unit tenant convicted and jailed for dealing with duty-not-paid cigarettes (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800224.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800224.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A tenant of a public rental housing (PRH)&nbsp;unit was sentenced to three months&#39; imprisonment by the Eastern Magistrates&#39; Courts yesterday (October 7) for dealing with duty-not-paid cigarettes, in contravention of the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (DCO) (Cap. 109).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During an anti-illicit cigarette operation in Fung Wah Estate, Chai Wan, on September 10, Customs officers intercepted a 56-year-old local man, and seized from his belongings and the PRH unit a total of 16 900 sticks of duty-not-paid cigarettes, with an estimated market value of about $76,000 and a duty potential of about $56,000. The male PRH unit tenant was also arrested immediately.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs welcomes the sentence. The custodial sentence has imposed a considerable deterrent effect and reflects the seriousness of the offences.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If a PRH unit is found to be involved in offences related to illicit cigarettes, Customs will notify the Housing Department after the court concludes the case so that follow-up action can be taken in accordance with the terms of the tenancy agreement or the Marking Scheme for Estate Management Enforcement in Public Housing Estates, which may include allotment of points or termination of tenancy.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue the multipronged enforcement strategy to vigorously combat illicit cigarette activities. Customs reminds members of the public that under the DCO, anyone involved in dealing with, possession of, selling or buying illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected illicit cigarette activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080, its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340746.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340746.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340747.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340747.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340748.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800224_photo_1340748.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCSD's "Hong Kong Artists" Series to present Dizi Recital by Candy Yau Kwan-lam in November (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700442.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700442.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Local young dizi performer Candy Yau will give a recital in November under the &quot;Hong Kong Artists&quot; Series presented by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department (LCSD). Showcasing the unique allure of three types of dizi&mdash;the bangdi, qudi and dadi&mdash;she will be joined by six long-standing musical collaborators. Through a rich combination of wind, plucked, bowed and percussion instruments alongside piano accompaniment, the performance will lead music lovers on a vibrant journey capturing the diverse musical landscapes of the country.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The repertoire in the first half of the recital revolves around a thematic progression of regional colours moving &quot;from subtle to intense&quot;. The performance will open with the Zhejiang-style piece &quot;Wujiang Scenery&quot;. It will then transition into a Cantonese music five-piece combo performance of the suite &quot;The Lion Dance, Stepping High, Billowing Waves&quot;, capturing the spirited essence of Lingnan music. This will be followed by &quot;Oasis&quot;, where the dialogue between dizi and hand drum heightens the passionate, exotic rhythms of Xinjiang. The first half will conclude with &quot;Welcoming Relatives in a Mountain Village&quot;, which draws inspiration from Shanxi opera music, and excerpts from &quot;The Song of Flight&quot; with piano accompaniment, infused with ethnic musical elements from southwestern China, weaving a brilliant dialogue between Chinese and Western music.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The second half will open with Yau leading &quot;Late Autumn Memories&quot;. She will then perform the classic &quot;Birds in Shade&quot;, which is adapted from Hebei suona music. Next will come the contemporary masterpiece &quot;Wilderness&quot;, expressing an enduring aspiration and praise towards wonderful things in the world. This will be followed by the traditional dizi piece &quot;Three Five Seven&quot;, accompanied by the bangu (clapper drum) to fully manifest the musical charm of Zhejiang Wu opera. The recital will culminate with &quot;Horizon&quot;, crafting a breathtaking picture of soaring eagles over snow-capped mountains with nimble, ethereal flute notes, bringing this dizi journey to a perfect end.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The six local musicians joining Yau in this recital are Chan Kai-him and Lam Pui-kuen (huqin), Chan Wing-hei (yangqin), Chan Sze-tung (ruan), Lo Hok-chun (percussion) and Liu Tsz-shing (piano).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Yau graduated with distinction from the Hong Kong Academy for Performing Arts with a Master of Music degree, majoring in the dizi under the tutelage of Principal Dizi of the Hong Kong Chinese Orchestra Sun Yongzhi, and was the Gold Award winner in the Professional Youth Group A at the 5th &quot;Song Ting Cup&quot; National Dizi and Xiao Competition. Yau is the founder of &quot;Tubic&quot;, an initiative dedicated to promoting and preserving traditional Chinese dizi and dongxiao music.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The &quot;Hong Kong Artists&quot; Series 2026: Dizi Recital by Candy Yau Kwan-lam will be staged at 8pm on November 9 (Monday) at the Theatre of Hong Kong City Hall. Tickets priced at $220 are now available at URBTIX (<a href="https://www.urbtix.hk" target="_blank">www.urbtix.hk</a>). For telephone bookings, please call 3166 1288. For programme enquiries and discount schemes, please call 2268 7321 or visit <a href="https://www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/music/programs_1987.html" target="_blank">www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/music/programs_1987.html</a>.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The &quot;Hong Kong Artists&quot; Series of the LCSD aims to provide a platform for local artists to showcase their remarkable talent. Other recitals to be held in October include today (October 8)&#39;s Saxophone Recital by Charles Ng and the Piano Recital by John Lee on October 27. Package discounts and group booking discounts are also available. For more information about the &quot;Hong Kong Artists&quot; Series 2026, please visit <a href="https://www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/music/groups_1985.html" target="_blank">www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/music/groups_1985.html</a>.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340533.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340533.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340534.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340534.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340535.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340535.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340536.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340536.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340537.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340537.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340538.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340538.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340539.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700442_photo_1340539.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[SFST visits leading gold refineries in Switzerland to encourage setting up and expanding operations in Hong Kong (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100600401.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100600401.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury, Mr Christopher Hui, proceeded to Ticino, Switzerland, on October 7 (Ticino time) to continue his visit after attending the Global Precious Metals Conference in Sorrento, Italy. He visited three world-leading gold refineries, namely MKS PAMP, Argor-Heraeus SA and Valcambi SA, to encourage high-end precious metals businesses and high-standard gold refining facilities to establish a presence and expand their operations in Hong Kong. The Chief Executive Officer of the Hong Kong Precious Metals Central Clearing Company Limited, Mr Bryan Chan, also joined the visit.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Hui first visited the MKS PAMP refinery in the morning. He met with its senior management, including the Group Chief Executive Officer, Mr Marwan Shakarchi, and the Chief Executive Officer, Mr James Emmett, followed by a tour to its vaulting facility and assay laboratory facility. MKS PAMP set up its third regional headquarters in Hong Kong last year to expand its footprint in the Asia-Pacific region through Hong Kong. During the meeting, the two parties discussed plans to leverage Hong Kong as a trading platform and further expand gold business in the region and on the Mainland.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Hui and his delegation then visited the Argor-Heraeus SA refinery to learn about the latest developments in its application of artificial intelligence, blockchain and various analytical technologies in precious metals supply chain traceability and precision casting. Argor-Heraeus SA has been a pillar in Hong Kong&#39;s precious metals refining ecosystem with its trading office and refinery facility operating in the city for decades. At his meeting with its Chief Executive Officer, Dr Robin Kolvenbach, Mr Hui told Dr Kolvenbach that Hong Kong has put forward a suite of targeted initiatives to foster the building of a modern and full-chain gold trading ecosystem. Coupled with the vast land supply and development potential brought about by the Northern Metropolis project, it is now an ideal time for the group to consider expanding business in Hong Kong and bringing to the city its advanced refining technology.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Before concluding his visit in Switzerland, Mr Hui toured the Valcambi SA refinery where he met its Chief Executive Officer, Mr Simone Knobloch, and other senior management. At the meeting, Valcambi SA disclosed that the company is planning a collaboration with Hong Kong&#39;s sole steel producer Shiu Wing Steel to develop a gold refining and vaulting facility in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Hui said, &quot;We are fully committed to developing a comprehensive gold trading ecosystem that integrates gold trading, clearing and settlement, storage, refining, and risk management all in Hong Kong. Refining is a crucial component of this ecosystem. Now that top-tier Swiss refiners are taking part or planning to participate in Hong Kong&#39;s gold business, it definitely gives a massive boost to our efforts in gaining international recognition and elevating industry standards. Building on the current strong momentum in gold market development, the Government will continue our proactive approach in exploring policy support measures with a view to attracting more international precious metals enterprises to establish presence and Asia-Pacific headquarters in Hong Kong.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Hui has completed his visit to Switzerland. He will be back to Hong Kong this evening (October 8).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340734.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340734.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340735.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340735.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340736.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340736.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340737.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340737.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340738.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340738.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340739.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340739.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340740.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340740.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340741.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340741.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340742.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340742.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340743.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340743.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340744.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340744.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340745.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100600401_photo_1340745.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCSD's "Cheers!" Series to present wonder-filled family theatre programmes (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700407.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100700407.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The annual &quot;Cheers!&quot; Series, presented by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department (LCSD), will make a triumphant return from December this year to January next year. Acclaimed troupes from Korea, Brazil, Australia and Denmark will debut in Hong Kong together with three local art groups, crafting an enchanting world of theatrical wonders for families to accompany members of the public through the festive periods. Tickets will go for sale from October 14 (Wednesday).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Winner at the Edinburgh Festival Fringe Theatre Awards 2025, &quot;Dream Space&quot; is a non-verbal omnibus production by Creative Group SSAK from Korea. Through puppetry and physical theatre, the production tells the stories of a traditional Korean folk tale; two castaways stranded on an island, and a lonely young girl meeting a whale in the deep blue sea. Interwoven with fabulous stage imagery, it leads audiences of all ages into a magical theatrical world.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&quot;Bubble Show in Space&quot; by Bubble Laboratory from Australia offers a space adventure with a flying bubble rocket soaring into the air, super-sized bubbles big enough to fit several people inside, and finishes with a spectacular &quot;million bubble party&quot;. This production was the winner of the Best in Fringe Kids Award at the Sydney Fringe 2024, and should not be missed.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Led by four comedy masters from Brazil&#39;s Parlapat&otilde;es, the physical clown theatre production &quot;The Mequetrefo&quot; transforms the mundane routine of going to work into a riotous adventure. The whimsical plot, combined with humorous physical clowning, unleashes a wave of side-splitting comedic energy.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Captivating clown theatre piece &quot;Frida&#39;s Incredible Journey&quot; is a solo performance produced by Denmark&#39;s Paolo Nani Teater. The protagonist, Frida, sets off on a whimsical adventure to find her best friend, packing along her hen, her cat, and a magical suitcase. The journey unfolds on a beautiful stage where a handmade aesthetic intertwines with vibrant visuals.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Extension activities such as workshops and meet-the-artist sessions will be held for the above-mentioned programmes. For details, please visit <a href="https://www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/f_entertainment/groups_2013.html" target="_blank">www.lcsd.gov.hk/CE/CulturalService/Programme/en/f_entertainment/groups_2013.html</a>.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Local art groups will also present a rich line-up of programmes. These include the multimedia children&#39;s theatre &quot;ANTNETIC LOST&quot; by Creman Concept; the puppet musical &quot;Sweetyland IV The Fluffy Fungus Fight&quot; by Make Friends with Puppet; and the &quot;Hong Kong Oratorio Society 70th Anniversary Concert III: A Thankful Christmas&quot;.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For programme dates, venues and ticket prices of the &quot;Cheers!&quot; Series, please see the Annex. Tickets will be available from October 14 (Wednesday) onwards at URBTIX (<a href="https://www.urbtix.hk" target="_blank">www.urbtix.hk</a>), and telephone bookings can be made by calling 3166 1288. Various discount schemes, such as package discounts and family package discounts, will be offered. For programme enquiries and discount schemes, please call 2268 7323 or visit the above-mentioned website.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340434.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340434.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340435.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340435.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340436.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340436.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340437.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100700407_photo_1340437.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[EMSD announces latest sampling results for legionella at fresh water cooling towers]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800196.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800196.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Electrical and Mechanical Services Department (EMSD) today (October 8) announced that the department tested 115 water samples collected from cooling towers in 62 buildings in its routine inspections in September 2026. Three samples were detected to have a total legionella count at or above the upper threshold, which is 1 000 colony-forming units per millilitre. The latest statistics are set out in Annex 1.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Two of the cooling towers in which the samples were collected are located at Pacific Trade Centre, 2 Kai Hing Road, Kwun Tong, and one is located at Luk Hop Industrial Building, 8 Luk Hop Street, Wong Tai Sin. The EMSD has issued nuisance notices under the Public Health and Municipal Services Ordinance to the owners of the cooling towers requiring appropriate disinfection work to be done. Disinfection of the cooling towers in these cases have already been completed by the owners. Details of the cases can be found in Annex 2.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The EMSD publishes the latest statistics of the above information on a half-monthly basis on its website<br />
(<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/en/other_regulatory_services/cooling_towers/water_sampling/index.html" target="_blank">www.emsd.gov.hk/en/other_regulatory_services/cooling_towers/water_sampling/index.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The EMSD reminds the owners of fresh water cooling towers that they have the responsibility to design, operate and maintain cooling towers properly. They should arrange regular inspections, timely maintenance and periodic testing of the water quality in their cooling towers in accordance with the Code of Practice for Fresh Water Cooling Towers issued by the department to prevent the proliferation of legionella.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at some beaches]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800216.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800216.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 10:41:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 8) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Hap Mun Bay Beach, Silverstrand Beach and Clear Water Bay Second Beach in Sai Kung District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Keynote speech by PSITI at Schneider Electric Innovation Summit Hong Kong 2026 (English only) (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800153.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/08/P2026100800153.htm</link><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 10:25:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is the keynote speech by the Permanent Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Mr Kevin Choi, at the Schneider Electric Innovation Summit Hong Kong 2026 today (October 8):<br />
<br />
Distinguished speakers and guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Good morning. It is my pleasure to join you at this Summit to share our insights on powering Hong Kong&#39;s next growth era through AI innovation. I would like to share how Hong Kong is building a dynamic AI ecosystem, one that supports innovation, strengthens business competitiveness and enables a smarter, more sustainable future.<br />
<br />
Alignment with National 15th Five-Year Plan (2026-2030)<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;First and foremost, this year marks the start of the National 15th Five-Year Plan, which recognises AI as an important engine for economic and social development and calls for the further advancement of &quot;Digital China&quot; and the &quot;AI+&quot; Initiative.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To align with national development strategies and better integrate into our country&#39;s overall development, the Hong Kong Government announced its First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development last month. It provides a strategic framework for long-term, high-quality development, building on Hong Kong&#39;s unique strengths and enhancing our attractiveness as an international business and investment destination.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At the heart of this strategy is our commitment to deepen &quot;AI+&quot; and digital transformation across the economy and society. Building on this, we will accelerate Hong Kong&#39;s development as an international I&amp;T (innovation and technology) centre by strengthening frontier research, technology transfer and commercialisation, through closer collaboration among the Government, industry, academia and research sectors.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;People are central to this effort. We will nurture local talent, attract high-calibre professionals from around the world, and open up more opportunities for young people to contribute to Hong Kong&#39;s I&amp;T development.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Finally, the Northern Metropolis will provide the strategic space and platform for the next stage of growth.<br />
<br />
Driving AI+ Development in HK<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;We are placing AI at the core of our digital economy strategy and accelerating both &quot;industries for AI&quot; and &quot;AI for industries&quot; to foster Hong Kong&#39;s AI development. This means building the essential foundations for AI development, including research capability, computing infrastructure, data, talent, capital and commercialisation, while promoting AI adoption across industries and public services to enhance productivity, improve services and create new business opportunities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Among all, the Government has formed the Committee on AI+ and Industry Development Strategy. Chaired by the Financial Secretary, the Committee brings together representatives from Government, industry and the professional sectors to formulate priorities and strategies for AI-driven industrial transformation, strengthen co-ordination across the ecosystem, and foster the deep integration of AI with different industries.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To build the city&#39;s AI ecosystem, the Government has been continuously strengthening our support for R&amp;D (research and development) transformation and the digital infrastructure that underpins it.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, we are actively empowering public services with AI to speed up Government&#39;s digital transformation and streamline processes.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As we scale AI adoption, we are putting in place AI governance safeguards that ensure responsible and transparent deployment. And through our &quot;AI for All&quot; Inclusive Programme, we are promoting AI literacy and broader adoption, so that the benefits of AI can be shared across society.<br />
<br />
Research and Development<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On the R&amp;D front, the Hong Kong Artificial Intelligence Research and Development Institute (AIRDI) will start operation this year. It plays a pioneering role in Hong Kong&#39;s AI ecosystem by advancing frontier AI research, promoting the integration of AI in key sectors, and providing a platform that matches AI solutions with industry needs, thereby driving the commercialisation of AI R&amp;D outcomes.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;InnoHK is another major government initiative to develop Hong Kong as a hub for global research collaboration. Its AIR@InnoHK research cluster has established 16 R&amp;D laboratories focused on AI and robotics, bringing together around 1 200 world-class talents.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the &quot;one river, two banks&quot; and &quot;one zone, two parks&quot; framework, we are accelerating the Hetao Shenzhen-Hong Kong Science and Technology Innovation Co-operation Zone and facilitating the safe flow of research data between its Shenzhen and Hong Kong parks. Together with the San Tin Technopole, this will form an end-to-end ecosystem linking research, pilot testing and mass production, supported by the Northern Metropolis University Towns in San Tin, Hung Shui Kiu and Ta Kwu Ling to nurture AI talent.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
Digital Infrastructure<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the AI era, computing power is crucial to Hong Kong&#39;s competitiveness. Our total computing power has reached 5 000 PFLOPS (peta-floating point operations per second), of which 3 000 PFLOPS comes from Cyberport&#39;s AI Supercomputing Centre.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To help make use of this capacity, the Government launched a $3 billion AI Subsidy Scheme in 2024. It has so far supported nearly 40 R&amp;D projects, in areas such as local large language models, new materials, synthetic biology and medical large models. As announced in the Policy Address this year, a further HK$1 billion will be injected into the Scheme to support the AIRDI&#39;s frontier research.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Looking ahead, the Sandy Ridge Data Facility Cluster in the Northern Metropolis is under construction, with the first phase starting operation next year. By 2032, it is expected to provide 180 000 PFLOPS, 36 times Hong Kong&#39;s current computing power.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At the same time, the Government will take forward a study on power usage effectiveness, or PUE, for data centres. Guided by the principles of &quot;high-efficiency computing power, stable electricity supply and low-carbon transition&quot;, we will also assess the market demand for computing power to optimise the city-level layout of computing power, supporting the sustainable development of our intelligent computing industry.<br />
<br />
Empower Public Services through AI<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Recognising the immense potential and value of AI in public services, the Government is spearheading the adoption of AI across public services to boost efficiency and effectiveness.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For example, the Digital Policy Office (DPO) is going to launch an AI Assistant for users of &quot;iAM Smart&quot; and the upcoming Digital Corporate Identity Platform , to answer enquiries about online government services, and recommend suitable funding schemes and public service support for enterprises, especially SMEs (small and medium enterprises).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To drive technological reform and steer government departments to apply AI, the Government set up the AI Efficacy Enhancement Team (AIEET) last year. To date, the AIEET has facilitated and implemented the first batch of 30 efficacy enhancement project across 13 departments.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To help government departments swiftly adopt suitable AI technologies, DPO has introduced the &quot;AI Solution Catalogue&quot; and the &quot;AI Toolbox&quot;, featuring over 260 AI-related solutions and tools meeting the departments&#39; operational needs. In addition, we are piloting the use of AI Agents within the Government.<br />
<br />
AI Governance<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To support the responsible development and wider adoption of AI, Hong Kong takes a pragmatic, adaptive and risk-based approach to governance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government has issued the Ethical Artificial Intelligence Framework and the Hong Kong Generative Artificial Intelligence Technical and Application Guideline. These provide practical guidance for developers, service providers and users, covering areas such as ethics, data security, system robustness and appropriate human oversight.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, the Inter-Departmental Working Group led by the Department of Justice is reviewing legislation to support the wider application of AI, while ensuring appropriate safeguards.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Building on these foundations, the latest Policy Address sets out an AI risk-governance strategy with seven areas of action. They address the risks of AI at every level: tackling misuse such as AI-enabled crime, protecting vulnerable underage users, ensuring ethical and safe application across industries, clarifying legal liability, and preparing our workforce for the changes AI will bring.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To drive this work across the Government, the Chief Secretary for Administration will co-ordinate the implementation of the strategy. A new Commissioner for AI post will also be created under the DPO to assist bureaux with the related work.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Together, these measures will help ensure that Hong Kong can foster innovation while maintaining a safe, orderly and trusted environment for AI development and application.<br />
<br />
&quot;AI for All&quot; Inclusive Programme<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To promote AI awareness and responsible use, the Government will, through Cyberport, the HKSTP (Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks) and the HKPC (Hong Kong Productivity Council), roll out over 200 training courses and activities under the &quot;AI for All&quot; Inclusive Programme by the first quarter of 2028.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Programme will adopt a tiered, collaborative model to support the needs of different community segments:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>Tier 1 aims to promote AI awareness and a culture of responsible AI use while bridging the digital divide. It targets students, parents, the elderly and underprivileged groups.&nbsp;</li>
	<li>Tier 2 targets university students, young researchers and professionals. It aims to translate AI knowledge into practical skills and employability.</li>
	<li>Tier 3 targets mainly SMEs and working professionals. It focuses on practical training and optimisation tailored to workplace scenarios, promotes knowledge sharing, and enhances efficiency and competitiveness.</li>
</ul>
<br />
Closing<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In closing, Hong Kong&#39;s ambition goes beyond adopting AI. We are building a complete AI ecosystem: frontier research, world-class computing infrastructure, AI-enabled public services, responsible governance, and a talent base that reaches every part of society.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;But no government can do this alone. Turning AI into real economic and social value needs industry, research institutions and the community working together. Schneider is a clear example of how our business leader, the world&#39;s most sustainable company ranked by the Time Magazine, can connect the community and contribute to R&amp;D and application of energy and AI solutions to better the world. With our international connectivity and our close links with the Mainland, Hong Kong is well placed to be your partner in this journey. We welcome you to develop, test and scale your innovations here.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;I wish this Summit every success today, and look forward to driving innovation together, forward. Thank you.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800153_photo_1340752.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/08/P2026100800153_photo_1340752.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Labour Department highly concerned about fatal work accident that happened in Tung Chung today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/07/P2026100700704.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/07/P2026100700704.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 22:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Labour Department (LD) is highly concerned about a fatal work accident that happened at a construction site in Tung Chung this afternoon (October 7), in which a male worker fell from height while dismantling a working platform on the external wall of a building under construction. He was certified dead later in hospital. The LD is saddened by the death of the worker and expresses its deepest sympathy to his family.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The LD&#39;s spokesman said, &quot;We commenced an immediate on-site investigation as soon as we were notified of the accident and have issued suspension notices to the contractors concerned, suspending the erection, alteration, dismantling and use of the working platforms on the external walls of buildings at the site. The contractors cannot resume the work process until the LD is satisfied that suitable measures to abate the relevant risks have been taken.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The spokesman added, &quot;We will complete the investigation as soon as possible to identify the cause of the accident, ascertain the liability of duty holders and recommend improvement measures. We will take action pursuant to the law if any violations are found during the investigation.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To prevent workers from falling from height while dismantling working platforms, the LD reminds contractors to take adequate preventive measures against falls, including providing every worker with a suitable full-body harness that is attached continuously to a suitable and secure anchor point, an independent lifeline or a fall arresting system, and ensuring that workers concerned properly use the above safety equipment throughout the work.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The general duty provisions of the Occupational Safety and Health Ordinance require employers to provide safe working environments, plant and systems of work for their employees. Those who contravene the relevant provisions are liable to a maximum fine of $10 million and imprisonment for two years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In regard to today&#39;s accident, the LD will issue a Work Safety Alert through its mobile application &quot;OSH 2.0&quot;, website and email, giving a brief account of the accident concerned to duty holders, workers&#39; unions, professional bodies of safety practitioners and others, and reminding the industry of the importance of following safety precautionary measures to prevent a recurrence of similar accidents.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The LD will also remind the employer concerned of the liability for employees&#39; compensation under the Employees&#39; Compensation Ordinance, assist family members of the deceased to claim employees&#39; compensation and closely follow up on the case. For those with financial difficulties, the LD will assist them to apply for appropriate emergency funds. Subject to the needs and wishes of the family members of the deceased, the LD will also liaise with the Social Welfare Department for financial or other assistance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For the sake of securing the safety and health of employees at work, the LD appeals to employers to provide plant and systems of work that are safe and without risks to health. Employees should also co-operate with their employers, adopt all safety measures and use personal protective equipment provided properly to avoid endangering their own work safety and that of other workers.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[307 nomination forms for Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/07/P2026100700738.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/07/P2026100700738.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 21:57:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The nomination period for the 2026 Election Committee (EC) Subsector Ordinary Elections runs from today (October 7) until October 20. A total of 305 nomination forms for candidates and two nomination forms from designated bodies were received by the Returning Officers for the various subsectors today.<br />
